/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:197:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_100","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex | sed -n '112,160p;400,440p'\"","aggregated_output":"   112\t% The paper states this result in a theorem environment with the label lem:ld-soundness.\n   113\t% We keep both the environment and the label verbatim for cross-reference fidelity.\n   114\t\\begin{theorem}[Quantum soundness of the simultaneous classical low individual degree test]\\label{lem:ld-soundness}\n   115\t\t\\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:projective-strategy-general, def:tensor-product-value, def:povm-distance, def:bracket}\n   116\t\tThere exists a function\n   117\t\t\\[\n   118\t\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}(\\eps, q, m, d, k) = a\\,(dmk)^a \\bigl(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd}\\bigr)\n   119\t\t\\]\n   120\t\tfor universal constants $a \\ge 1$ and $0 < b \\le 1$ such that the following holds. For all $\\eps > 0$ and every parameter tuple $\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ as in Definition~\\ref{def:ld-game} (so $q$ is an admissible field size and $m$ divides $q$), and for all projective strategies $(\\psi, A, B)$ that succeed with probability at least $1-\\eps$ in the game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\mathsf{ldparams}}$, there exist measurements\n   121\t\t\\[\n   122\t\t\tG^w \\in \\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)\n   123\t\t\\]\n   124\t\ton $\\hilb_w$ for $w \\in \\{\\mathrm A,\\mathrm B\\}$, where by slight abuse of notation $m$, $q$, $d$ denote the constant tuples $(m,\\ldots,m)$, $(q,\\ldots,q)$, $(d,\\ldots,d)$ of length $k$, such that\n   125\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   126\t\t\tA^{\\mathsf{Point},\\, u}_{a_1,\\ldots,a_k} \\ot I_{\\mathrm B}\n   127\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}}\n   128\t\t\tI_{\\mathrm A} \\ot G^{\\mathrm B}_{[(g_1(u),\\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\\ldots,a_k)]}\\;,\\\\\n   129\t\t\tG^{\\mathrm A}_{[(g_1(u),\\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\\ldots,a_k)]} \\ot I_{\\mathrm B}\n   130\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}}\n   131\t\t\tI_{\\mathrm A} \\ot B^{\\mathsf{Point},\\, u}_{a_1,\\ldots,a_k}\\;,\\\\\n   132\t\t\tG^{\\mathrm A}_{g_1,\\ldots,g_k} \\ot I_{\\mathrm B}\n   133\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}}\n   134\t\t\tI_{\\mathrm A} \\ot G^{\\mathrm B}_{g_1,\\ldots,g_k}\\;,\n   135\t\t\\end{align*}\n   136\t\twhere $\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}} = \\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}(\\eps,q,m,d,k)$, all three approximations are with respect to the state $\\ket{\\psi}$, and the bracket notation for post-processed outcomes is that of Definition~\\ref{def:bracket}. The first two approximations hold on average over the uniform distribution on $u \\in \\F_q^m$; no question distribution is associated with the third.\n   137\t\\end{theorem}\n   138\t\\begin{proof}\n   139\t\t\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:povm-distance}\n   140\t\tLet $\\Sigma = \\F_q$ and let $\\code \\subseteq \\Sigma^q$ be the Reed--Solomon code of degree $d$: the set of all $(p(x_1),\\ldots,p(x_q))$ where $p\\colon \\F_q \\to \\F_q$ is a univariate polynomial of degree at most $d$ and $x_1,\\ldots,x_q$ enumerate $\\F_q$. We may assume $d < q$. In the opposite regime the statement is immediate: for $k \\ge 1$ and $d \\ge q$ the prefactor gives $\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}} \\ge a\\,(dmk)^a\\, q^{-b} \\ge q^{a-b} \\ge 1$, while the quantity bounded in Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance} never exceeds $2$ for families of POVM elements, so the three approximations hold for an arbitrary choice of $G^w \\in \\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$; and for $k = 0$ every answer tuple is empty, all measurements involved are single-outcome, and the approximations hold with error zero. For $d < q$, evaluation is injective on polynomials of degree at most $d$ and a nonzero polynomial of degree at most $d$ vanishes at no more than $d$ of the $q$ points, so $\\code$ is a linear $[q,\\, d+1,\\, q-d]_\\Sigma$ code.\n   141\t\t% The paper prints the parameters as [q, d+1, n-d+1] with an undefined n. With the\n   142\t\t% intended value n = q the printed distance q-d+1 exceeds the true minimum distance\n   143\t\t% q-d of the degree-d evaluation code, and the dimension claim d+1 requires d < q.\n   144\t\t% Both points are corrected above; the regime d >= q, which the parameter tuple of\n   145\t\t% Definition def:ld-game permits, is handled by the triviality argument.\n   146\t\tThe two-prover tensor-code test for the code $\\code^{\\ot m}$, as described in Figures~1 and~2 of~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, coincides with the game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}$ with parameters $\\mathsf{ldparams}$ and $k=1$: the axis-parallel line-versus-point test is their axis-parallel lines test, with the roles of the two players chosen uniformly at random, which is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor-code test from Section~4.1 there; the diagonal line-versus-point test is their subcube commutation test, again with the added symmetrization between the players; and the consistency test is their synchronicity test. Theorem~4.7 of~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$ (this parameter is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of $\\mathsf{ldparams}$), therefore yields the present statement for $k = 1$. The case of general $k$ follows by a standard reduction, following exactly the derivation of Theorem~4.43 from Theorem~4.40 in~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Quantum soundness of the low individual degree test itself is the result of~\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}; Remark~\\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider} relates the present statement to Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal}, which proves that result.\n   147\t\\end{proof}\n   148\t\n   149\t\\begin{remark}[Relation to the low individual degree test of Chapter~\\ref{chap:test}]\\label{rem:ld-soundness-provider}\n   150\t\tTheorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is imported: the proof above reduces it to Theorem~4.7 of~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$, followed by the extension to simultaneous $k$-tuples of~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The result of~\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} that underlies it is proved here as Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal}: for every projective strategy passing the $(m,q,d)$ low individual degree test of Definition~\\ref{def:low-individual-degree-test} with probability at least $1-\\eps$, that theorem produces measurements in $\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ satisfying consistency relations of the same shape as the three approximations of Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is not a consequence of Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal} alone: a derivation from it would require, in addition, an equivalence between the typed game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{(q,m,d,1)}$ and the $(m,q,d)$ test of Chapter~\\ref{chap:test} --- matching the two question distributions, the two win conditions, and the two error functions --- together with the extension from $k = 1$ to general $k$ by the reduction of~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} cited above. Neither implication is proved in this chapter, and the proof of Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} given above does not invoke Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal}.\n   151\t\\end{remark}\n   152\t\n   153\t\\section{The Magic Square game}\n   154\t% Paper label sec:ms kept for cross-reference fidelity.\n   155\t\\label{sec:ms}\n   156\t\n   157\tWe recall the Magic Square game of Mermin and Peres. It is a simple self-test for two EPR pairs, and it allows one to test that a pair of binary observables \\emph{anticommutes}; both properties are used in the Pauli basis test. Here it is presented as a binary constraint satisfaction game. Throughout this section and the next, $\\sigma^X$ and $\\sigma^Z$ denote the single-qubit Pauli observables, with associated two-outcome projective measurements $\\{\\sigma^W_b\\}_{b \\in \\F_2}$ for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$.\n   158\t\n   159\t\\begin{definition}[The Magic Square game]\\label{def:ms-game}\n   160\t\t\\uses{def:game, def:graph-distribution}\n   400\t\n   401\t\\section{Canonical parameters of the Pauli basis test}\n   402\t% Paper label sec:qld-complexity kept for cross-reference fidelity (the complexity\n   403\t% statements of that subsection are outside the scope of this development).\n   404\t\\label{sec:qld-complexity}\n   405\t\n   406\tWe specify a canonical setting of the parameter tuple $\\mathsf{qldparams}$ as a function of an integer $R$, and bound the resulting soundness function. All logarithms are base $2$.\n   407\t\n   408\t\\begin{definition}[Canonical parameters of the Pauli basis test]\\label{def:introparams}\n   409\t\t\\uses{thm:pauli}\n   410\t\tLet $a, b$ be the universal constants of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli}, and let $c$ be the smallest \\emph{even} integer that is at least $(b+a)/b$. For every integer $R \\ge 4$, define the tuple $\\mathsf{introparams}(R) = (q, m, d)$ by\n   411\t\t\\begin{itemize}\n   412\t\t\t\\item $q = 2^k$ for $k = c \\lceil \\log \\log R \\rceil + 1$,\n   413\t\t\t\\item $m = 2^j$, where $j$ is the unique integer with $2^j \\le c \\lceil \\log R \\rceil + 1 < 2^{j+1}$,\n   414\t\t\t\\item $d = 1$.\n   415\t\t\\end{itemize}\n   416\t\tThe letter $k$ here denotes the exponent of the field size and is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of the classical game. With this choice the Pauli basis test certifies the presence of an $M = 2^m$-qudit maximally entangled state of local dimension $q$, equivalently an $(M \\log q)$-qubit EPR state, and the choice $d = 1$ runs the low individual degree test multilinearly.\n   417\t\\end{definition}\n   418\t\n   419\t\\begin{lemma}[Soundness bound for the canonical parameters]\\label{lem:delta-bound}\n   420\t\t\\uses{def:introparams, def:admissible, thm:pauli}\n   421\t\tFor all integers $R \\ge 4$, the parameter tuple $\\mathsf{introparams}(R) = (q,m,d)$ is admissible (Definition~\\ref{def:admissible}) and satisfies $2^m \\ge R$. Furthermore, there exist universal constants $a' \\ge 1$ and $0 < b' \\le 1$ such that the function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps, m, d, q)$ of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} is at most\n   422\t\t\\[\n   423\t\t\ta' \\bigl( (\\log R)^{a'} \\cdot \\eps^{b'} + (\\log R)^{-b'} \\bigr)\\;.\n   424\t\t\\]\n   425\t\\end{lemma}\n   426\t\\begin{proof}\n   427\t\t\\uses{def:introparams, def:admissible, def:admissible-size, thm:pauli}\n   428\t\tSince $c$ is even, the exponent $k = c \\lceil \\log\\log R \\rceil + 1$ is odd, so $q = 2^k$ is an admissible field size; and $m$ divides $q$ because $2^j \\le c \\lceil \\log R \\rceil + 1 \\le 2 \\log^c R \\le 2^k$, so $j \\le k$ and both are powers of $2$. Since $m = 2^j \\ge (c/2) \\log R$, we get $2^m \\ge R^{c/2} \\ge R$, using $c \\ge 2$.\n   429\t\n   430\t\tFor the soundness bound, write $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = a(md)^a \\eps^b + a(md)^a q^{-b} + a(md)^a 2^{-bmd}$ and use $d = 1$, $m \\le 2c \\log R$, and $q = 2^k \\ge \\log^c R$. The first term is at most $a(2c)^a (\\log R)^a \\eps^b$. The second term is at most $a(2c)^a (\\log R)^{a - cb} \\le a(2c)^a (\\log R)^{-b}$, since $cb - a \\ge b$ by the choice of $c$. For the third term, $m \\ge (c/2) \\log R \\ge \\log R$ gives $2^{-bm} \\le R^{-b}$, so\n   431\t\t\\[\n   432\t\t\ta(md)^a\\, 2^{-bm}\n   433\t\t\t\\le a(2c)^a (\\log R)^a R^{-b}\n   434\t\t\t= a(2c)^a \\bigl( (\\log R)^{a+b} R^{-b} \\bigr) (\\log R)^{-b}\n   435\t\t\t\\le a(2c)^a\\, C\\, (\\log R)^{-b}\\;,\n   436\t\t\\]\n   437\t\twhere $C = \\sup_{R' \\ge 4} (\\log R')^{a+b} (R')^{-b}$ is finite --- the function is continuous in $R'$ and tends to $0$ as $R' \\to \\infty$ --- and depends only on the universal constants $a$ and $b$. Summing the three bounds, the statement follows with $a' = a(2c)^a (1 + C)$ and $b' = b$.\n   438\t\\end{proof}\n   439\t\n   440\t\\begin{remark}[A comparison of exponents in the source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:delta-bound}]\\label{rem:delta-bound-exponent-comparison}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:203:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_103","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex | sed -n '360,400p'; rg -n 'Fact 4.35|lem:pasting' references/neexp-paper references/qpbt-paper | head -30\"","aggregated_output":"   360\t\t\\end{equation}\n   361\t\\end{lemma}\n   362\t\\begin{proof}\n   363\t\tThe source proof is a citation: the argument is identical to the proof of Fact 4.34 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, with the only modification being the insertion of the dependence on the question $x$ for all measurements considered. In outline, the consistency hypotheses \\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-1} allow one to insert the projective factors $G^{i,\\,x}$ next to $A^x$ one index at a time, and the $\\eps$-bound on the pairwise agreement of distinct functions in $\\mathcal G_i$ controls the events on which two different outcomes collide at the sampled point $y$. Each of the $k$ insertions costs $(\\delta+\\eps)^{1/2}$ by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, producing the sandwich $C^x$ with total error $k(\\delta+\\eps)^{1/2}$.\n   364\t\\end{proof}\n   365\t\n   366\t\\begin{remark}[Index pairing in the sandwich]\\label{rem:ld-sandwich-indexing}\n   367\t\tAs printed in the source, the sandwich in Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich} pairs the $i$-th measurement with the outcome $g_{k+1-i}$: the outermost factor $G^{k,\\,x}$ carries the outcome $g_1$ and the innermost factor $G^{1,\\,x}$ carries $g_k$. Since the measurement $\\{G^{i,\\,x}_g\\}$ is indexed by $g \\in \\mathcal G_i$, the operator $G^{k,\\,x}_{g_1}$ is undefined whenever $\\mathcal G_1 \\ne \\mathcal G_k$, so the printed pairing is a typographical slip. The statement above instead uses the pairing $G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k} \\cdots G^{1,\\,x}_{g_1} \\cdots G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k}$ of Fact 4.34 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, from which the lemma is imported. The source also leaves the case $k = 0$ implicit; with the empty-product convention recorded in the statement, both sides of \\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-2} are then the identity on the single empty-tuple outcome and the conclusion holds with error zero.\n   368\t\\end{remark}\n   369\t\n   370\t\\begin{lemma}[Pasting two consistent measurements; Fact 4.35 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{lem:pasting}\n   371\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n   372\t\tLet $\\mathcal D$ be a distribution on $(x, y_1, y_2) \\in \\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y_1 \\times \\mathcal Y_2$. For $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$ let $\\mathcal G_i$ be a collection of functions $g_i \\colon \\mathcal Y_i \\to \\mathcal R_i$ and let $\\{(G_i)^x_g\\}_{g \\in \\mathcal G_i}$ be families of measurements such that $\\{(G_2)^x_g\\}_g$ is projective for every $x$. Suppose further that for every $(x, y_1)$ it holds that for $g_2 \\ne g_2' \\in \\mathcal G_2$ the probability, on average over $y_2$ chosen from $\\mathcal D$ conditioned on $(x, y_1)$, that $g_2(y_2) = g_2'(y_2)$ is at most $\\eta$. Let $\\{A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2}\\}$ be a family of projective measurements with outcomes $(a_1, a_2) \\in \\mathcal R_1 \\times \\mathcal R_2$ such that for $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$,\n   373\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-1}\n   374\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_i} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot (G_i)^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_i}(\\cdot)=a_i]}\n   375\t\t\\end{equation}\n   376\t\tand\n   377\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-2}\n   378\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2}\\,,\n   379\t\t\\end{equation}\n   380\t\twhere $A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_i}$ denotes the marginal on the $i$-th part of the outcome (Definition~\\ref{def:povm-conventions}). Define a family of measurements $\\{J^x_{g_1,\\,g_2}\\}$ by\n   381\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-2a}\n   382\t\t\tJ^x_{g_1,\\,g_2} = (G_2)^x_{g_2}\\, (G_1)^x_{g_1}\\, (G_2)^x_{g_2}\\,.\n   383\t\t\\end{equation}\n   384\t\tThen there is a\n   385\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:def-deltap}\n   386\t\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}} = \\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}(\\eta, \\delta) = \\poly(\\eta, \\delta)\n   387\t\t\\end{equation}\n   388\t\tsuch that\n   389\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-3}\n   390\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{pasting}}} \\Id \\ot J^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_1}(\\cdot)=a_1,\\, \\operatorname{eval}_{y_2}(\\cdot)=a_2]}\\,.\n   391\t\t\\end{equation}\n   392\t\tNeither the exponent nor the constant in $\\poly(\\eta, \\delta)$ is made explicit in the source; both are universal constants inherited from the proof of Fact 4.35 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}.\n   393\t\\end{lemma}\n   394\t\\begin{proof}\n   395\t\tStated in the source without proof, as an import of Fact 4.35 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The measurement $J^x$ sandwiches the family $(G_1)^x$ between two copies of the projective family $(G_2)^x$. The consistency hypotheses \\eqref{eq:pasting-1} and the self-consistency \\eqref{eq:pasting-2} allow the joint measurement $A^{x,y_1,y_2}$ to be compared with the sandwiched product one factor at a time, and the $\\eta$-bound on the pairwise agreement of distinct functions in $\\mathcal G_2$ controls the error terms arising when two different outcomes $g_2 \\ne g_2'$ agree at the sampled point $y_2$.\n   396\t\\end{proof}\n   397\t\n   398\t\\begin{lemma}[Close strategies have close values; Fact 4.32 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{lem:close-strategies-have-close-values}\n   399\t\t\\uses{def:strategy-distance, def:tensor-product-value, def:projective-strategy-general}\n   400\t\tLet $\\mathfrak G$ be a nonlocal game, and let $\\mathcal S$, $\\mathcal S'$ be two strategies that are $\\delta$-close in the sense of Definition~\\ref{def:strategy-distance}, for some $\\delta \\in [0,1]$, and that use the same state $\\ket{\\psi}$. If either $\\mathcal S$ or $\\mathcal S'$ is projective, then\nreferences/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:504:\\begin{lemma}[Fact 4.35 in~\\cite{NW19}]\\label{lem:pasting}\nreferences/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:955:\tThen the hypotheses of \\Cref{lem:pasting} hold, with $\\delta$ in the lemma set to $\\delta_Q$, and $\\eta$ in the lemma set to $md/q$ (this follows from the Schwartz-Zippel lemma applied to the polynomials $f_X, f_Z$, which are of total degree at most $md$). Applying the lemma, we conclude that\nreferences/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex:960:\twhere $\\delta_P = \\poly(\\eps,md/q)$ is the $\\delta_{pasting}$ as given by Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}, conditions~\\eqref{eq:pasting-1} and~\\eqref{eq:pasting-2} in the lemma are given by~\\eqref{eq:pasting-q1} and~\\eqref{eq:qld-q-self-cons} respectively, \n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:205:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_104","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch11_qpbt_algebra.tex | sed -n '370,430p'; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/04_preliminaries.tex | sed -n '865,935p'\"","aggregated_output":"   370\t\tindividual-degree form.\n   371\t\\end{remark}\n   372\t\n   373\tThe Schwartz--Zippel lemma implies that low-degree polynomials form an\n   374\terror-correcting code with good distance, the \\emph{low-degree code} (a special\n   375\tcase of the generalized Reed--Muller code).\n   376\t\n   377\t\\begin{definition}[Low-degree encoding]\\label{def:low-degree-encoding}\n   378\t\t\\uses{def:polynomials-degree}\n   379\t\tFix an integer $m \\in \\N$ and let $M = 2^m$. For every $y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$\n   380\t\tdefine the $m$-variate multilinear polynomial over $\\F_q$\n   381\t\t\\[\n   382\t\t\t\\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}(x)\n   383\t\t\t= \\prod_{i : y_i = 1} x_i \\cdot \\prod_{i : y_i = 0} (1 - x_i)\\;,\n   384\t\t\\]\n   385\t\tidentifying $\\{0,1\\}$ with a subset of $\\F_q$. Restricted to the subcube\n   386\t\t$\\{0,1\\}^m$, the polynomial $\\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}$ vanishes everywhere except\n   387\t\tat $x = y$, where it equals $1$. For $a \\in \\F_q^M$, label the coordinates\n   388\t\tof $a$ as $a_y$ for $y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$ (identifying $\\{0,1\\}^m$ with\n   389\t\t$\\{1, \\dots, M\\}$ by the lexicographic ordering). The \\emph{low-degree\n   390\t\tencoding of $a$} is the multilinear polynomial $g_a : \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$,\n   391\t\t% Paper equation label kept on the display it names.\n   392\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:ld-encoding}\n   393\t\t\tg_a(x) = \\sum_{y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m} a_y \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}(x)\\;.\n   394\t\t\\end{equation}\n   395\t\tFor every $y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$ one has $g_a(y) = a_y$, and $g_a$ has total\n   396\t\tdegree at most $m$.\n   397\t\\end{definition}\n   398\t\n   399\t\\begin{definition}[Indicator vector and linearity of the encoding]\\label{def:indicator-vector}\n   400\t\t\\uses{def:low-degree-encoding, def:dot-product-orthogonal}\n   401\t\tFor $x \\in \\F_q^m$, the \\emph{indicator vector} is\n   402\t\t\\[\n   403\t\t\t\\mathrm{ind}_m(x)\n   404\t\t\t= \\bigl(\\mathrm{ind}_{m,y}(x)\\bigr)_{y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m} \\in \\F_q^M\\;.\n   405\t\t\\]\n   406\t\tWith this notation the low-degree encoding satisfies, for every\n   407\t\t$a \\in \\F_q^M$ and $x \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   408\t\t% Paper equation label kept on the display it names.\n   409\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}\n   410\t\t\tg_a(x) = a \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(x)\\;;\n   411\t\t\\end{equation}\n   412\t\tin particular the map $a \\mapsto g_a$ is $\\F_q$-linear.\n   413\t\\end{definition}\n   414\t\n   415\tSince encodings $g_a$ are multilinear, hence of total degree at most $m$, two\n   416\tencodings of distinct strings agree on at most an $m/q$ fraction of points of\n   417\t$\\F_q^m$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel}.\n   418\t\n   419\t\\begin{definition}[Decoding maps]\\label{def:decoding-map}\n   420\t\t\\uses{def:low-degree-encoding}\n   421\t\tFor $H \\subseteq \\F_q$, the \\emph{decoding map} $\\mathrm{Dec}_H$ takes as\n   422\t\tinput a polynomial $g : \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$ and returns the vector\n   423\t\t$a \\in \\F_q^M$ defined by: for all $y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$, set $a_y = g(y)$ if\n   424\t\t$g(y) \\in H$, and $a_y = 0$ otherwise. For all $a \\in H^M$ one has\n   425\t\t$\\mathrm{Dec}_H(g_a) = a$. We write $\\mathrm{Dec}(\\cdot)$ for the boolean\n   426\t\tdecoding map $\\mathrm{Dec}_{\\{0,1\\}}(\\cdot)$.\n   427\t\\end{definition}\n   428\t\n   429\t\\section{Lines}\n   430\t\n   865\t\n   866\t\n   867\t\\paragraph{The low-degree code.}\n   868\tThe Schwartz-Zippel lemma implies that the set of low-degree polynomials form an\n   869\terror-correcting code with good distance, which we call the \\emph{low-degree\n   870\t  code}.\\footnote{It is also known as the \\emph{generalized Reed-Muller code} in\n   871\t  the coding theory literature.}\n   872\t\n   873\tFix an integer $m \\in \\N$ and let $M = 2^m$.\n   874\tFor every $y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$ define the following $m$-variate multilinear\n   875\tpolynomial over $\\F_q$:\n   876\t\\[\n   877\t\t\\ind_{m,y}(x) = \\prod_{i: y_i = 1} x_i \\cdot \\prod_{i: y_i = 0} (1 - x_i) \\;.\n   878\t\\]\n   879\tHere, we identify $\\{0,1\\}$ as a subset of $\\F_q$.\n   880\tNotice that, when restricted to the subcube $\\{0,1\\}^m$, $\\ind_{m, y}$ is zero\n   881\teverywhere except for when $x = y$.\n   882\tFor $a\\in \\F_q^M$, label the coordinates of $a$ as $a_y$ for $y\\in \\{0,1\\}^m$\n   883\t(identifying the latter set with $\\{1,\\ldots,M\\}$ using, say, the lexicographic\n   884\tordering on strings).\n   885\tFor any such $a$ define the multilinear polynomial $g_a: \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$,\n   886\tcalled the \\emph{low-degree encoding of $a$}, as follows:\n   887\t\\begin{equation}\n   888\t\\label{eq:ld-encoding}\n   889\t\t g_a(x) = \\sum_{y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m} a_y \\cdot \\ind_{m,y}(x)\\;.\n   890\t\\end{equation}\n   891\tNote that for any $y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$, $g_a(y) = a_y$.\n   892\tFurthermore, the map $a\\mapsto g_a$ is linear: for every $x \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   893\t\\begin{equation}\n   894\t\\label{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}\n   895\t\tg_a(x) = a \\cdot \\ind_m(x)\\;,\n   896\t\\end{equation}\n   897\twhere $\\ind_m(x)$ is the vector $(\\ind_{m,y}(x))_{y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m}\\in\\F_q^M$.\n   898\t\n   899\t\\begin{lemma}\n   900\t\\label{lem:ld-encoding-complexity}\n   901\tLet $q$ be an admissible field size, and let $M = 2^m$ for some integer $m$.\n   902\tThe complexity of computing the evaluation of the low-degree encoding $g_a(x)$,\n   903\tgiven $a \\in \\F_q^M$ and $x \\in \\F_q^m$ represented in binary, is $\\poly(M,\\log\n   904\tq)$.\n   905\t\\end{lemma}\n   906\t\\begin{proof}\n   907\t\tComputing $g_a(x)$ requires computing the sum of products $a_y \\cdot\n   908\t  \\ind_{m,y}(x)$ over all $y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$.\n   909\t  Via \\Cref{lem:efficient_arithmetic}, evaluating $\\ind_{m,y}(x)$ takes time\n   910\t  $\\poly(m,\\log q)$ because it requires performing $m$ multiplications of $\\F_q$\n   911\t  elements, and therefore the product $a_y \\cdot \\ind_{m,y}(x)$ requires\n   912\t  $\\poly(m,\\log q)$ time.\n   913\t  Computing the sum over all $y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$ requires $M \\cdot \\poly(m,\\log q)\n   914\t  = \\poly(M,\\log q)$ time, as claimed.\n   915\t\\end{proof}\n   916\t\n   917\tFinally, for any $H \\subseteq \\F_q$ we define the \\emph{decoding} map\n   918\t$\\coded_H(\\cdot)$ which takes as input a polynomial $g : \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$ and\n   919\treturns the following vector $a \\in \\F_q^M$: for all $y \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$, if $g(y)\n   920\t\\in H$, then set $a_y = g(y)$, and otherwise set $a_y = 0$.\n   921\tNote that for all $a \\in H^M$ we have $\\coded_H(g_a) = a$ where $g_a$ is the\n   922\tlow-degree encoding of $a$.\n   923\tFor notational brevity we often write $\\coded(\\cdot)$ to denote the boolean\n   924\tdecoding map $\\coded_{\\{0,1\\}}(\\cdot)$.\n   925\t\n   926\t\\subsection{Linear spaces and registers}\n   927\t\\label{sec:lin-reg}\n   928\t\n   929\tFor a set $V$, we write $\\C^V$ for the complex vector space of dimension $|V|$.\n   930\tThe space $\\C^V$ is endowed with a canonical orthonormal basis\n   931\t$\\{\\ket{x}\\}_{x\\in V}$.\n   932\tBy ``a quantum state on $V$'' we mean a unit vector\n   933\t\\begin{equation*}\n   934\t  \\ket{\\psi}_V \\in \\complex^V\\;.\n   935\t\\end{equation*}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:207:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_105","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n '\\\\\\\\label\\\\{fact:omega-anticomm-prob\\\\}|\\\\\\\\label\\\\{lem:qld-win-implications\\\\}' references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex; nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex | sed -n '60,110p;230,270p'\"","aggregated_output":"71:\\label{fact:omega-anticomm-prob}\n198:\\label{lem:qld-win-implications}\n    60\tRecall the low-degree code defined in \\Cref{sec:ld-encoding}, in which vectors $h \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$ get mapped to polynomials $g_h \\in \\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ such that for every $u \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$, $g_h(u) = h_u$ where the coordinates of $h$ are indexed by binary strings of length $m$. For $x\\in \\F_q^m$, recall the definition of the vector $\\ind_m(x) \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$ from Section~\\ref{sec:ld-encoding}. Recall also the finite field trace $\\tr(\\cdot)=\\tr_{q\\to 2}(\\cdot):\\F_q\\to\\F_2$, where $q=2^k$ for odd $k$, defined in Section~\\ref{sec:subfields}. \n    61\t\n    62\t\n    63\t\n    64\tCall a tuple $\\omega = (u_\\xpt,u_\\zpt,r_\\xpt,r_\\zpt) \\in (\\F_q^m)^2\n    65\t\\times (\\F_q)^2$ \\emph{anticommuting} if the quantity \n    66\t \\[\\gamma\\,=\\,\\tr ((r_\\xpt \\cdot\n    67\t\\ind_m(u_\\xpt)) \\cdot (r_\\zpt \\cdot \\ind_m(u_\\zpt)))\\]\n    68\tintroduced in~\\Cref{eq:gamma-value} is not $0$, and \\emph{commuting} if $\\gamma=0$.\n    69\t\n    70\t\\begin{fact}\n    71\t\\label{fact:omega-anticomm-prob}\n    72\t\tThe probability that a tuple $\\omega= (u_\\xpt,u_\\zpt,r_\\xpt,r_\\zpt)$ sampled uniformly at random from $(\\F_q^m)^2 \\times (\\F_q)^2$\n    73\t\tis anti-commuting is at least \n    74\t\t\\[\n    75\t\t\t(1 - q^{-m}) \\cdot (1 - q^{-1}) \\cdot \\Big (1 - \\frac{md}{q} \\Big )  \\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\geq \\Big (1 - \\frac{3md}{q} \\Big ) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}.\n    76\t\t\\]\n    77\t\tThe probability that $\\omega$ is commuting is also at least $\\Big(1 - \\frac{3md}{q} \\Big ) \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}$.\n    78\t\\end{fact}\n    79\t\\begin{proof}\n    80\t\tFirst, note that $\\ind_m(u_\\xpt) = 0$ if and only if $u_\\xpt = 0$. If $u_\\xpt=0$ then $\\omega$ is commuting. Otherwise, if $u_\\xpt \\neq 0$, then observe that for all $r_\\xpt, r_\\zpt, u_\\zpt$,\n    81\t\t\\begin{align*}\n    82\t\t\t\\tr( (r_\\xpt \\cdot \\ind_m(u_\\xpt)) \\cdot (r_\\zpt \\cdot\n    83\t                \\ind_m(u_\\zpt))) &= \\tr( r_\\xpt r_\\zpt (\\ind_m(u_\\xpt) \\cdot \\ind_m(u_\\zpt))) \\\\\n    84\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\tr( (r_\\xpt r_\\zpt) \\cdot g_{\\ind_m(u_\\xpt)}(u_\\zpt))\\;,\n    85\t\t\\end{align*}\n    86\t\twhere $g_{\\ind_m(u_\\xpt)}$ is a nonzero polynomial in $\\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$.\n    87\t\t\n    88\t\tIf $r_\\xpt = 0$, then $\\omega$ is commuting. Condition on $r_\\xpt \\neq 0$.  Since $g_{\\ind_m(u_\\xpt)}\\neq 0$ has total degree at most $md$, by the Schwartz-Zippel lemma the probability that $g_{\\ind_m(u_\\xpt)}(u_\\zpt)$ is zero over a uniformly random choice of $u_\\zpt$ is at most $md/q$. Conditioned on $g_{\\ind_m(u_\\xpt)}(u_\\zpt) \\neq 0$, the product $(r_\\xpt r_\\zpt) \\cdot g_{\\ind_m(u_\\xpt)}(u_\\zpt)$ is a uniformly random element of $\\F_q$ when $r_\\zpt$ is chosen uniformly at random. \n    89\t\t\n    90\t\tThe probability that $u_\\xpt \\neq 0, r_\\xpt \\neq 0, g_{\\ind_m(u_\\xpt)}(u_\\zpt) \\neq 0$ is at least $(1 - q^{-m}) \\cdot (1 - q^{-1}) \\cdot (1 - md/q)$ since $u_\\xpt, r_\\xpt, u_\\zpt$ are chosen independently.\n    91\t\t\n    92\t\tSince $q = 2^t$ is an admissible field size, we have that for all $a \\in \\F_q$, the trace of $a$ is equal to $\\sum_i a_i$ where $(a_1,\\ldots,a_t)=\\kappa(a) \\in \\F_2^t$ are defined in Section~\\ref{sec:subfields}. Thus the probability that a uniformly random element of $\\F_q$ has trace $0$ is exactly $1/2$. \n    93\t\\end{proof}\n    94\t\n    95\tIn this appendix we often use the ``$\\approx_\\delta$'' notation to specify closeness of general operators (not necessarily POVMs) that are indexed by questions but not answers: given sets of operators $\\{A^x \\}$ and $\\{B^x\\}$ indexed by questions $x \\in \\cal{X}$, we write $A^x \\approx_\\delta B^x$ to denote \\[\n    96\t\t\\E_{x \\sim \\mu} \\bra{\\psi} (A^x - B^x)^\\dagger (A^x - B^x)\n    97\t        \\ket{\\psi}\\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta)\n    98\t      \\]\n    99\twhere $\\mu$ is a question distribution and $\\ket{\\psi}$ is a state that has been fixed by context. This will be useful for expressing closeness of observables, for example. The following two lemmas relate the closeness between sets of operators and weighted sums of those operators. \n   100\t\\begin{lemma}\n   101\t\\label{lem:avg-closeness}\n   102\tLet $\\{A^x \\}$ and $\\{B^x\\}$ be operators indexed by some finite set $\\mathcal{X}$, and let $\\mu$ be a probability distribution over $\\mathcal{X}$. Let $\\{\\alpha_x \\}$ be a set of complex numbers such that $|\\alpha_x| \\leq 1$. Then if $A^x \\approx_\\delta B^x$ on average over $x$ drawn from $\\mu$, then $A \\approx_\\delta B$, where $A = \\E_x \\alpha_x A^x$ and $B= \\E_x \\alpha_x B^x$.\n   103\t\\end{lemma}\n   104\t\\begin{proof}\n   105\tExpand\n   106\t\\begin{align*}\n   107\t\t\\Big\\| \\E_x \\alpha_x \\big ( A^x - B^x \\big) \\ket{\\psi} \\Big\\|^2 &\\leq \\Big( \\E_x \\norm{ (A^x -B^x) \\ket{\\psi}} \\Big)^2 \\\\\n   108\t\t&\\leq \\E_x \\norm{(A^x - B^x) \\ket{\\psi}}^2 \\\\\n   109\t\t&\\leq \\delta\\;.\n   110\t\\end{align*}\n   230\t\\begin{proof}\n   231\t\tSince the question types are sampled according to the type graph $G^\\Pauli$ of\n   232\t  finite size in \\cref{fig:type-graph-pauli} in the game\n   233\t  $\\game^\\pauli_\\qldparams$, the probability of any of the subtests of\n   234\t  $\\decider^\\pauli_\\qldparams$ (described in \\cref{fig:decider_pauli})\n   235\t  being executed is at least some universal constant.\n   236\t  Thus the consistency conditions expressed in Items\n   237\t  \\ref{enu:qld-cons},~\\ref{enu:qld-ld},~\\ref{enu:qld-pauli-basis-cons} of the\n   238\t  lemma follow directly from the corresponding tests in\n   239\t  \\cref{fig:decider_pauli} (where \\Cref{enu:qld-ld} follows from the\n   240\t  consistency test that is a subtest of $\\decider^\\ld_\\ldparams$) and the fact\n   241\t  that the strategy $\\strategy$ is assumed to succeed with probability at least\n   242\t  $1 - \\eps$ in $\\game^\\pauli_\\qldparams$.\n   243\t\t\t\t\t\n   244\t  For the remaining items, recall that by Fact~\\ref{fact:omega-anticomm-prob}\n   245\t  the probability that a tuple $\\omega=(u_\\xpt,u_\\zpt,r_\\xpt,r_\\zpt)$ sampled as\n   246\t  question content for a question of type $\\Pair$, $(\\Pair,W)$, $\\Constraint_i$\n   247\t  or $\\Variable_j$ is commuting is at least $(1 - 3md/q) \\cdot (1/2)$, which is\n   248\t  at least $1/4$ assuming $6md \\leq q$. We claim this assumption holds\n   249\t  without loss of generality. Indeed, observe that the function\n   250\t  $\\delta$ in \\Cref{thm:pauli-appendix} is monotonically increasing in $a$, so without loss of generality we may assume that $a\n   251\t  \\geq 6$.  Then it follows that we may assume that $6md \\leq q$\n   252\t  without loss of generality, since otherwise, $\\delta \\geq a md /q\n   253\t  \\geq 1$ and the conclusion of the theorem holds trivially.\n   254\t  The same lower bound holds for the probability that $\\omega$ is anticommuting.\n   255\t  Therefore, the consistency conditions in\n   256\t  Items~\\ref{enu:qld-comm},~\\ref{enu:qld-comm-cons} and~\\ref{enu:qld-ms-cons} of\n   257\t  the lemma also follow directly from the corresponding tests in\n   258\t  \\cref{fig:decider_pauli}.\n   259\t  Finally, \\Cref{enu:qld-ms} in the lemma follows from the fact that the Magic\n   260\t  Square check in \\cref{fig:decider_pauli} is executed with constant probability\n   261\t  over the choice of a pair of questions.\n   262\t\t\t\t\t\n   263\tThat all of the approximations in the Lemma statement also hold with\n   264\t$\\simeq_\\eps$ replaced by $\\approx_\\eps$ is immediate from Fact~\\ref{fact:agreement}.\n   265\t\\end{proof}\n   266\t\n   267\t\\begin{lemma}\n   268\t\\label{len:qld-win-implications-obs}The following hold for the observables defined in~\\eqref{eq:qld-strat-obs}. \n   269\t\t\\begin{itemize}\n   270\t\t\t\\item For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, $r \\in \\F_q$, on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$ we have\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:222:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_113","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex | sed -n '155,190p;270,305p'\"","aggregated_output":"   155\t\\begin{definition}[Consistent strategies]\\label{def:consistent-strategy}\n   156\t\t\\uses{def:consistent-measurement, def:projective-strategy-general}\n   157\t\tLet $\\mathcal S = (\\ket{\\psi}, A, B)$ be a projective strategy with state $\\ket{\\psi} \\in \\mathcal H \\ot \\mathcal H$, for some Hilbert space $\\mathcal H$, defined on question alphabets $\\mathcal X$ and $\\mathcal Y$ and answer alphabets $\\mathcal A$ and $\\mathcal B$, respectively. We say that $\\mathcal S$ is \\emph{consistent} if for all $x \\in \\mathcal X$ the measurement $\\{A^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ is consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$, and for all $y \\in \\mathcal Y$ the measurement $\\{B^y_b\\}_{b \\in \\mathcal B}$ is consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$.\n   158\t\\end{definition}\n   159\t\n   160\tWe almost always restrict attention to symmetric strategies; in this case consistency implies that whenever both players are sent the same question, they provide the same answer.\n   161\t\n   162\t\\begin{definition}[PCC and SPCC strategies]\\label{def:spcc}\n   163\t\t\\uses{def:projective-strategy-general, def:consistent-strategy, def:comm-strategy, def:symmetric-game}\n   164\t\tWe say that a strategy $\\mathcal S$ for a game $\\mathfrak G$ is \\emph{PCC} if it is projective, consistent, and commuting for $\\mathfrak G$. Additionally, we say that a PCC strategy $\\mathcal S$ is \\emph{SPCC} if it is furthermore symmetric.\n   165\t\\end{definition}\n   166\t\n   167\t\\section{Distance measures}\n   168\t\n   169\t\\begin{definition}[Distance between states]\\label{def:state-distance}\n   170\t\tLet $\\{\\ket{\\psi_n}\\}_{n \\in \\N}$ and $\\{\\ket{\\psi'_n}\\}_{n \\in \\N}$ be two families of states in the same space $\\mathcal H$. For a function $\\delta \\colon \\N \\to [0,1]$ we say that $\\{\\ket{\\psi_n}\\}$ and $\\{\\ket{\\psi'_n}\\}$ are \\emph{$\\delta$-close}, denoted $\\ket{\\psi} \\approx_\\delta \\ket{\\psi'}$, if\n   171\t\t\\[\n   172\t\t\t\\lVert \\ket{\\psi_n} - \\ket{\\psi'_n} \\rVert^2 = O(\\delta(n))\\,.\n   173\t\t\\]\n   174\t\tThe dependence of the states and of $\\delta$ on the indexing parameter $n$ is generally left implicit; we write, e.g., $\\ket{\\psi}$ for $\\ket{\\psi_n}$.\n   175\t\\end{definition}\n   176\t\n   177\t\\begin{definition}[Consistency between POVMs]\\label{def:consistency}\n   178\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:state-distance}\n   179\t\tLet $\\mathcal X$ be a finite set and $\\mu$ a distribution on $\\mathcal X$. Let $\\ket{\\psi} \\in \\mathcal H_A \\ot \\mathcal H_B$ be a quantum state, and for all $x \\in \\mathcal X$ let $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ be POVMs. We write\n   180\t\t\\[\n   181\t\t\tA^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a\n   182\t\t\\]\n   183\t\ton state $\\ket{\\psi}$ and distribution $\\mu$ if\n   184\t\t\\[\n   185\t\t\t\\E_{x \\sim \\mu} \\sum_{a \\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^x_b \\ket{\\psi} = O(\\delta)\\,.\n   186\t\t\\]\n   187\t\tIn this case we say that $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ are \\emph{$\\delta$-consistent} on $\\ket{\\psi}$. As in Definition~\\ref{def:state-distance}, the $O(\\cdot)$ refers to families of POVMs and states indexed by an implicit parameter $n \\in \\N$, and is taken as $n \\to \\infty$.\n   188\t\\end{definition}\n   189\t\n   190\tA measurement that is consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$ in the sense of Definition~\\ref{def:consistent-measurement} is $0$-consistent with itself on the same state under the singleton distribution, and vice versa: if $\\sum_a \\bra{\\psi} M_a \\ot M_a \\ket{\\psi} = 1$, then $M_a \\ot \\Id\\, \\ket{\\psi} = \\Id \\ot M_a\\, \\ket{\\psi}$ for every $a$, by the equality case of the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality applied outcome by outcome.\n   270\t\twhere the last step uses $A^x_a \\le \\Id$. All expectations are taken according to $\\mu$, and the last quantity is at most $\\delta$ by assumption.\n   271\t\\end{proof}\n   272\t\n   273\t\\begin{lemma}[Triangle inequality; Fact 4.28 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:triangle}\n   274\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   275\t\tIf $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ and $B^x_a \\approx_{\\eps} C^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\approx_{\\delta+\\eps} C^x_a$.\n   276\t\\end{lemma}\n   277\t\\begin{proof}\n   278\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   279\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.28 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For every $x$ and $a$, $\\lVert (A^x_a - C^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 \\le 2\\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 + 2\\lVert (B^x_a - C^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$. Averaging over $x$ and summing over $a$ gives the claim; the factor $2$ is absorbed by the $O(\\cdot)$ in Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance}.\n   280\t\\end{proof}\n   281\t\n   282\t\\begin{lemma}[Triangle inequality for ``$\\simeq$''; Proposition 4.29 in \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}]\\label{fact:triangle-for-simeq}\n   283\t\t\\uses{def:consistency}\n   284\t\tIf $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\eps} \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, and $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, and $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\gamma \\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$, then\n   285\t\t\\[\n   286\t\t\tA^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\eps + 2\\sqrt{\\delta+\\gamma}} \\Id_A \\ot D^x_a\\,.\n   287\t\t\\]\n   288\t\\end{lemma}\n   289\t\\begin{proof}\n   290\t\t\\uses{fact:agreement}\n   291\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Proposition 4.29 of \\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}. The two hypotheses involving $C$ convert, via Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, into state-dependent closeness of $C^x_a \\ot \\Id_B$ to both $\\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ and $\\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$, hence of $\\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ to $\\Id_A \\ot D^x_a$ at cost $O(\\delta+\\gamma)$. The first consistency then transfers from $B$ to $D$ by expanding the off-diagonal mass and applying the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, which turns the squared-distance bound into the term $2\\sqrt{\\delta+\\gamma}$.\n   292\t\\end{proof}\n   293\t\n   294\t\\begin{lemma}[Data processing; Fact 4.26 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:data-processing}\n   295\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket}\n   296\t\tSuppose $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$. Then for any function $f$ on the outcome set,\n   297\t\t\\[\n   298\t\t\tA^x_{[f(\\cdot)=b]} \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{[f(\\cdot)=b]}\\,.\n   299\t\t\\]\n   300\t\\end{lemma}\n   301\t\\begin{proof}\n   302\t\tStated in the source without proof; it is Fact 4.26 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Every off-diagonal pair $(b, b')$ with $b \\ne b'$ of the post-processed families pulls back to pairs $(a, a')$ with $f(a) = b \\ne b' = f(a')$, and in particular $a \\ne a'$. Coarse-graining therefore only merges terms of the off-diagonal sum, so the post-processed off-diagonal mass is at most the original one.\n   303\t\\end{proof}\n   304\t\n   305\tThe state-dependent distance is the right tool for reasoning about the closeness of measurement operators in a strategy. The following lemma converts joint consistency with a single projective measurement into an approximate commutation relation.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
