R(x)) = \\bigl( (L^{(i)}(x^{(i)}))_{i=1}^m,\\, (R^{(i)}(x^{(i)}))_{i=1}^m \\bigr)$ is distributed exactly as the product of the distributions $\\mu_{L^{(i)},\\,R^{(i)}}$ for $i = 1, 2, \\dots, m$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex:526:\tThis is identical to the uniform distribution over ordered pairs $(u,v) \\in U \\times U$ such that $\\{u,v\\} \\in E$: each edge that is not a self-loop contributes two ordered pairs, and each self-loop contributes one, whence the normalization $2m-k$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:15:The classical low individual degree game is a two-player game based on the low individual degree variant of the multilinearity test of Babai, Fortnow, and Lund. In the basic version, one player (the ``points player'') receives a uniformly random point $u \\in \\F_q^m$ and answers with a value $a \\in \\F_q$, while the other player (the ``lines player'') receives a random line through $u$ --- either axis-parallel or diagonal --- and answers with a univariate polynomial describing the restriction of a global function to that line. If the players share a polynomial $g\\colon \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$ of individual degree at most $d$ and answer with $g(u)$ and with the restriction $g|_\\ell$, they win with probability $1$; the soundness theorem below states that the converse approximately holds. In the simultaneous version, the players' answers are $k$-tuples, checked coordinatewise against a $k$-tuple of low individual degree polynomials.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:50:\tThe question distribution of $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}$ is the typed CL distribution obtained as follows: sample a type pair $(t_{\\mathrm A}, t_{\\mathrm B})$ uniformly from $\\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{ld}} \\times \\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{ld}}$, sample $z = (u,s,v)$ uniformly from $V$, and send each player $w \\in \\{\\mathrm A,\\mathrm B\\}$ the question $(t_w, L_{t_w}(z))$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:57:\tConsider the pair $(\\ell, u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the tuple $\\bigl((u_0,s,0),(u,0,0)\\bigr)$ from the CL distribution $\\mu_{L_{\\mathsf{ALine}}, L_{\\mathsf{Point}}}$, and let $\\ell = \\ell(u_0, e_i)$ where $i = \\chi(s)$. Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\F_q^m$, and $\\ell$ is a line that goes through $u$ and is parallel to a uniformly random axis $i \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:61:\tSince $s$ is uniformly random in $\\F_q$ and $m$ divides $q$, the index $i = \\chi(s)$ is uniformly random in $\\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$, so $(u_0,s)$ specifies a uniformly random axis-parallel line $\\ell(u_0,e_i)$. Since $u_0 = L^{\\mathrm{Ln}}_{e_i}(u)$ is the canonical representative of the line through $u$ in direction $e_i$, we have $u \\in \\ell(u_0,e_i)$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:66:\tConsider the pair $(\\ell, u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the tuple $\\bigl((u_0,s,v),(u,0,0)\\bigr)$ from the CL distribution $\\mu_{L_{\\mathsf{DLine}}, L_{\\mathsf{Point}}}$, and let $\\ell = \\ell(u_0, v)$ where $i = \\chi(s)$. Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\F_q^m$, and $\\ell$ is a random diagonal line that goes through $u$ and shares its first $i-1$ coordinates with $u$, for uniformly random $i \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:136:\twhere $\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}} = \\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}(\\eps,q,m,d,k)$, all three approximations are with respect to the state $\\ket{\\psi}$, and the bracket notation for post-processed outcomes is that of Definition~\\ref{def:bracket}. The first two approximations hold on average over the uniform distribution on $u \\in \\F_q^m$; no question distribution is associated with the third.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex:171:\tThe question distribution is as follows: the verifier samples $\\mathsf{Constraint}_i$ uniformly from $\\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{mc}}$, then samples $\\mathsf{Variable}_j$ uniformly among the three variables appearing in the row or column of $\\mathsf{Constraint}_i$; one player, chosen uniformly at random, receives $\\mathsf{Constraint}_i$ and the other receives $\\mathsf{Variable}_j$. Equivalently, the verifier samples a uniformly random edge of the bipartite \\emph{type graph} $G^{\\mathrm{ms}}$ on vertex sets $\\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{mc}}$ and $\\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{mv}}$, whose edges are $\\mathsf{Constraint}_i \\,\\text{---}\\, \\mathsf{Variable}_{3(i-1)+j}$ for $i,j \\in \\{1,2,3\\}$ and $\\mathsf{Constraint}_i \\,\\text{---}\\, \\mathsf{Variable}_{(i-3)+3(j-1)}$ for $i \\in \\{4,5,6\\}$, $j \\in \\{1,2,3\\}$ ($18$ edges in total, and no self-loops), and sends one endpoint to a uniformly random player and the other endpoint to the other player.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:257:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_131","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex | sed -n '1,175p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\chapter{Extracting the Pauli observables}\n     2\t\\label{chap:qpbt-extraction}\n     3\t% Paper subsection label sec:separating kept for cross-reference fidelity.\n     4\t\\label{sec:separating}\n     5\t% Source: references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex,\n     6\t% lines 1415--1877 (subsection ``Pulling the X and Z measurements apart''\n     7\t% and the closing proof of the soundness theorem).\n     8\t\n     9\tThis chapter completes the analysis of the Pauli basis test~\\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}. Throughout, $(q,m,d)$ is an admissible parameter tuple (Definition~\\ref{def:admissible}), $q = 2^t$, and $M = 2^m$ denotes the number of $q$-ary qudits tested for per player. (The letter $M$ also denotes measurement operators, always carrying super- and subscripts; the meaning is determined by context, as in the source.) We fix a projective strategy for the Pauli basis test game $\\mathfrak{G}^{\\mathrm{Pauli}}_{(q,m,d)}$ that succeeds with probability at least $1-\\eps$, in the setting of Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-observables}: the shared state $\\ket{\\psi}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}$, $\\mathrm{B}$ is assumed padded with sufficiently many ancilla qubits in the state $\\ket{0}$ (Definition~\\ref{def:projective-strategy-setup}), and $\\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ denotes the expanded state on the six registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, obtained by adjoining to each player the two halves of $M$ pairs $\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}$ (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-state}). Unless stated otherwise, every $\\approx_\\delta$ and $\\simeq_\\delta$ statement below is taken with respect to the state $\\ket{\\hat\\psi}$, over the question distribution named in the statement, with register placements indicated by subscripts; ``all symmetric equivalents'' refers to the register interchanges of Remark~\\ref{def:symmetric-equivalents}.\n    10\t\n    11\tWe use the following objects from the preceding chapters: the combined point projectors $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ acting on the expanded space (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}); the projective polynomial-pair measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, with outcomes pairs of polynomials in $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ and error function\n    12\t\\[\n    13\t\t\\delta_S(\\eps,m,d,q) = a(md)^a\\bigl(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd}\\bigr)\n    14\t\\]\n    15\tfor universal constants $a > 1$, $0 < b < 1$; the decoding map $\\mathrm{Dec} = \\mathrm{Dec}_{\\F_q}\\colon \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q) \\to \\F_q^{M}$ of the low-degree code (Definition~\\ref{def:decoding-map}, applied with $H = \\F_q$; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}), which maps a polynomial $g$ to the string of its evaluations on the hypercube $\\{0,1\\}^m$; and the low-degree encoding $h \\mapsto g_h$ together with the vectors $\\mathrm{ind}_m(u) \\in \\F_q^M$, characterized by $h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_h(u)$ for all $u \\in \\F_q^m$ \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}. Both maps are $\\F_q$-linear, and $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_h) = h$ for every $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$. We call a polynomial $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ an \\emph{encoding} if $g = g_h$ for some $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$ or, equivalently, if $g$ is multilinear; since a multilinear polynomial is determined by its values on $\\{0,1\\}^m$, the encodings are exactly the $g$ with $g_{\\mathrm{Dec}(g)} = g$, and\n    16\t\\[\n    17\t\t\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g(u)\n    18\t\t\\qquad \\text{for every encoding $g$ and every } u \\in \\F_q^m\\;.\n    19\t\\]\n    20\tFor a $g$ that is not an encoding the two sides of this identity are, as functions of $u$, distinct polynomials; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}.\n    21\t\n    22\t% Local correction of the source (lines 1419 and 1492 of\n    23\t% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex): the source invokes its boolean\n    24\t% decoding map but describes it as returning all hypercube evaluations, and\n    25\t% asserts the decoder--encoder identity for every g of individual degree at\n    26\t% most d. The remark below records the corrected statements.\n    27\t\\begin{remark}[Choice of decoding map and the decoder--encoder identity]\\label{rem:qld-decoding-identity}\n    28\t\tThe source states the constructions of this chapter in terms of its boolean decoding map (the case $H = \\{0,1\\}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:decoding-map}) while describing that map as returning the string of \\emph{all} evaluations of $g$ on the hypercube. The proofs require the latter reading, $H = \\F_q$, which is adopted throughout this chapter: the linearity of $\\mathrm{Dec}$ and the identity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_h) = h$ for all $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$, both of which fail for the boolean map, are used in the symmetry step of the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} and in the conjugation identity \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}. The source further asserts the identity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g(u)$ for every $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$. Since the left-hand side equals $g_{\\mathrm{Dec}(g)}(u)$, the identity holds exactly when $g$ is an encoding, and fails otherwise: for $m = 1$, $d \\geq 2$, and $q > 2$, the polynomial $g(x) = x^2$ has $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_1(u) = u \\neq u^2$. Accordingly, the proofs below invoke the identity only for encodings; in the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} the contribution of the remaining outcomes is controlled by the bound \\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} on the mass that the measurement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} places on polynomials that are not encodings, while in the conjugation identity \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the quantity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly.\n    29\t\\end{remark}\n    30\t\n    31\t\\section{The pulled-apart measurements and observables}\n    32\t\n    33\tThe measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ reads out an $X$-polynomial and a $Z$-polynomial simultaneously. We now separate the two readings again, this time as commuting actions on distinct registers: the polynomial outcome is measured on the players' original registers, while a genuine Pauli measurement acts on the EPR register $\\mathrm{A}''$ (resp.\\ $\\mathrm{B}''$).\n    34\t\n    35\t\\begin{definition}[Single-basis marginals of the polynomial-pair measurement]\\label{def:s-w-marginals}\n    36\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7}\n    37\t\tLet $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ be the projective measurement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}. For every $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ define\n    38\t\t\\[\n    39\t\t\t\\hat{S}^X_g = \\sum_{g_Z} \\hat{S}_{g,g_Z}\n    40\t\t\t\\qquad \\text{and} \\qquad\n    41\t\t\t\\hat{S}^Z_g = \\sum_{g_X} \\hat{S}_{g_X,g}\\;,\n    42\t\t\\]\n    43\t\tthe sums ranging over $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$. Since $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ is projective, its elements are mutually orthogonal projectors; hence each $\\hat{S}^W_g$, for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, is a projector, and $\\{\\hat{S}^W_g\\}_{g}$ is a projective measurement with outcomes in $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$.\n    44\t\\end{definition}\n    45\t\n    46\t\\begin{definition}[Pauli dot-product projectors]\\label{def:tau-dot-product-projector}\n    47\t\t\\uses{def:generalized-pauli}\n    48\t\tFor $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$ let $\\tau^W_h$ denote the projector corresponding to measuring $(\\C^q)^{\\ot M}$ in the $W$ basis and obtaining the outcome $h$ (Definition~\\ref{def:generalized-pauli}). For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^{M}$, and $a \\in \\F_q$, define the projector\n    49\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:def-tauwu}\n    50\t\t\t\\tau^{W}_{a}(\\tilde{u}) = \\sum_{h \\in \\F_q^{M} \\,:\\, h \\cdot \\tilde{u} = a} \\tau^W_{h}\\;.\n    51\t\t\\end{equation}\n    52\t\tFor each fixed $\\tilde{u}$, the family $\\{\\tau^W_a(\\tilde{u})\\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ is a projective measurement on $(\\C^q)^{\\ot M}$.\n    53\t\\end{definition}\n    54\t\n    55\t\\begin{definition}[Pulled-apart Pauli basis measurements]\\label{def:tilde-m-measurement}\n    56\t\t\\uses{def:s-w-marginals, def:tau-dot-product-projector, def:decoding-map}\n    57\t\tFor all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^{M}$ define the measurement $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ by\n    58\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:tilde_M}\n    59\t\t\t\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a = \\sum_{g} \\hat{S}^W_g \\ot \\tau_{(\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a}^W(\\tilde{u})\\;,\n    60\t\t\\end{equation}\n    61\t\tthe sum ranging over $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$. If $\\hat{S}^W_g$ is viewed as an operator acting on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'$ (resp.\\ $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$), then $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a$ acts on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ (resp.\\ $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$), the $\\tau^W$ factor acting on $\\mathrm{A}''$ (resp.\\ $\\mathrm{B}''$). Each family $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ is a projective measurement, because the $\\hat{S}^W_g$ and the $\\tau^W$ projectors are projective and, for each $g$, the projectors $\\tau^W_{(\\mathrm{Dec}(g)\\cdot\\tilde{u})-a}(\\tilde{u})$ sum to the identity as $a$ ranges over $\\F_q$.\n    62\t\\end{definition}\n    63\t\n    64\t\\begin{definition}[Pulled-apart Pauli observables]\\label{def:tilde-w-observables}\n    65\t\t\\uses{def:tilde-m-measurement, def:dual-self-dual-normal-basis, def:subfield-trace}\n    66\t\tLet $\\{e_j\\}_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}}$ be the self-dual basis for $\\F_q$ over $\\F_2$ of Definition~\\ref{def:dual-self-dual-normal-basis}, and let $\\tr = \\tr_{q \\to 2} \\colon \\F_q \\to \\F_2$ be the subfield trace. For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M$, and $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$ define the operator\n    67\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:def-tildewj}\n    68\t\t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) = \\sum_{a \\in \\F_q} (-1)^{\\tr(e_j a)} \\, \\tilde{M}_a^{W,\\tilde{u}}\\;,\n    69\t\t\\end{equation}\n    70\t\tacting on the same registers as $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a$.\n    71\t\\end{definition}\n    72\t\n    73\t% In the source the following properties are stated inline with the\n    74\t% definition of the observables; they are recorded here as a lemma.\n    75\t\\begin{lemma}[Product form and Pauli relations of the pulled-apart observables]\\label{lem:tildew-product-form}\n    76\t\t\\uses{def:tilde-w-observables, def:s-w-marginals, def:generalized-pauli, def:decoding-map, lem:qld-4-7}\n    77\t\tLet $\\tau^W(\\cdot)$ denote the generalized Pauli $W$ observable (Definition~\\ref{def:generalized-pauli}). For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M$, and $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$,\n    78\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:tildewj-product-form}\n    79\t\t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})\n    80\t\t\t= \\Big( \\sum_{g_X,g_Z} (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\mathrm{Dec}(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} \\, \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z} \\Big) \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})\\;,\n    81\t\t\\end{equation}\n    82\t\twhere $g_W$ denotes $g_X$ if $W = X$ and $g_Z$ if $W = Z$. In particular each $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ is Hermitian and squares to the identity. Moreover, the pulled-apart observables satisfy exactly the twisted commutation relations of the generalized Pauli observables $\\tau^X(e_j \\tilde{u})$ and $\\tau^Z(e_{j'} \\tilde{v})$: for all $j,j' \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$ and $\\tilde{u},\\tilde{v} \\in \\F_q^M$,\n    83\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation}\n    84\t\t\t\\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u})\\, \\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\tilde{v})\n    85\t\t\t= (-1)^{\\tr((e_j \\tilde{u}) \\cdot (e_{j'} \\tilde{v}))}\\, \\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\tilde{v})\\, \\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u})\\;,\n    86\t\t\\end{equation}\n    87\t\twhere $\\tr((e_j \\tilde{u}) \\cdot (e_{j'} \\tilde{v})) = \\tr(e_j e_{j'} (\\tilde{u} \\cdot \\tilde{v}))$.\n    88\t\\end{lemma}\n    89\t\\begin{proof}\n    90\t\t\\uses{def:tilde-m-measurement, def:tau-dot-product-projector, def:s-w-marginals, lem:pauli-observable-expansion, lem:twisted-commutation, lem:qld-4-7}\n    91\t\tExpanding $\\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = \\sum_{h} (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\tilde{u} \\cdot h))}\\, \\tau^W_h$ on the right-hand side of \\eqref{eq:tildewj-product-form} (Lemma~\\ref{lem:pauli-observable-expansion}), grouping the $h$ according to the value $a' = h \\cdot \\tilde{u}$ into the projectors $\\tau^W_{a'}(\\tilde{u})$ of \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu}, collecting the pairs $(g_X,g_Z)$ with a common value $g_W = g$ into $\\hat{S}^W_g$ (Definition~\\ref{def:s-w-marginals}), and changing the summation variable to $a = (\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a'$ recovers, by \\eqref{eq:tilde_M} and \\eqref{eq:def-tildewj}, the operator $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$; this proves \\eqref{eq:tildewj-product-form}. Since the $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$ are mutually orthogonal projectors summing to the identity (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}) and $\\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})$ is a Hermitian involution, the product form exhibits $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ as Hermitian with square $\\sum_{g_X,g_Z} \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z} \\ot \\Id = \\Id$. For \\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation}, the two sign-weighted sums of the projectors $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$ appearing in the product forms of $\\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u})$ and $\\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\tilde{v})$ commute with each other, so the commutation phase is that of $\\tau^X(e_j \\tilde{u})$ and $\\tau^Z(e_{j'} \\tilde{v})$, which is $(-1)^{\\tr((e_j \\tilde{u}) \\cdot (e_{j'} \\tilde{v}))}$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:twisted-commutation}.\n    92\t\\end{proof}\n    93\t\n    94\t% Local correction of the source (lines 1451-1456 of\n    95\t% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex): the source asserts commutation\n    96\t% whenever j != j'.\n    97\t\\begin{remark}[The commutation phase across basis indices]\\label{rem:qld-cross-phase}\n    98\t\tFor $j \\neq j'$ the source asserts that $\\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u})$ and $\\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\tilde{v})$ commute for all $\\tilde{u}, \\tilde{v} \\in \\F_q^M$. Since $e_j e_{j'} \\neq 0$ and the bilinear form $(a,b) \\mapsto \\tr(ab)$ on $\\F_q$ is nondegenerate, there exist $\\tilde{u}, \\tilde{v}$ with $\\tr(e_j e_{j'} (\\tilde{u} \\cdot \\tilde{v})) = 1$, and for these \\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} yields anticommutation instead. By self-duality of the basis $\\{e_j\\}$, the dichotomy asserted by the source --- commutation for $j \\neq j'$, and the phase $(-1)^{\\tr((e_j\\tilde{u}) \\cdot (e_j\\tilde{v}))}$ for $j = j'$ --- agrees with \\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} whenever $\\tilde{u} \\cdot \\tilde{v} \\in \\{0,1\\}$, in particular for all $\\tilde{u}, \\tilde{v} \\in \\{0,1\\}^M$. The relations \\eqref{eq:tildew-twisted-commutation} are not used in the remainder of the chapter.\n    99\t\\end{remark}\n   100\t\n   101\t\\section{Consistency with the points measurements}\n   102\t\n   103\tThe next lemma shows that the pulled-apart measurements are consistent with the original points measurements of the strategy, and self-consistent. In its statement, and for the remainder of the chapter, $\\delta_S$ denotes the function of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, enlarged by an adjustment of the universal constants $a$ and $b$ so as to also dominate additional error terms of order $\\sqrt{\\eps}$, $\\eps$, and $md/q$ that the source absorbs into $\\delta_S$ along the way; such an adjustment is possible since, after shrinking $b$ to at most $1/2$, the term $\\eps^b$ dominates $\\sqrt{\\eps}$ and $\\eps$ for $\\eps \\leq 1$, while $(md)^a q^{-b}$ dominates $md/q$.\n   104\t\n   105\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency and self-consistency of the pulled-apart measurements]\\label{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}\n   106\t\t\\uses{def:tilde-m-measurement, def:tilde-w-observables, def:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:consistency, def:povm-distance, lem:qld-4-7, def:indicator-vector}\n   107\t\tThe measurements $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ and observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ satisfy the following properties.\n   108\t\t% The paper's statement also quantifies item 1 over j, but j does not occur\n   109\t\t% in item 1; the vacuous quantifier is dropped here.\n   110\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   111\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   112\t\t\t\\[\n   113\t\t\t\t\\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\simeq_{\\delta_S} M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}\n   114\t\t\t\\]\n   115\t\t\tand\n   116\t\t\t\\[\n   117\t\t\t\t\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''} \\simeq_{\\delta_S} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a\\;,\n   118\t\t\t\\]\n   119\t\t\twith the answer summation over $a \\in \\F_q$.\n   120\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, on average over uniformly random $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^{M}$,\n   121\t\t\t\\[\n   122\t\t\t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\ot \\Id_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\ot \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})\\;.\n   123\t\t\t\\]\n   124\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   125\t\\end{lemma}\n   126\t\\begin{proof}\n   127\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper, def:expanded-point-measurement, def:tilde-m-measurement, def:tau-dot-product-projector, def:decoding-map, def:indicator-vector, lem:qld-win-implications, lem:qld-comm-cons, fact:add-a-proj, fact:agreement, fact:triangle, fact:data-processing, def:bracket, lem:povm-to-obs, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}\n   128\t\t% Support restriction added: the source (line 1492 of\n   129\t\t% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex) rewrites Dec(g).ind_m(u) as\n   130\t\t% g(u) for every outcome g; see Remark rem:qld-decoding-identity.\n   131\t\tWe first bound the mass that the marginal measurements $\\{\\hat{S}^W_g\\}$ place on polynomials that are not encodings. On average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$, the point-consistency relation \\eqref{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob} of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} gives $(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''}$ (Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}); the consistency and Pauli-basis-consistency checks of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications} give, again on average over uniform $u$ and combining through Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}, Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, and Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle}, that $(M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{a'})_{\\mathrm{A}} \\approx_{O(\\eps)} (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a']})_{\\mathrm{A}}$; and replacing the point factor of the convolution form of $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}) accordingly costs the same error, because the projectors $\\tau^W_{a''}(\\mathrm{ind}_m(u))$ of \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} are mutually orthogonal (Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}). Writing\n   132\t\t\\[\n   133\t\t\tK^u_a = \\sum_{a' + a'' = a} \\bigl(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a']}\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\bigl(\\tau^W_{a''}(\\mathrm{ind}_m(u))\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n   134\t\t\\]\n   135\t\ta projective measurement, Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle} and Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} therefore give, using $\\delta_S \\geq \\eps$,\n   136\t\t\\[\n   137\t\t\t\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (K^u_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\geq 1 - O(\\delta_S)\\;.\n   138\t\t\\]\n   139\t\tExpanding $K^u_a$ over pairs $(h, h'')$ of Pauli outcomes, a term of the left-hand side with outcomes $(g, h, h'')$ is constrained by $g(u) = g_h(u) + g_{h''}(u) = g_{h + h''}(u)$, by the linearity of the encoding \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}. If $g$ is not an encoding then $g \\neq g_{h+h''}$, and both are polynomials of total degree at most $md$, so the constraint holds for at most an $md/q$ fraction of $u$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}). Since all terms are nonnegative and, for each $g$, the operators of the $(h,h'')$-sum add up to the identity, it follows that\n   140\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass}\n   141\t\t\t\\sum_{g \\,\\text{not an encoding}} \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta_S) + \\frac{md}{q} \\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta_S)\\;,\n   142\t\t\\end{equation}\n   143\t\tand symmetrically with the registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'$ in place of $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$. (If $d = 0$, every element of $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ is constant, hence an encoding, and the left-hand side vanishes.)\n   144\t\n   145\t\tFor the first item, in the case where $M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ acts on register $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$ on $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, expanding \\eqref{eq:tilde_M} and \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} and using the convolution form of the combined projectors $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}) gives the exact identity\n   146\t\t\\[\n   147\t\t\t\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   148\t\t\t= \\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_g \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''} \\ot (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\;.\n   149\t\t\\]\n   150\t\tFor encodings $g$ the subscript $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ equals $g(u)$; discarding the (nonnegative) terms with $g$ not an encoding and reinstating the corresponding terms with subscript $g(u)$, each of which is at most $\\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$, shows that the right-hand side is at least\n   151\t\t\\[\n   152\t\t\t\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_g \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''} \\ot (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} - O(\\delta_S)\n   153\t\t\t\\geq 1 - O(\\delta_S)\n   154\t\t\\]\n   155\t\tby \\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, and the register-interchanged relation follows analogously. For the second item one first shows that, on average over uniformly random $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M$,\n   156\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-pulling-cons}\n   157\t\t\t\\bigl( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\bigl( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}\\;:\n   158\t\t\\end{equation}\n   159\t\tright-multiplying by the resolution $\\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id$ of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} (with Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}) and expanding the products of $\\tau^W$ projectors via \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} and $h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_h(u)$ places $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''}$ within $O(\\delta_S)$ of a sum over pairs $(g,h)$ with $(\\mathrm{Dec}(g)-h)\\cdot\\tilde{u} = a$. One then applies, in turn, the self-consistency of the points measurements (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}) at cost $\\eps$; the exact identity $\\ket{\\hat\\psi} = \\sum_{h'} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ of the EPR registers; Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} twice more (once through a Cauchy--Schwarz detour of cost $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ using the self-consistency of the $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$, Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}); and finally a Schwartz--Zippel step (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}, for polynomials of total degree at most $md$) which replaces the event $(g'-g_{h'})(u) = (g-g_h)(u)$ by the polynomial identity $g'-g_{h'} = g-g_h$ at cost $md/q$. The resulting expression\n   160\t\t\\[\n   161\t\t\t\\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} = g - g_h \\\\ (\\mathrm{Dec}(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}}\n   162\t\t\t\\Bigl((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\tau_h^W)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\Bigr) \\cdot \\Bigl( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\Bigr)\n   163\t\t\\]\n   164\t\tis symmetric under exchanging $(g,h)$ with $(g',h')$: since $\\mathrm{Dec}$ is linear with $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_{h}) = h$ for all $h \\in \\F_q^M$ (Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}), the constraint $g' - g_{h'} = g - g_h$ implies $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) - h = \\mathrm{Dec}(g') - h'$, so the two constraint sets coincide. The same derivation therefore bounds the distance of the displayed expression to $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}$, and \\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-cons} follows after absorbing the error terms $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ and $O(md/q)$ into $\\delta_S$. Finally, since $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}$ is projective, Item~2 of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, data processing along the map $a \\mapsto \\tr(e_j a)$ (Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}), and Item~1 of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} yield $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}$ with answer summation over $b \\in \\F_2$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:povm-to-obs} converts this into the claimed self-consistency of the observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$.\n   165\t\\end{proof}\n   166\t\n   167\tThe following lemma was used in several derivations above. It is stated after Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} to follow the source, but its proof depends only on Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}, so there is no circularity.\n   168\t\n   169\t\\begin{lemma}[The marginals absorb the point measurements]\\label{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper}\n   170\t\t\\uses{def:s-w-marginals, def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state, def:povm-distance, lem:qld-4-7, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   171\t\tFor $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   172\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-sg-cons}\n   173\t\t\t\\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id\n   174\t\t\\end{equation}\n   175\t\tand\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:258:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_132","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex | sed -n '1430,1605p'\"","aggregated_output":"  1430\t\t\\begin{equation}\n  1431\t\t\\label{eq:tilde_M}\n  1432\t\t\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a =  \\sum_{g} \\hat{S}^W_g \\otimes \\tau_{(\\coded(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a}^W(\\tilde{u})\\;.\n  1433\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1434\t\tIf $\\hat{S}^W_g$ is viewed as an operator acting on registers $\\alice \\alice'$ (resp. $\\bob \\bob'$) then we view $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a$ as an operator acting on registers $\\alice \\alice' \\alice''$ (resp. $\\bob \\bob' \\bob''$). (When necessary we will indicate via subscripts which registers the operators are acting on.)\n  1435\tThe $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a$ are projective because the $\\hat{S}^W_g$ and $\\tau^W$ operators are projective.\n  1436\t\n  1437\t\n  1438\t\tFor all $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$ define the observable\n  1439\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:def-tildewj}\n  1440\t\t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) = \\sum_a (-1)^{\\tr(e_j a)} \\, \\tilde{M}_a^{W,\\tilde{u}} \\;,\n  1441\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1442\t\twhere $\\{e_j\\}_{j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}}$ denotes the self-dual basis for $\\F_q$ over $\\F_2$ specified in \\Cref{sec:subfields}. Let $\\tau^W$ be the generalized Pauli observable defined in Section~\\ref{sec:generalized-pauli}. Observe that\n  1443\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1444\t\t\\Big ( \\sum_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\coded(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} \\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\Big ) \\otimes \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})\n  1445\t\t&= \\sum_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\sum_h (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\coded(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))} (-1)^{\\tr(e_j(\\tilde{u} \\cdot h))} \\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\otimes \\tau^W_h\\\\\n  1446\t\t&= \\sum_{g_W} \\sum_a  (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\tilde{u} \\cdot\\coded(g_W) + a))} \\hat{S}_{g_W}^W \\otimes \\Big( \\sum_{h:\\, h\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}\\tau^W_h\\Big)\\\\\n  1447\t\t\t&= \\sum_{g_W} \\sum_a  (-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\tilde{u} \\cdot\\coded(g_W) + a))} \\hat{S}_{g_W}^W \\otimes \\tau^W_a(\\tilde{u})\\\\\n  1448\t\t\t&= \t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\;,\n  1449\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1450\t\twhere the first equality uses the definition of $\\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})$, the second uses the definition of $\\hat{S}^W_{g_W}$ and regroups terms, the third uses the definition~\\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} of $\\tau^W_a(\\tilde{u})$, and the last is by a change of variables. These equations in particular show that the observable $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ is Hermitian and squares to the identity, because the measurement $\\{ \\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\}$ is projective. \n  1451\t\tFurthermore, it can be verified by direct calculation that the observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ satisfy the same relations as the generalized Pauli group. Specifically, for all $j,j' \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, $\\tilde{u}, \\tilde{v} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$,\n  1452\t\t\\[\n  1453\t\t\t\\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\tilde{v}) = \\begin{cases}\n  1454\t\t\t\\tilde{Z}^{j'}(\\tilde{v}) \\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u})  & \\qquad j \\neq j' \\\\\n  1455\t\t\t(-1)^{\\tr((e_j \\tilde{u}) \\cdot (e_j \\tilde{v}))} \\tilde{Z}^{j}(\\tilde{v}) \\tilde{X}^j(\\tilde{u}) & \\qquad j = j'\n  1456\t\t\t\\end{cases}~.\n  1457\t\t\\]\n  1458\t\tThe next lemma shows that the measurements $\\{ \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\}$  and observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$  are consistent with the original points measurements, and are self-consistent. \n  1459\t\t\n  1460\t\n  1461\t\n  1462\t\n  1463\t\\begin{lemma}\n  1464\t  \\label{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis}\n  1465\tLet $\\delta_S$ as in Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}.\n  1466\tFor all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, all $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$ and $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, the measurements $\\{ \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\}_{a \\in \\F_q}$ and observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$ satisfy the following properties:\n  1467\t\\begin{enumerate}\n  1468\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, for all $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, and on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n  1469\t\t\\[\n  1470\t\t\t\\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\otimes \\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a \\simeq_{\\delta_S} M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''}\n  1471\t\t\\]\n  1472\t\tand\n  1473\t\t\\[\n  1474\t\t\t\\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''} \\simeq_{\\delta_S} \\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\otimes M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a \\;.\n  1475\t\t\\]\t\n  1476\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) On average over uniformly random $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$,\n  1477\t\t\\[\n  1478\t\t\t\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u}) \\otimes \\Id_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\otimes \\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})\\,.\n  1479\t\t\\]\n  1480\t\\end{enumerate}\n  1481\t\\end{lemma}\n  1482\t\n  1483\t\\begin{proof}\n  1484\tWe show the first item of the lemma for the case when $M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a$ acts on register $\\alice$ and $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a$ acts on registers $\\bob \\bob' \\bob''$ (the argument for when the $\\alice$ and $\\bob$ registers are interchanged proceeds analogously):\n  1485\t\t\\begin{align*}\n  1486\t\t\t&\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a \\otimes \\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1487\t\t\t&= \\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_{g,a} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a)_{\\alice} \\otimes (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_{\\coded(g) \\cdot \\ind_m(u) -a}(\\ind_m(u)))_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1488\t\t\t&= \\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_{g} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{\\coded(g) \\cdot \\ind_m(u)})_{\\alice \\bob''} \\otimes (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1489\t\t\t&= \\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_{g} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\bob''} \\otimes (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1490\t\t\t&\\geq 1 - \\delta_S\\;.\n  1491\t\t\\end{align*}\n  1492\t\tThe second line follows from expanding the definition of $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\ind_m(u)}_a$ using~\\eqref{eq:tilde_M}, the third line follows from the definition of $\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_a$ given in Section~\\ref{sec:expanding}, the fourth line follows from the fact that $\\coded(g) \\cdot \\ind_m(u) = g(u)$, and the fifth line follows from \\Cref{lem:qld-4-7}. This implies the first item of the Lemma.\n  1493\t\n  1494\t\n  1495\tTo show the second item we first argue that, on average over $\\tilde{u}$, \n  1496\t\t\\begin{equation}\n  1497\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-pulling-cons}\n  1498\t\t\t\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''}.\n  1499\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1500\t\tThis follows because on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$ and $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$ we have\n  1501\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1502\t\t\t&\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-0} \\\\\n  1503\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_S}& \\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice'\\alice''} \\cdot \\Big ( \\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes  (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-1} \\\\\n  1504\t\t\t=_{\\phantom{\\delta_S}}& \\sum_g \\Big ( (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_{(\\coded(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a}(\\tilde{u}))_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big(\\sum_{r,s: r + s = g(u)} (M^{(\\Point,W),u}_r)_{\\bob} \\otimes (\\tau_s^W(\\ind_m(u)))_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-2} \\\\\n  1505\t\t\t=_{\\phantom{\\delta_S}}& \\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}}  (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_h)_{\\alice''} \\otimes (M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)})_{\\bob}~. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-2b}\n  1506\t\t\\end{align}\n  1507\t\tThe first inequality follows from right-multiplying~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-0} by the approximation~\\eqref{eq:qld-sg-cons} in \\Cref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} (to be proved below) and using Fact~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}. Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-2} follows from expanding the definition of $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a$.  To obtain Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-2b}, we expanded the definitions of $\\tau^W_{(\\coded(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a}(\\tilde{u})$ and $\\tau^W_s(\\ind_m(u))$ using~\\cref{eq:def-tauwu}, and the fact that $h \\cdot \\ind_m(u) = g_h(u)$ (due to \\cref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}). Thus for all $g \\in \\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ and $s \\in \\F_q$ we have\n  1508\t\t\\[\n  1509\t\t\t\\tau^W_{(\\coded(g) \\cdot \\tilde{u}) - a}(\\tilde{u}) \\cdot \\tau^W_s(\\ind_m(u)) = \\sum_{\\substack{h: \\\\ (\\coded(g) - h) \\cdot \\tilde{u} = a \\\\ g_h(u) = s}} \\tau^W_h~.\n  1510\t\t\\]\n  1511\t\n  1512\t\n  1513\t\tUsing the self-consistency of $\\{M^{(\\Point,W),u}_a\\}$ shown in Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}, we then obtain\n  1514\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1515\t\t\t\\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-2b}} &\\approx_{\\eps} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\cdot \\Big((M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)})_{\\alice} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big ( M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)} \\Big)_{\\bob} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-3} \\\\\n  1516\t\t\t&\\approx_{0}\\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\cdot \\Big((M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)})_{\\alice} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big ( M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)} \\Big)_{\\bob} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-3a} \\\\\n  1517\t\t\t&= \\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\cdot \\Big((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big ( M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)} \\Big)_{\\bob} \\;. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-3b}\n  1518\t\t\\end{align}\n  1519\t\tThe ``$\\approx_0$'' in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-3a} indicates that the left hand side is equal to the right hand side when applied to the state $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$. Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-3a} follows from the self-consistency of the operators $\\{\\tau^W_h\\}$, and Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-3b} follows from the definition of $\\{ \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_a\\}$. Using the fact that $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} = \\sum_{h'} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$, we get that \n  1520\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1521\t\t\\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-3b}} & \\approx_0 \\sum_{\\substack{g,h: \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( M^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h)(u)} \\otimes \\sum_{h'} \\tau_{h'}^W \\otimes \\tau_{h'}^W\\Big)_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-4} \\\\\n  1522\t\t\t&= \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,h': \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\;. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-5}\n  1523\t\\end{align}\n  1524\t\tLine~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-5} follows from~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-4} by expanding the definition of $\\{ \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_a\\}$. Next, we argue that, on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n  1525\t\\begin{align}\n  1526\t\t\t\\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-5}} &\\approx_{\\delta_S} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,h': \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\otimes \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big)~. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-7}\n  1527\t\t\t\\end{align}\n  1528\t\t\tWe show this by bounding the magnitude of the difference. Using the fact that $\\hat{S}^W_g$ and $\\tau^W_h$ are projective, we have:\n  1529\t\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1530\t\t\t&\\E_u \\sum_a \\Big \\|\\sum_{\\substack{g,h,h': \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} (\\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\otimes (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\, \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big \\|^2 \\notag \\\\\n  1531\t      =& \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,h'}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big(((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\notag \\\\\n  1532\t      & \\hspace{20em} \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})^2_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\notag \\\\\n  1533\t\t\t\\leq& \\E_u \\sum_{g} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_g \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)}))_{\\alice \\alice'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\label{eq:qld-pulling-8} \\\\\n  1534\t\t\t\\leq& \\delta_S\\;. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-9}\n  1535\t\t\t\\end{align}\n  1536\t\t\tThe inequality in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-8} follows from the fact that for all $g,h$, the sum $\\sum_{h'} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})^2_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''}$ is at most $\\Id$. The inequality in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-9} follows from the second item of \\Cref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} (to be proved below). \n  1537\t\t\tNext, we show that on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n  1538\t\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1539\t\t\t\\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-7}} &=\\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-9a} \\\\\n  1540\t\t\t&\\approx_{O(\\delta_S + \\sqrt{\\eps})} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-10} \\\\\n  1541\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_S} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\label{eq:qld-pulling-11} \\\\\n  1542\t\t\t&\\approx_{md/q} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} = g -g_h \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big)~. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-12}\n  1543\t\t\t\\end{align}\n  1544\t\t\tBefore we justify the sequence of steps to go from Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-7} to Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12}, we first argue how this establishes Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-cons}, and hence the second item of the Lemma statement.\n  1545\t\t\t\n  1546\t\t\tAbsorbing the error terms $O(md/q)$ and $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ into $\\delta_S$, we have shown that \n  1547\t\t\t\\[\n  1548\t\t\t\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} = g -g_h \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big).\n  1549\t\t\t\\]\n  1550\t\t\tNote that the conditions $(\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a$ and $g - g_h = g' - g_{h'}$ are equivalent to the conditions $(\\coded(g') - h') \\cdot \\tilde{u} = a$ and $g - g_h = g' - g_{h'}$. Thus the expression in~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12} is symmetric between $g,h$ and $g',h'$, and so an analogous derivation shows that $\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''}$ is $\\delta_S$-close to~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12}, and thus $\\delta_S$-close to $\\Big ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\Big)_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''}$. This shows~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-cons}. Since $\\{ \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\}$ is projective, by using Item 2 of \\Cref{fact:agreement}, followed by \\Cref{fact:data-processing}, and then followed by Item 1 of \\Cref{fact:agreement}, we get for all $j \\in \\{1,\\ldots,t\\}$, on average over $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^\\nqubits$,\n  1551\t\\[\n  1552\t\t(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\alice \\alice' \\alice''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} ( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\bob \\bob' \\bob''}\n  1553\t\\]\n  1554\twhere the answer summation is over $b \\in \\F_2$. Finally, the second item of the Lemma follows from applying~\\Cref{lem:povm-to-obs}.\n  1555\t\n  1556\t\t\tWe now return to justifying the sequence of steps between Lines~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-7} and~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12}. The equality in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-9a} follows by left-multiplying~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-7} by $I_{\\bob \\bob'} = \\sum_{g'} (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'}$. \n  1557\t\t\t\n  1558\t\t\t\\paragraph{The approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-10}.} The approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-10} follows by bounding the magnitude of the difference:\t\n  1559\t\\begin{align}\n  1560\t\t\t&\\E_u \\sum_a \\Big \\| \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) \\neq (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big \\|^2 \\notag \\\\\n  1561\t&= \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) \\neq (g -g_h)(u)}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big(\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\notag\\\\\n  1562\t      &\\hspace{20em}\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{(g-g_h+g_{h'})(u)})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\notag \\\\\n  1563\t\t\t&\\leq \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h',a': \\\\ a' \\neq g'(u)}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big(\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a'} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\notag \\\\\n  1564\t\t\t&= \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\label{eq:qld-pulling-13a} \\\\\n  1565\t\t\t&\\approx_{O(\\sqrt{\\eps})} \\E_u \\sum_{g} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\alice \\bob''} \\otimes ( \\hat{S}^W_{g})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\label{eq:qld-pulling-13} \\\\\n  1566\t\t\t&\\leq \\delta_S \\;. \\label{eq:qld-pulling-14}\n  1567\t\\end{align}\n  1568\tThe approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-13} follows from the following calculation:\n  1569\t\\begin{align}\n  1570\t\t&\\Big | \\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-13}} - \\text{\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-13a}} \\Big | \\notag \\\\\n  1571\t\t&\\leq \\Big | \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\cdot ((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'}) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big | \\label{eq:qld-pulling-15} \\\\\n  1572\t\t& \\qquad + \\Big | \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'}) \\cdot (\\hat{S}^W_{g})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\cdot (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big | \\label{eq:qld-pulling-15a}\n  1573\t\\end{align}\n  1574\tUsing Cauchy-Schwarz, we can bound the first term on the right hand side of the inequality by\n  1575\t\\begin{align}\n  1576\t\t&\\Biggl( \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a} \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g} \\cdot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\Biggr)^{1/2} \\cdot \\notag \\\\\n  1577\t\t&\\Biggl( \\E_u \\sum_{g,a: a \\neq g(u)} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'}) \\cdot (\\hat{S}^W_{g})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\cdot \\notag\\\\\n  1578\t  & \\hspace{12em}((\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} - (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a})_{\\bob \\bob'})  \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\Biggr)^{1/2} \\notag \\\\\n  1579\t\t\\leq & \\sqrt{1} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\eps} \\notag\n  1580\t\\end{align}\n  1581\tThe inequality follows from the self-consistency of $\\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{a}$, as established in \\Cref{lem:qld-comm-cons}. Similarly, we can bound the second term in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-15a} by $\\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n  1582\tThis establishes the approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-13}. \n  1583\t\n  1584\tThe inequality in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-14} follows from \\Cref{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob} of \\Cref{lem:qld-4-7}. This concludes the derivation of the approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-10}.\n  1585\t\n  1586\t\\paragraph{The approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-11}.} We establish this approximation by bounding the magnitude of the difference:\n  1587\t\\begin{align*}\n  1588\t\t&\\E_u \\sum_a \\Big \\| \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}))_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big \\|^2 \\\\\n  1589\t\t=& \\E_u \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) \\\\ \\quad = (g -g_h)(u)}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\otimes ((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot \\notag \\\\\n  1590\t  &\\hspace{20em}(\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}))_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\\\\n  1591\t\t\\leq& \\E_u \\sum_{g'} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} ((\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}) \\cdot \\hat{S}^W_{g'} \\cdot (\\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,W),u}_{g'(u)}))_{\\bob \\bob'} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\\\\n  1592\t\t\\leq& \\delta_S \\;.\n  1593\t\\end{align*}\n  1594\tThe last inequality follows from the second item of \\Cref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} (to be proved below). \n  1595\t\n  1596\t\\paragraph{The approximation in Line~\\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-12}.} We establish this approximation by bounding the magnitude of the difference:\n  1597\t\\begin{align*}\n  1598\t\t&\\E_u \\sum_a \\Big \\| \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} \\neq g -g_h \\\\ (g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u) \\\\ (\\coded(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big \\|^2 \\\\\n  1599\t\t&= \\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} \\neq g -g_h}} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} \\Big((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\tau_h^W)_{\\alice''} \\Big) \\cdot \\Big( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\otimes (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\bob''} \\Big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} \\cdot \\E_u \\mathbf{1}[(g' - g_{h'})(u) = (g -g_h)(u)] \\\\\n  1600\t\t&\\leq md/q.\n  1601\t\\end{align*}\n  1602\tThe last inequality follows from the Schwartz-Zippel lemma: two distinct polynomials of total degree at most $md$ cannot agree on more than $md/q$ fraction of points. \n  1603\t\n  1604\t\t\t\n  1605\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:260:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_133","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"rg -n 'qld-nonencoding-mass|encoding|Dec_' blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex\"","aggregated_output":"blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex:325:done by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:15:for universal constants $a > 1$, $0 < b < 1$; the decoding map $\\mathrm{Dec} = \\mathrm{Dec}_{\\F_q}\\colon \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q) \\to \\F_q^{M}$ of the low-degree code (Definition~\\ref{def:decoding-map}, applied with $H = \\F_q$; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}), which maps a polynomial $g$ to the string of its evaluations on the hypercube $\\{0,1\\}^m$; and the low-degree encoding $h \\mapsto g_h$ together with the vectors $\\mathrm{ind}_m(u) \\in \\F_q^M$, characterized by $h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_h(u)$ for all $u \\in \\F_q^m$ \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}. Both maps are $\\F_q$-linear, and $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_h) = h$ for every $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$. We call a polynomial $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ an \\emph{encoding} if $g = g_h$ for some $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$ or, equivalently, if $g$ is multilinear; since a multilinear polynomial is determined by its values on $\\{0,1\\}^m$, the encodings are exactly the $g$ with $g_{\\mathrm{Dec}(g)} = g$, and\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:18:\t\\qquad \\text{for every encoding $g$ and every } u \\in \\F_q^m\\;.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:20:For a $g$ that is not an encoding the two sides of this identity are, as functions of $u$, distinct polynomials; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:28:\tThe source states the constructions of this chapter in terms of its boolean decoding map (the case $H = \\{0,1\\}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:decoding-map}) while describing that map as returning the string of \\emph{all} evaluations of $g$ on the hypercube. The proofs require the latter reading, $H = \\F_q$, which is adopted throughout this chapter: the linearity of $\\mathrm{Dec}$ and the identity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_h) = h$ for all $h \\in \\F_q^{M}$, both of which fail for the boolean map, are used in the symmetry step of the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} and in the conjugation identity \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}. The source further asserts the identity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g(u)$ for every $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$. Since the left-hand side equals $g_{\\mathrm{Dec}(g)}(u)$, the identity holds exactly when $g$ is an encoding, and fails otherwise: for $m = 1$, $d \\geq 2$, and $q > 2$, the polynomial $g(x) = x^2$ has $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_1(u) = u \\neq u^2$. Accordingly, the proofs below invoke the identity only for encodings; in the proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} the contribution of the remaining outcomes is controlled by the bound \\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} on the mass that the measurement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} places on polynomials that are not encodings, while in the conjugation identity \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the quantity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:131:\tWe first bound the mass that the marginal measurements $\\{\\hat{S}^W_g\\}$ place on polynomials that are not encodings. On average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$, the point-consistency relation \\eqref{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob} of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} gives $(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''}$ (Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}); the consistency and Pauli-basis-consistency checks of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications} give, again on average over uniform $u$ and combining through Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}, Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, and Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle}, that $(M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{a'})_{\\mathrm{A}} \\approx_{O(\\eps)} (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a']})_{\\mathrm{A}}$; and replacing the point factor of the convolution form of $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}) accordingly costs the same error, because the projectors $\\tau^W_{a''}(\\mathrm{ind}_m(u))$ of \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} are mutually orthogonal (Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}). Writing\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:139:\tExpanding $K^u_a$ over pairs $(h, h'')$ of Pauli outcomes, a term of the left-hand side with outcomes $(g, h, h'')$ is constrained by $g(u) = g_h(u) + g_{h''}(u) = g_{h + h''}(u)$, by the linearity of the encoding \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}. If $g$ is not an encoding then $g \\neq g_{h+h''}$, and both are polynomials of total degree at most $md$, so the constraint holds for at most an $md/q$ fraction of $u$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}). Since all terms are nonnegative and, for each $g$, the operators of the $(h,h'')$-sum add up to the identity, it follows that\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:140:\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass}\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:141:\t\t\\sum_{g \\,\\text{not an encoding}} \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta_S) + \\frac{md}{q} \\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta_S)\\;,\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:143:\tand symmetrically with the registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'$ in place of $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$. (If $d = 0$, every element of $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ is constant, hence an encoding, and the left-hand side vanishes.)\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:150:\tFor encodings $g$ the subscript $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ equals $g(u)$; discarding the (nonnegative) terms with $g$ not an encoding and reinstating the corresponding terms with subscript $g(u)$, each of which is at most $\\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$, shows that the right-hand side is at least\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:155:\tby \\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, and the register-interchanged relation follows analogously. For the second item one first shows that, on average over uniformly random $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M$,\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:238:\tcancels the sign $(-1)^{\\tr(e_j (\\mathrm{Dec}(g_W) \\cdot \\tilde{u}))}$ term by term, leaving $\\sum_{g_X,g_Z} \\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z} \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u}) = \\Id \\ot \\tau^W(e_j \\tilde{u})$. For \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}, expand $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$ by \\eqref{eq:tilde_M} and \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu}; by the orthogonality of the $\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}$, conjugation replaces, in the term of $\\hat{S}^W_g$, each projector $\\tau^W_h$ by $\\tau^W_{h + \\mathrm{Dec}(g)}$, using the identities $\\tau^X(s)\\, \\tau^Z_h \\,\\tau^X(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^Z_{h+s}$ and $\\tau^Z(s)\\, \\tau^X_h\\, \\tau^Z(s)^\\dagger = \\tau^X_{h+s}$. Substituting $h' = h + \\mathrm{Dec}(g)$ turns the constraint $h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = (\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)) - a$ into $h' \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = -a = a$, since $\\F_q$ has characteristic $2$; the quantity $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ cancels exactly, so no relation between it and $g(u)$ is used (Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}). Since $h' \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_{h'}(u)$ \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}, summing over $g$ gives \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6}; the computation for $V_{\\mathrm{A}}$ on registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''$ is identical.\nblueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex:263:\tIf $\\delta_S > 1/8$, then, after enlarging the universal constant in $\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}$, both items of the lemma hold with $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ an arbitrary unit state, since their left-hand sides never exceed $4$; assume therefore $\\delta_S \\leq 1/8$. Then the vector $(\\bra{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M})_{\\mathrm{A}''\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\vartheta}$ has squared norm at least $1 - 4\\delta_S \\geq 1/2 > 0$, and $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}$ may be defined as its normalization; the overlap of $\\ket{\\vartheta}$ with $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$ equals the norm of that vector and is at least $\\sqrt{1 - 4\\delta_S} \\geq 1 - 4\\delta_S$, which yields the first item with a bound of $8\\delta_S = O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$. For the second item, Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications} gives, on average over $u \\in \\F_q^m$, $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\eps \\Id \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ and $\\Id \\ot M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\simeq_\\eps M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\Id$, where $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} = \\sum_{h : g_h(u) = a} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h$; combining these with Item~1 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} by Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle-for-simeq}, using $\\delta_S \\geq \\eps$, yields $M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_{\\sqrt{\\delta_S}} \\Id \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$. Writing $\\ket{\\Delta} = \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$, the quantity to be bounded for the $\\mathrm{A}$-side relation equals $2 - 2\\sum_h \\bra{\\Delta} V_{\\mathrm{A}} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_h V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\ot (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\Delta}$, since $(\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} = (\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$. Replacing $(\\tau^W_h)_{\\mathrm{B}''}$ by the averaged coarse-graining $\\E_u \\sum_{h' : g_{h'}(u) = g_h(u)} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}''}$ costs at most $md/q$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree} (distinct $h \\neq h'$ have distinct encodings $g_h \\neq g_{h'}$, of total degree at most $md$), and by \\eqref{eq:qld-unitary-6} the resulting sum equals $\\E_u \\sum_{a} \\bra{\\Delta} V_{\\mathrm{A}} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} V_{\\mathrm{A}}^\\dagger \\ot V_{\\mathrm{B}} \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a V_{\\mathrm{B}}^\\dagger \\ket{\\Delta}$. Exchanging $\\ket{\\Delta}$ for $\\ket{\\vartheta}$ costs $O(\\delta_S^{1/4})$ by the first item, and the resulting expression $\\E_u \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a]} \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ is at least $1 - O(\\sqrt{\\delta_S})$ by the $\\simeq_{\\sqrt{\\delta_S}}$ relation above. Altogether the distance is $O(\\delta_S^{1/4} + md/q)$; the $\\mathrm{B}$-side relation is proved identically.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:262:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_134","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch16_qpbt_extraction.tex | sed -n '126,170p'\"","aggregated_output":"   126\t\\begin{proof}\n   127\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-7, lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper, def:expanded-point-measurement, def:tilde-m-measurement, def:tau-dot-product-projector, def:decoding-map, def:indicator-vector, lem:qld-win-implications, lem:qld-comm-cons, fact:add-a-proj, fact:agreement, fact:triangle, fact:data-processing, def:bracket, lem:povm-to-obs, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}\n   128\t\t% Support restriction added: the source (line 1492 of\n   129\t\t% 14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex) rewrites Dec(g).ind_m(u) as\n   130\t\t% g(u) for every outcome g; see Remark rem:qld-decoding-identity.\n   131\t\tWe first bound the mass that the marginal measurements $\\{\\hat{S}^W_g\\}$ place on polynomials that are not encodings. On average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$, the point-consistency relation \\eqref{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob} of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} gives $(\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''}$ (Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}); the consistency and Pauli-basis-consistency checks of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications} give, again on average over uniform $u$ and combining through Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}, Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, and Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle}, that $(M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{a'})_{\\mathrm{A}} \\approx_{O(\\eps)} (M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a']})_{\\mathrm{A}}$; and replacing the point factor of the convolution form of $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}) accordingly costs the same error, because the projectors $\\tau^W_{a''}(\\mathrm{ind}_m(u))$ of \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} are mutually orthogonal (Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}). Writing\n   132\t\t\\[\n   133\t\t\tK^u_a = \\sum_{a' + a'' = a} \\bigl(M^{(\\mathrm{Pauli},W)}_{[g_h(u)=a']}\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}} \\ot \\bigl(\\tau^W_{a''}(\\mathrm{ind}_m(u))\\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}''}\\;,\n   134\t\t\\]\n   135\t\ta projective measurement, Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle} and Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} therefore give, using $\\delta_S \\geq \\eps$,\n   136\t\t\\[\n   137\t\t\t\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_{[g(u)=a]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (K^u_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\geq 1 - O(\\delta_S)\\;.\n   138\t\t\\]\n   139\t\tExpanding $K^u_a$ over pairs $(h, h'')$ of Pauli outcomes, a term of the left-hand side with outcomes $(g, h, h'')$ is constrained by $g(u) = g_h(u) + g_{h''}(u) = g_{h + h''}(u)$, by the linearity of the encoding \\eqref{eq:low-degree-encoding-definition}. If $g$ is not an encoding then $g \\neq g_{h+h''}$, and both are polynomials of total degree at most $md$, so the constraint holds for at most an $md/q$ fraction of $u$ (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}). Since all terms are nonnegative and, for each $g$, the operators of the $(h,h'')$-sum add up to the identity, it follows that\n   140\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass}\n   141\t\t\t\\sum_{g \\,\\text{not an encoding}} \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta_S) + \\frac{md}{q} \\,\\leq\\, O(\\delta_S)\\;,\n   142\t\t\\end{equation}\n   143\t\tand symmetrically with the registers $\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'$ in place of $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'$. (If $d = 0$, every element of $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ is constant, hence an encoding, and the left-hand side vanishes.)\n   144\t\n   145\t\tFor the first item, in the case where $M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ acts on register $\\mathrm{A}$ and $\\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a$ on $\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''$, expanding \\eqref{eq:tilde_M} and \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} and using the convolution form of the combined projectors $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$ (Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement}) gives the exact identity\n   146\t\t\\[\n   147\t\t\t\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi} M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\ot \\tilde{M}^{W,\\mathrm{ind}_m(u)}_a \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   148\t\t\t= \\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_g \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''} \\ot (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\;.\n   149\t\t\\]\n   150\t\tFor encodings $g$ the subscript $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ equals $g(u)$; discarding the (nonnegative) terms with $g$ not an encoding and reinstating the corresponding terms with subscript $g(u)$, each of which is at most $\\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$, shows that the right-hand side is at least\n   151\t\t\\[\n   152\t\t\t\\E_{u \\in \\F_q^m} \\sum_g \\bra{\\hat\\psi} (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{B}''} \\ot (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ket{\\hat\\psi} - O(\\delta_S)\n   153\t\t\t\\geq 1 - O(\\delta_S)\n   154\t\t\\]\n   155\t\tby \\eqref{eq:qld-nonencoding-mass} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7}, and the register-interchanged relation follows analogously. For the second item one first shows that, on average over uniformly random $\\tilde{u} \\in \\F_q^M$,\n   156\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:qld-pulling-cons}\n   157\t\t\t\\bigl( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\bigl( \\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a \\bigr)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}\\;:\n   158\t\t\\end{equation}\n   159\t\tright-multiplying by the resolution $\\sum_g (\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{g(u)})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} \\Id$ of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} (with Lemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}) and expanding the products of $\\tau^W$ projectors via \\eqref{eq:def-tauwu} and $h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u) = g_h(u)$ places $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''}$ within $O(\\delta_S)$ of a sum over pairs $(g,h)$ with $(\\mathrm{Dec}(g)-h)\\cdot\\tilde{u} = a$. One then applies, in turn, the self-consistency of the points measurements (Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}) at cost $\\eps$; the exact identity $\\ket{\\hat\\psi} = \\sum_{h'} (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau^W_{h'})_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ of the EPR registers; Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper} twice more (once through a Cauchy--Schwarz detour of cost $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ using the self-consistency of the $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$, Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}); and finally a Schwartz--Zippel step (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}, for polynomials of total degree at most $md$) which replaces the event $(g'-g_{h'})(u) = (g-g_h)(u)$ by the polynomial identity $g'-g_{h'} = g-g_h$ at cost $md/q$. The resulting expression\n   160\t\t\\[\n   161\t\t\t\\sum_{\\substack{g,h,g',h': \\\\ g' - g_{h'} = g - g_h \\\\ (\\mathrm{Dec}(g)-h)\\cdot \\tilde{u} = a}}\n   162\t\t\t\\Bigl((\\hat{S}^W_g)_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'} \\ot (\\tau_h^W)_{\\mathrm{A}''} \\Bigr) \\cdot \\Bigl( (\\hat{S}^W_{g'})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'} \\ot (\\tau_{h'}^W)_{\\mathrm{B}''} \\Bigr)\n   163\t\t\\]\n   164\t\tis symmetric under exchanging $(g,h)$ with $(g',h')$: since $\\mathrm{Dec}$ is linear with $\\mathrm{Dec}(g_{h}) = h$ for all $h \\in \\F_q^M$ (Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-decoding-identity}), the constraint $g' - g_{h'} = g - g_h$ implies $\\mathrm{Dec}(g) - h = \\mathrm{Dec}(g') - h'$, so the two constraint sets coincide. The same derivation therefore bounds the distance of the displayed expression to $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a)_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}$, and \\eqref{eq:qld-pulling-cons} follows after absorbing the error terms $O(\\sqrt{\\eps})$ and $O(md/q)$ into $\\delta_S$. Finally, since $\\{\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_a\\}$ is projective, Item~2 of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}, data processing along the map $a \\mapsto \\tr(e_j a)$ (Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}), and Item~1 of Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} yield $(\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\mathrm{A}\\mathrm{A}'\\mathrm{A}''} \\approx_{\\delta_S} (\\tilde{M}^{W,\\tilde{u}}_{[\\tr(e_j \\cdot) = b]})_{\\mathrm{B}\\mathrm{B}'\\mathrm{B}''}$ with answer summation over $b \\in \\F_2$, and Lemma~\\ref{lem:povm-to-obs} converts this into the claimed self-consistency of the observables $\\tilde{W}^j(\\tilde{u})$.\n   165\t\\end{proof}\n   166\t\n   167\tThe following lemma was used in several derivations above. It is stated after Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-construct-the-paulis} to follow the source, but its proof depends only on Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}, so there is no circularity.\n   168\t\n   169\t\\begin{lemma}[The marginals absorb the point measurements]\\label{lem:qld-constructing-the-paulis-helper}\n   170\t\t\\uses{def:s-w-marginals, def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state, def:povm-distance, lem:qld-4-7, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:265:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_136","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex | sed -n '320,350p;510,590p;780,855p;860,940p'\"","aggregated_output":"   320\t\\label{sec:apply-ldt}\n   321\t\n   322\tThe combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next\n   323\tmerged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that\n   324\tencode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is\n   325\tdone by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking\n   326\tthe quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test\n   327\t(Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}).\n   328\t\n   329\t\\begin{definition}[The combining map]\n   330\t\t\\label{def:combine-map}\n   331\t\t\\uses{def:polyfunc, def:line}\n   332\t\tFor $f, g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$, the polynomial\n   333\t\t$\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g} \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,2m+2}(\\F_q)$ is defined by\n   334\t\t\\[\n   335\t\t\t\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g}(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta)\n   336\t\t\t= \\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(z)\\,,\n   337\t\t\t\\qquad x \\in \\F_q^m,\\ z \\in \\F_q^m,\\ \\alpha \\in \\F_q,\\\n   338\t\t\t\\beta \\in \\F_q\\,.\n   339\t\t\\]\n   340\t\tThe combining map is also defined for polynomials restricted to lines.\n   341\t\tLet $\\ell$ be a line in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ with intercept\n   342\t\t$u = (u_X, u_Z, u_\\alpha, u_\\beta)$ and slope\n   343\t\t$v = (v_X, v_Z, v_\\alpha, v_\\beta)$, and let $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$ be\n   344\t\tlines in $\\F_q^m$ such that for every point\n   345\t\t$(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\ell$ it holds that $x \\in \\ell_X$ and\n   346\t\t$z \\in \\ell_Z$. Then for any two polynomials\n   347\t\t$f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X)$ and $g \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$, the polynomial\n   348\t\t$\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g} \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$ is defined by\n   349\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   350\t\t\t\\label{eq:combine-lines}\n   510\t\tis at most $\\delta$ therefore bounds each mixture component by\n   511\t\t$2m\\delta$, and products of two such mixtures give the factor\n   512\t\t$(2m)^2 = 4m^2$. The instance \\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted} is item\n   513\t\t2 applied to the conclusion of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines}, whose\n   514\t\t$\\simeq_{\\delta_P}$ relation over two independent copies of the\n   515\t\tline-point distribution is precisely the statement that the left-hand\n   516\t\tside of \\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}, averaged over\n   517\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{Line}} \\times D_{\\mathrm{Line}}$, is at most $\\delta_P$.\n   518\t\\end{proof}\n   519\t\n   520\t\\begin{lemma}[The sub-line distribution]\n   521\t\t\\label{lem:qld-sublines}\n   522\t\t\\uses{def:line, def:line-point-dist, def:ith-restricted-line}\n   523\t\tThere exists a distribution $D$ over tuples $(\\ell, \\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$,\n   524\t\twhere $\\ell$ is a line in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and $\\ell_X, \\ell_Z$ are lines in\n   525\t\t$\\F_q^m$, with the following properties:\n   526\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   527\t\t\t\\item The marginal distribution over $\\ell$ is the same as the\n   528\t\t\tmarginal of the line-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$.\n   529\t\t\t\\item For any point of the form $u = (u_X, u_Z, \\alpha, \\beta)$\n   530\t\t\tlying on $\\ell$, it holds that $u_X \\in \\ell_X$ and\n   531\t\t\t$u_Z \\in \\ell_Z$. Moreover, for $u$ chosen uniformly at random on\n   532\t\t\t$\\ell$, the marginal distribution over $(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z, u_X)$\n   533\t\t\t(resp.\\ over $(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z, u_Z)$) is such that the marginal over\n   534\t\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$ is a mixture of the product distributions\n   535\t\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{ALine},i} \\times D_{\\mathrm{ALine},j}$ and\n   536\t\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{DLine},i} \\times D_{\\mathrm{DLine},j}$ for\n   537\t\t\t$i, j \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$, and, conditioned on\n   538\t\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$, the point $u_X$ is uniformly random on $\\ell_X$\n   539\t\t\t(resp.\\ $u_Z$ is uniformly random on $\\ell_Z$).\n   540\t\t\t\\item If $\\ell$ is axis-parallel, then $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$ are\n   541\t\t\taxis-parallel.\n   542\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   543\t\t(Property~3 is not part of the numbered statement in the source; see\n   544\t\tRemark~\\ref{rem:qld-sublines-property-three}.)\n   545\t\\end{lemma}\n   546\t\\begin{proof}\n   547\t\t\\uses{def:line-representative, def:ld-question-distribution,\n   548\t\t\tdef:line-point-dist, def:ith-restricted-line}\n   549\t\tThe distribution $D$ is described by a sampling procedure. First sample\n   550\t\t$(\\ell, u)$ from the line-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and write\n   551\t\t$u = (u_X, u_Z, \\alpha, \\beta)$, so that Property~1 holds by\n   552\t\tconstruction. If $\\ell$ is axis-parallel, $\\ell = (v, e_j)$ with\n   553\t\t$v = (v_X, v_Z, \\alpha_0, \\beta_0)$, set\n   554\t\t$\\ell_X = (v'_X, e_{i_X})$ and $\\ell_Z = (v'_Z, e_{i_Z})$ where: for\n   555\t\t$1 \\le j \\le m$, $i_X = j$ and $i_Z$ is uniformly random in\n   556\t\t$\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$; for $m+1 \\le j \\le 2m$, $i_X$ is uniformly random and\n   557\t\t$i_Z = j - m$; for $2m+1 \\le j \\le 2m+2$, both $i_X$ and $i_Z$ are\n   558\t\tuniformly random; and $v'_X, v'_Z$ are the canonical line\n   559\t\trepresentatives of $v_X, v_Z$ for the chosen directions\n   560\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:line-representative}), so that $v_X \\in \\ell_X$\n   561\t\tand $v_Z \\in \\ell_Z$. If $\\ell$ is diagonal, $\\ell = (v, s, w)$ with\n   562\t\tdirection $w = (w_X, w_Z, \\gamma, \\delta)$ whose first nonzero\n   563\t\tcoordinate has index $j = \\chi(s)$, sample\n   564\t\t$\\ell_X = (v'_X, s_X, w'_X)$ and $\\ell_Z = (v'_Z, s_Z, w'_Z)$ where: for\n   565\t\t$1 \\le j \\le m$, $w'_X = w_X$ and $w'_Z = w_Z$ with $s_X, s_Z$ chosen so\n   566\t\tthat $\\chi(s_X) = j$ and $\\chi(s_Z) = 1$; for $m+1 \\le j \\le 2m$,\n   567\t\t$w'_X$ is a uniformly random diagonal direction (with $s_X$ chosen\n   568\t\taccordingly) and $w'_Z = w_Z$ with $\\chi(s_Z) = j - m$; for\n   569\t\t$2m+1 \\le j \\le 2m+2$, both directions are uniformly random with\n   570\t\t$s_X, s_Z$ chosen accordingly; and again $v'_X, v'_Z$ are the canonical\n   571\t\trepresentatives. In each case a uniformly random point $u$ on $\\ell$\n   572\t\tprojects to a uniformly random point $u_X$ on $\\ell_X$ (resp.\\ $u_Z$ on\n   573\t\t$\\ell_Z$), and a case analysis over $j$ shows that the marginal over\n   574\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$ is a mixture of the required product distributions:\n   575\t\tfor instance, in the axis-parallel case with $1 \\le j \\le m$, the\n   576\t\tmarginal over $(\\ell_X, u_X)$ conditioned on $j$ equals\n   577\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{ALine},j}$, and even conditioned on $(\\ell_X, u_X)$ the line\n   578\t\t$\\ell_Z$ is distributed as $D_{\\mathrm{ALine},i_Z}$ for uniformly random\n   579\t\t$i_Z$; the remaining cases are analogous, differing only in how the\n   580\t\tindices are chosen. This gives Property~2. Property~3 is immediate from\n   581\t\tthe procedure: when $\\ell$ is axis-parallel, both $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$\n   582\t\tare constructed to be axis-parallel.\n   583\t\\end{proof}\n   584\t\n   585\t\\begin{remark}[Property~3 of the sub-line distribution]\n   586\t\t\\label{rem:qld-sublines-property-three}\n   587\t\tThe source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} in \\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}\n   588\t\tappeals to a ``Property~3'' of the sub-line lemma, while the statement\n   589\t\tgiven there lists only two numbered properties, so the reference is\n   590\t\tdangling. The intended assertion is the axis-parallel closure property\n   780\t\tFor every line $\\ell$ in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and $f \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$\n   781\t\tdefine\n   782\t\t\\[\n   783\t\t\t\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}_{f}\n   784\t\t\t= \\E_{(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D_{|\\ell}}\n   785\t\t\t\\sum_{\\substack{f_X \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X),\\\n   786\t\t\t\tf_Z \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z):\\\\\n   787\t\t\t\tf = \\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}}}\n   788\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\,,\n   789\t\t\\]\n   790\t\ta valid POVM; the polynomial $\\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}$ of\n   791\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:combine-map} is well defined here because Property~2\n   792\t\tof Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} guarantees that $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$\n   793\t\tare compatible with $\\ell$. If $\\ell$ is axis-parallel, then by\n   794\t\tProperty~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} so are $\\ell_X$ and\n   795\t\t$\\ell_Z$, hence $f_X, f_Z \\in \\deg_d$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines};\n   796\t\talong such a line either $(\\alpha,\\beta)$ is constant, in which case\n   797\t\t$f = \\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}$ is a fixed linear combination of\n   798\t\tpolynomials of degree at most $d$, or $(x,z)$ is constant, in which case\n   799\t\t$f$ is affine in the line parameter, so in either case\n   800\t\t$f \\in \\deg_d(\\ell)$. Chaining Lemmas~\\ref{lem:claim-17-1},\n   801\t\t\\ref{lem:claim-17-2} and~\\ref{lem:claim-17-3} with the triangle\n   802\t\tinequality for scalars gives the consistency bound\n   803\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   804\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}\n   805\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   806\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   807\t\t\t\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   808\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   809\t\t\t\\big(\\Id - \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}\n   810\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   811\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   812\t\t\t= O\\big(m\\,(\\eps^{1/4} + \\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4}\n   813\t\t\t+ \\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2})\\big)\\,;\n   814\t\t\\end{equation}\n   815\t\tthe function\n   816\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}} = \\poly(m^2\\eps, md/q)$ dominates this\n   817\t\tbound. (The source states a second bound for the same quantity, whose\n   818\t\tderivation is not supported by the properties proved for the sub-line\n   819\t\tdistribution; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}.) Now\n   820\t\tif the pair $(f_X, f_Z)$ evaluates to $(a, b)$ at $(x, z)$, then\n   821\t\t$\\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}$ evaluates to $\\alpha a + \\beta b$ at\n   822\t\t$(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta)$; hence the consistency of $T$ with\n   823\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}$ along the sub-line pairs expressed by\n   824\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}, the definition of\n   825\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}$ as the image of $T$ under the combining map, and the\n   826\t\tconstruction of $\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}$ in\n   827\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-12} as the corresponding coarse-graining of\n   828\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}$ together yield the first relation of\n   829\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-4-13}. The second relation follows from the analogous\n   830\t\targument with the registers swapped.\n   831\t\\end{proof}\n   832\t\n   833\t\\begin{remark}[Two error routes in the source proof of\n   834\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}]\n   835\t\t\\label{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}\n   836\t\tThe source proof in \\cite{Ji2020MIPStar} states two different bounds for\n   837\t\tthe quantity \\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}:\n   838\t\t$O(m(\\eps^{1/4} + \\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4} +\n   839\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}))$, obtained by chaining the three claims\n   840\t\t(Lemmas~\\ref{lem:claim-17-1}--\\ref{lem:claim-17-3}), and\n   841\t\t$O(m^2 \\delta_P)$, obtained by decomposing the left-hand side of\n   842\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency} into restricted-distribution\n   843\t\tterms and bounding each by \\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}; a\n   844\t\tsentence is also duplicated at the corresponding point of the source,\n   845\t\tthe second copy with a malformed reference. The second route requires\n   846\t\tthe joint law of the pair $((\\ell_X, x), (\\ell_Z, z))$, for\n   847\t\t$(\\ell, \\ell_X, \\ell_Z) \\sim D$ and $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$ uniform on\n   848\t\t$\\ell$, to be a mixture of the product distributions\n   849\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{v}_1,i} \\times D_{\\mathrm{v}_2,j}$, since\n   850\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted} averages over two independently\n   851\t\tsampled line-point pairs; Property~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines}\n   852\t\tdetermines only the two marginal laws of $(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z, x)$ and of\n   853\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z, z)$ separately, not the joint law of\n   854\t\t$((\\ell_X, x), (\\ell_Z, z))$, so the decomposition invoked by the\n   855\t\tsource is not established there. The proof above therefore uses only\n   860\t\tthe source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:claim-17-2} asserts the bound\n   861\t\t$O(m^2\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}})$ for a consistency term that is close to\n   862\t\tone rather than for its deficit; the statements and sketches above\n   863\t\trecord the corrected assertions.\n   864\t\\end{remark}\n   865\t\n   866\tThe measurements of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-12} and Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}\n   867\tconstitute a strategy for the classical low individual degree game in $2m+2$\n   868\tvariables. Applying the quantum soundness of that game yields a single\n   869\tprojective measurement whose outcomes are pairs of global polynomials.\n   870\t\n   871\t\\begin{lemma}[The global polynomial-pair measurement]\n   872\t\t\\label{lem:qld-4-7}\n   873\t\t\\uses{def:polyfunc, def:expanded-state, def:expanded-point-measurement,\n   874\t\t\tdef:consistency, def:bracket}\n   875\t\tThere exists a function\n   876\t\t$\\delta_S(\\eps,m,d,q) = a(md)^a(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$, for some\n   877\t\tuniversal constants $a > 1$ and $0 < b < 1$, such that the following\n   878\t\tholds. For $\\hilb \\in \\{\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}, \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}\\}$ there\n   879\t\texists a projective measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_{g_X,g_Z}\\}$ acting on\n   880\t\t$\\hilb \\ot (\\C^q)^{\\ot n}$, with outcomes consisting of pairs\n   881\t\t$(g_X, g_Z)$ of polynomials, each in $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$, such\n   882\t\tthat for $W \\in \\{X, Z\\}$, on average over uniformly random\n   883\t\t$u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   884\t\t\\begin{align}\n   885\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{S}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot_W) = a]}\n   886\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   887\t\t\t&\\simeq_{\\delta_S}\n   888\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{a}\n   889\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   890\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-s-point-con-alice} \\\\\n   891\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{S}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot_W) = a]}\n   892\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{B}'}\n   893\t\t\t&\\simeq_{\\delta_S}\n   894\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{a}\n   895\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{B}''}\\,,\n   896\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob}\n   897\t\t\\end{align}\n   898\t\twhere $\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot_W)$ denotes the evaluation at the point $u$\n   899\t\tof the first outcome $g_X$ if $W = X$, and of the second outcome $g_Z$\n   900\t\tif $W = Z$.\n   901\t\\end{lemma}\n   902\t\\begin{proof}\n   903\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-13, lem:qld-4-12, thm:naimark, lem:ld-soundness,\n   904\t\t\tdef:ld-game, fact:agreement, fact:add-a-proj2,\n   905\t\t\tfact:data-processing, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree,\n   906\t\t\tdef:combine-map}\n   907\t\tBy Lemmas~\\ref{lem:qld-4-12} and~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}, the expanded state\n   908\t\t$\\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ together with the lines measurements\n   909\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}_{f}\\}$ and the points measurements\n   910\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\}$ constitutes a strategy for the\n   911\t\tclassical low individual degree game (Definition~\\ref{def:ld-game}) with\n   912\t\tparameters $(q, 2m+2, d, 1)$ succeeding with probability\n   913\t\t$1 - \\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}$; the axis-parallel clause of\n   914\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} supplies the degree-$d$ answer format that the\n   915\t\tgame requires on axis-parallel lines.\n   916\t\t% Caveat added locally: the source applies the classical soundness\n   917\t\t% theorem at dimension 2m+2 without the divisibility hypothesis that\n   918\t\t% def:ld-game requires; see rem:qld-4-7-divisibility.\n   919\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:ld-game} is stated only when its dimension\n   920\t\tparameter divides $q$; the divisibility of $q$ by $2m+2$ needed here\n   921\t\tdoes not follow from admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ and in general fails,\n   922\t\tso this instantiation, which follows the source, is not covered by\n   923\t\tTheorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} as stated; see\n   924\t\tRemark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility}.) Since $\\{\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}_{f}\\}$ is\n   925\t\tonly a POVM, it is first made projective by Naimark dilation\n   926\t\t(Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark}), which preserves the $\\simeq$ conclusions of\n   927\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}. The quantum soundness of the classical test\n   928\t\t(Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}, applied with $m$ replaced by $2m+2$ and\n   929\t\t$\\eps$ by $\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}$) then yields a POVM\n   930\t\t$\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$ with outcomes $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,2m+2}(\\F_q)$ that\n   931\t\tis $\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}$-self-consistent and\n   932\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}$-consistent with the points measurements\n   933\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\}$, where\n   934\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}\n   935\t\t= a(md)^a(\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$\n   936\t\tfor universal constants $a, b$; a second application of Naimark dilation\n   937\t\tmakes $\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$ projective with the same guarantees. Let\n   938\t\t$\\mathcal{G}$ be the set of polynomials of the form\n   939\t\t$g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) = \\alpha g_X(x) + \\beta g_Z(z)$ with\n   940\t\t$g_X, g_Z \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$. A chain of approximations\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:267:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_137","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex | sed -n '780,845p'; nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex | sed -n '450,520p;590,780p'\"","aggregated_output":"   780\t\tprojective measurement.\n   781\t\\end{definition}\n   782\t\n   783\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency of the expanded line measurements]\n   784\t\t\\label{lem:qld-comm-line-cons}\n   785\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-line-measurement, def:expanded-state, def:line-point-dist, def:approx-question-indexed-operators, def:povm-distance, def:ideg-deg-polynomials, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   786\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}(\\eps) = \\poly(\\eps)$\n   787\t\tsuch that the following holds, with respect to the state\n   788\t\t$\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-state}. For all\n   789\t\t$W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and lines $\\ell$ there exists a projective measurement\n   790\t\t$\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)}$,\n   791\t\tacting on registers $\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'$ or\n   792\t\t$\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''$, such that:\n   793\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   794\t\t\t\\item \\label{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons}\n   795\t\t\t(\\textbf{Self-consistency}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and\n   796\t\t\t$r \\in \\F_q$ (the relation does not depend on $r$), on average over\n   797\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   798\t\t\tdistribution (Definition~\\ref{def:line-point-dist}),\n   799\t\t\t\\[\n   800\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   801\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   802\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   803\t\t\t\\]\n   804\t\t\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$.\n   805\t\t\t\\item \\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons}\n   806\t\t\t(\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements I}) On average over\n   807\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   808\t\t\tdistribution,\n   809\t\t\t\\[\n   810\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   811\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   812\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   813\t\t\t\t\\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{f(u)})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   814\t\t\t\\]\n   815\t\t\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$.\n   816\t\t\t\\item \\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2}\n   817\t\t\t(\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements II}) On average over\n   818\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   819\t\t\tdistribution,\n   820\t\t\t\\[\n   821\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   822\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   823\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   824\t\t\t\\]\n   825\t\t\twhere\n   826\t\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]} = \\sum_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell) \\colon f(u)=a} \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f$\n   827\t\t\tand the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$.\n   828\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   829\t\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also\n   830\t\thold. The proof exhibits the measurements of\n   831\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:expanded-line-measurement} as witnesses, with\n   832\t\titem~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} holding with error $\\eps$ and\n   833\t\titems~\\ref{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons}\n   834\t\tand~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2} with\n   835\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}(\\eps) = \\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n   836\t\\end{lemma}\n   837\t\\begin{proof}\n   838\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-line-measurement, def:expanded-point-measurement, lem:qld-win-implications, fact:data-processing, fact:add-a-proj2, fact:agreement, def:line-point-dist}\n   839\t\tTake $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}$ to be the measurement of\n   840\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:expanded-line-measurement}.\n   841\t\tItem~\\ref{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons} follows from the fact that the\n   842\t\toriginal line measurements $\\{M^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}$ are\n   843\t\t$\\eps$-self-consistent between registers $\\mathsf{A}$ and $\\mathsf{B}$\n   844\t\tin $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$ (item~\\ref{enu:qld-cons} of\n   845\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}), the Pauli projections\n   450\tand a diagonal component $D_{\\mathrm{DLine}}$.\n   451\t\n   452\t\\begin{definition}[Restricted line distributions]\n   453\t\t\\label{def:ith-restricted-line}\n   454\t\t\\uses{def:line-point-dist, def:ld-question-distribution}\n   455\t\tFor any $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$, the \\emph{$i$-th restricted\n   456\t\taxis-parallel line distribution} $D_{\\mathrm{ALine},i}$ is the\n   457\t\trestriction of $D_{\\mathrm{ALine}}$ to pairs $(\\ell, u)$ where the line\n   458\t\t$\\ell$ is along the direction $e_i$, i.e.\\ is of the form\n   459\t\t$\\ell = (u_0, e_i)$. Similarly, the \\emph{$i$-th restricted diagonal line\n   460\t\tdistribution} $D_{\\mathrm{DLine},i}$ is the restriction of\n   461\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{DLine}}$ to pairs $(\\ell, u)$ where the line\n   462\t\t$\\ell = (u_0, s, v)$ satisfies $\\chi(s) = i$ and coordinates\n   463\t\t$1, \\dots, i-1$ of $v$ are $0$. (The function $\\chi$ is the one used in\n   464\t\tthe question distribution of the low individual degree game,\n   465\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution}.)\n   466\t\\end{definition}\n   467\t\n   468\t\\begin{lemma}[Restricted line distributions and error inflation]\n   469\t\t\\label{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds}\n   470\t\t% This node corresponds to unlabelled prose in the source (the discussion\n   471\t\t% following def:ith-restricted-line, lines 1049-1061 of the paper file).\n   472\t\t\\uses{def:ith-restricted-line, def:line-point-dist, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   473\t\t\tlem:qld-4-10, def:expanded-state}\n   474\t\tThe distribution $D_{\\mathrm{ALine}}$ is a uniform mixture of the\n   475\t\tdistributions $D_{\\mathrm{ALine},i}$ for $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$, and\n   476\t\tlikewise $D_{\\mathrm{DLine}}$ is a uniform mixture of the\n   477\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{DLine},i}$. As a consequence:\n   478\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   479\t\t\t\\item any approximation that holds on average over the line-point\n   480\t\t\tdistribution $D_{\\mathrm{Line}}$ with error $\\delta$ holds over each\n   481\t\t\trestricted distribution $D_{\\mathrm{ALine},i}$ and\n   482\t\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{DLine},i}$ with error $2m\\delta$;\n   483\t\t\t\\item any approximation that holds on average over\n   484\t\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{Line}} \\times D_{\\mathrm{Line}}$ holds over any product\n   485\t\t\tof restricted distributions with error $4m^2\\delta$;\n   486\t\t\t\\item in particular, by Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines}, for any types\n   487\t\t\t$\\mathrm{v}_1, \\mathrm{v}_2 \\in \\{\\mathrm{ALine},\n   488\t\t\t\\mathrm{DLine}\\}$ and any $i, j \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$,\n   489\t\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   490\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}\n   491\t\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell_X,x) \\sim D_{\\mathrm{v}_1,i}}\\\n   492\t\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell_Z,z) \\sim D_{\\mathrm{v}_2,j}}\n   493\t\t\t\t\\sum_{f_X,f_Z}\n   494\t\t\t\t\\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   495\t\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   496\t\t\t\t\\ot\n   497\t\t\t\t\\big(\\Id - \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}\n   498\t\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   499\t\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   500\t\t\t\t= O(m^2 \\delta_P)\\,.\n   501\t\t\t\\end{equation}\n   502\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   503\t\\end{lemma}\n   504\t\\begin{proof}\n   505\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-xz-lines, def:line-point-dist, def:consistency}\n   506\t\tThe line-point distribution places weight $\\tfrac{1}{2}$ on each of the\n   507\t\taxis-parallel and diagonal components and, within each component, uniform\n   508\t\tweight on the index $i$, so each restricted distribution carries mixture\n   509\t\tweight $\\tfrac{1}{2m}$ in $D_{\\mathrm{Line}}$. A nonnegative average that\n   510\t\tis at most $\\delta$ therefore bounds each mixture component by\n   511\t\t$2m\\delta$, and products of two such mixtures give the factor\n   512\t\t$(2m)^2 = 4m^2$. The instance \\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted} is item\n   513\t\t2 applied to the conclusion of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines}, whose\n   514\t\t$\\simeq_{\\delta_P}$ relation over two independent copies of the\n   515\t\tline-point distribution is precisely the statement that the left-hand\n   516\t\tside of \\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}, averaged over\n   517\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{Line}} \\times D_{\\mathrm{Line}}$, is at most $\\delta_P$.\n   518\t\\end{proof}\n   519\t\n   520\t\\begin{lemma}[The sub-line distribution]\n   590\t\tdangling. The intended assertion is the axis-parallel closure property\n   591\t\trecorded as Property~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} above, which is\n   592\t\timmediate from the sampling procedure in the proof.\n   593\t\\end{remark}\n   594\t\n   595\tThe proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} rests on three scalar estimates, stated\n   596\tnext. In all three, $D$ is the distribution of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines},\n   597\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}$ is the measurement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines} placed\n   598\ton registers $\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'$, the second tensor factor is placed on\n   599\tregisters $\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''$, and the sums range over\n   600\t$(f_X, f_Z) \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X) \\times \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$.\n   601\t\n   602\t\\begin{lemma}[Replacing the combined point measurement by the ordered product]\n   603\t\t\\label{lem:claim-17-1}\n   604\t\t% claim:17-1 in the paper; the original label is recorded here as a\n   605\t\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity.\n   606\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10,\n   607\t\t\tdef:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state}\n   608\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   609\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   610\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   611\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   612\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   613\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   614\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\\\\n   615\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q^{1/2}}\\\n   616\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   617\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   618\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   619\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   620\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\,\n   621\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{f_X(x)}\n   622\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   623\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\,.\n   624\t\t\\end{align*}\n   625\t\\end{lemma}\n   626\t\\begin{proof}\n   627\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   628\t\t\tdef:line-point-dist}\n   629\t\tWrite\n   630\t\t$W^{x,z}_{a,b} = \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\n   631\t\t- \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b}\n   632\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}$\n   633\t\tand apply the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality to the difference of the two\n   634\t\tsides. The first factor is at most $1$ because\n   635\t\t$\\sum_{f_X,f_Z \\,:\\, f_X(x)=a,\\ f_Z(z)=b}\n   636\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z} \\le \\Id$\n   637\t\tfor every $x, z, a, b$. For the second factor, note that when $\\ell$ is\n   638\t\tdrawn from the line-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$, a uniformly\n   639\t\trandom point $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$ on $\\ell$ is a uniformly random point\n   640\t\tof $\\F_q^{2m+2}$, so the coordinates $x, z$ are independent and uniform\n   641\t\tin $\\F_q^m$; hence\n   642\t\t$\\E_{x,z} \\sum_{a,b}\n   643\t\t\\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\Id \\ot (W^{x,z}_{a,b})^2 \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   644\t\t\\le \\delta_Q$\n   645\t\tby \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx}. The total error is therefore at most\n   646\t\t$\\sqrt{\\delta_Q}$.\n   647\t\\end{proof}\n   648\t\n   649\t\\begin{lemma}[Removing the $X$-point factor]\n   650\t\t\\label{lem:claim-17-2}\n   651\t\t% claim:17-2 in the paper; the original label is recorded here as a\n   652\t\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity. The source right-hand side\n   653\t\t% carries a typographical superscript \"Z\" in place of the point \"z\";\n   654\t\t% the corrected form is stated here.\n   655\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   656\t\t\tdef:expanded-point-measurement, lem:qld-comm-line-cons,\n   657\t\t\tdef:expanded-state}\n   658\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   659\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   660\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   661\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   662\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   663\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\,\n   664\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{f_X(x)}\n   665\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   666\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\\\\n   667\t\t\t\\approx_{m\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}}\\\n   668\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   669\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   670\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   671\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   672\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}\n   673\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   674\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\,,\n   675\t\t\\end{align*}\n   676\t\twhere $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}$ is the error function of\n   677\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-line-cons}.\n   678\t\\end{lemma}\n   679\t\\begin{proof}\n   680\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-comm-line-cons, lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   681\t\t\tlem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds, def:ith-restricted-line,\n   682\t\t\tdef:expanded-line-measurement}\n   683\t\tBy Cauchy--Schwarz, the difference of the two sides is bounded by the\n   684\t\tsquare root of the deficit\n   685\t\t\\[\n   686\t\t\t1 - \\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   687\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell} \\sum_{a}\n   688\t\t\t\\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   689\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,x}_{a} \\ot\n   690\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\n   691\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\,,\n   692\t\t\t\\qquad\n   693\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,x}_{a}\n   694\t\t\t= \\sum_{f_X,f_Z \\,:\\, f_X(x)=a}\n   695\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\,.\n   696\t\t\\]\n   697\t\tBy the sandwich definition of $T$ and the projectivity of the lines\n   698\t\tmeasurement $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{f_X}\\}$, the\n   699\t\tsubtracted expectation equals\n   700\t\t$\\E \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   701\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a]} \\ot\n   702\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$,\n   703\t\twhich by Property~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} is a mixture of the\n   704\t\tcorresponding averages over the restricted distributions\n   705\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{v},i}$. By the error inflation of\n   706\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds} applied to the\n   707\t\tline--point consistency in item~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-line-cons},\n   708\t\teach restricted term has deficit $O(m\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}})$, so the\n   709\t\ttotal deficit is $O(m^2\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}})$ (the final line of the\n   710\t\tsource proof asserts this bound for the consistency term itself rather\n   711\t\tthan for its deficit; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}).\n   712\t\tTaking the square root gives the claimed error\n   713\t\t$m\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}$.\n   714\t\\end{proof}\n   715\t\n   716\t\\begin{lemma}[The remaining $Z$-point term is close to one]\n   717\t\t\\label{lem:claim-17-3}\n   718\t\t% claim:17-3 in the paper; the original label is recorded here as a\n   719\t\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity.\n   720\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10,\n   721\t\t\tdef:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state}\n   722\t\t\\[\n   723\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   724\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   725\t\t\t\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   726\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   727\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}\n   728\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   729\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   730\t\t\t\\ \\approx_{\\sqrt{m}\\,(\\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4} +\n   731\t\t\t\t\\eps^{1/4})}\\ 1\\,.\n   732\t\t\\]\n   733\t\\end{lemma}\n   734\t\\begin{proof}\n   735\t\t\\uses{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds, lem:qld-sublines,\n   736\t\t\tlem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10, lem:qld-comm-cons,\n   737\t\t\tdef:ith-restricted-line}\n   738\t\tBy Cauchy--Schwarz it suffices to bound the deficit\n   739\t\t$1 - \\E \\sum_b \\bra{\\hat\\psi} T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z}_{b} \\ot\n   740\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$,\n   741\t\twhere\n   742\t\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z}_{b}\n   743\t\t= \\sum_{f_X} T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,[\\mathrm{eval}_z(\\cdot)=b]}$.\n   744\t\tBy Property~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines}, the expectation can be\n   745\t\trewritten over the mixture of product distributions\n   746\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{v},i} \\times D_{\\mathrm{v},j}$ with $z$ uniformly random on\n   747\t\t$\\ell_Z$, and, since\n   748\t\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z}_{b}\n   749\t\t= \\E_{x \\in \\ell_X} \\sum_a\n   750\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}\n   751\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a,\\ \\mathrm{eval}_z(\\cdot)=b]}$,\n   752\t\tone may additionally average over $x$ uniformly random on $\\ell_X$. Up\n   753\t\tto error $m(\\sqrt{\\delta_P} + \\sqrt{\\delta_Q})$ --- obtained by\n   754\t\tapplying the error inflation of\n   755\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds} to the conclusion of\n   756\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines} (transported to the sub-line distribution\n   757\t\tvia Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines}) and to the consistency relations of\n   758\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10} --- the resulting quantity is close to\n   759\t\t$\\E \\sum_{a,b} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   760\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\n   761\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ot\n   762\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$,\n   763\t\twhich, since $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\\}$ is a measurement,\n   764\t\tequals\n   765\t\t$\\sum_b \\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ot\n   766\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   767\t\t\\ge 1 - \\sqrt{\\eps}$\n   768\t\tby the self-consistency of the points measurements\n   769\t\t(Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}). The deficit is therefore\n   770\t\t$O(m(\\sqrt{\\delta_P} + \\sqrt{\\delta_Q}) + \\sqrt{\\eps})$, and taking the\n   771\t\tsquare root gives the claim.\n   772\t\\end{proof}\n   773\t\n   774\t\\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}]\n   775\t\t\\proves{lem:qld-4-13}\n   776\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-sublines, def:combine-map,\n   777\t\t\tlem:qld-4-12, lem:claim-17-1, lem:claim-17-2, lem:claim-17-3}\n   778\t\tLet $D$ be the distribution of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} and let\n   779\t\t$D_{|\\ell}$ denote $D$ conditioned on the first element being $\\ell$.\n   780\t\tFor every line $\\ell$ in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and $f \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:269:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_138","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/14_analysis_of_the_pauli_basis_test.tex | sed -n '960,1045p;1050,1145p;1260,1295p'\"","aggregated_output":"   960\t\twhere $\\delta_P = \\poly(\\eps,md/q)$ is the $\\delta_{pasting}$ as given by Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}, conditions~\\eqref{eq:pasting-1} and~\\eqref{eq:pasting-2} in the lemma are given by~\\eqref{eq:pasting-q1} and~\\eqref{eq:qld-q-self-cons} respectively, \n   961\t\tand the distribution over $(\\ell_\\xpt,\\ell_\\zpt)$ are two independently and uniformly chosen lines. This is a symmetric equivalent of the consistency relation in the conclusion of Lemma. Thus, by applying an identical argument with the appropriate modifications to the registers, all symmetric equivalents of this relation hold, establishing the conclusion of the Lemma.\n   962\t\n   963\t\n   964\t\n   965\t\\end{proof}\n   966\t\n   967\t\n   968\t      \\subsection{Applying the classical low-degree test}\n   969\t      \\label{sec:apply-ldt}\n   970\t      In the previous section we showed how to combine the\n   971\t      approximately commuting $X$ and $Z$ basis point and line\n   972\t      measurements into a joint point measurement and a joint line\n   973\t      measurement. In this section we show how these measurements can be combined\n   974\t\t\t\tin a single measurement\n   975\t      that returns a pair of global polynomials encoding the $X$-basis and\n   976\t      $Z$-basis information, respectively, by applying the lemma that states quantum soundness of the classical low-degree test, Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}.\n   977\t\t\t\t\n   978\t      Introduce a ``combining'' map that\n   979\t      takes two polynomials $f, g \\in \\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ and returns a new polynomial $\\combine_{f,g} \\in \\ideg_{d,\n   980\t        2m+2}(\\F_q)$ defined by\n   981\t      \\[ \\combine_{f,g}(\\underbrace{x}_{\\in \\F_q^{m}},\n   982\t        \\underbrace{z}_{\\in \\F_q^m}, \\underbrace{\\alpha}_{\\in \\F_q},\n   983\t        \\underbrace{\\beta}_{\\in \\F_q}) = \\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(z)\\;. \\]\n   984\t      This combining map can also be defined for polynomials restricted to lines. Let $\\line$ be a line in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ with intercept $u = (u_X, u_Z, u_\\alpha, u_\\beta)$ and slope $v = (v_X, v_Z, v_\\alpha, v_\\beta)$. Let $\\line_X$ and $\\line_Z$ be lines in $\\F_q^{m}$ such that for any point $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\line$, it holds that $x \\in \\line_X$ and $z \\in \\line_Z$. Then for any two polynomials $f \\in \\deg_{dm}(\\line_X)$ and $g \\in \\deg_{dm}(\\line_Z)$, we define $\\combine_{f,g} \\in \\deg_{dm + 1}(\\line)$ by\n   985\t      \\begin{equation}\n   986\t        \\combine_{f,g}(u + t \\cdot v) = (u_\\alpha + t\\cdot v_\\alpha) f(u_X + t \\cdot v_X) + (u_\\beta + t \\cdot v_\\beta) g(u_Z + t \\cdot v_Z)\\;. \\label{eq:combine-lines}\n   987\t      \\end{equation}\n   988\t      \n   989\t\n   990\t      In the following two lemma we define combined point and line\n   991\t      measurements according to this definition of $\\combine_{f,g}$.\n   992\t\n   993\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:qld-4-12}\n   994\t\tFor all $x,z \\in \\F_q^m$ and $\\alpha,\\beta \\in \\F_q$ and $\\mH \\in \\{ \\mH_\\alice, \\mH_\\bob\\}$ there exists a projective measurement $\\{ \\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_c \\}_{c \\in \\F_q}$ acting on $\\mathcal{H} \\otimes (\\C^q)^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$ such that the following holds:\n   995\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   996\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) On average over uniformly random $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\F_q^{2m + 2}$:\n   997\t\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   998\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-12-self-cons}\n   999\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\approx_{\\delta_Q}  (\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\bob \\alice''}\\,.\n  1000\t\t\t\\end{equation}\n  1001\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency with $\\hat{M}$}) On average over uniformly random $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\F_q^{2m + 2}$:\n  1002\t\t\t\\begin{gather}\n  1003\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-12-cons-m-hat}\n  1004\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\approx_{\\delta_Q} \\Big ( \\sum_{\\substack{a,b: \\\\ \\alpha a + \\beta b = c}} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b} \\Big )_{\\bob \\alice''} \\;, \\\\\n  1005\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-12-cons-m-hat2}\n  1006\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\approx_{\\delta_Q}  \\Big ( \\sum_{\\substack{a,b: \\\\ \\alpha a + \\beta b = c}} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b} \\, \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{a}  \\Big )_{\\bob \\alice''}  \\,.\t\t\t\n  1007\t\t\t\\end{gather}\n  1008\t              \\end{enumerate}\n  1009\t                    Furthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations\n  1010\t      also hold.\n  1011\t\\end{lemma}\n  1012\t\\begin{proof}\n  1013\t\tDefine \n  1014\t\t\\[\n  1015\t\t\t\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c} = \\sum_{\\substack{a,b:\\\\ \\alpha a + \\beta b = c}} \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}.\n  1016\t\t\\]\n  1017\t\tThe self-consistency and consistency properties of $\\{ \\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c} \\}$ follow from the consistency properties of $\\{ \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b} \\}$ established by Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}, and Fact~\\ref{fact:data-processing}. \n  1018\t\\end{proof}\n  1019\t\n  1020\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:qld-4-13}\n  1021\tThere exists a function $\\delta_{\\combine}(\\eps,m,d,q) = \\poly(m^2\\eps,md/q)$ such that the following holds. \n  1022\tFor every line $\\line$ in\n  1023\t$\\F_q^{2m + 2}$ and $\\mH \\in \\{ \\mH_\\alice,\\mH_\\bob\\}$ there exists a POVM $\\{ \\hat{Q}^\\line_f \\}$ acting on\n  1024\t$\\mathcal{H} \\otimes (\\C^q)^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$ with outcomes $f\\in\n  1025\t\\deg_{md+1}(\\line)$ such that on average over a uniformly random\n  1026\tpair $(\\line, (x,z,\\alpha, \\beta))$ drawn from the line-point\n  1027\tdistribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$,\n  1028\t\\begin{gather}\n  1029\t\\label{eq:qld-4-13}\n  1030\t(\\hat{Q}^\\line_{[\\eval_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)}(\\cdot) = a]})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\simeq_{\\delta_{\\combine}} (\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a} )_{\\bob \\alice''}\\;, \\\\\n  1031\t(\\hat{Q}^\\line_{[\\eval_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)}(\\cdot) = a]})_{\\bob \\bob'} \\simeq_{\\delta_{\\combine}} ( \\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a} )_{\\alice \\bob''}\\;,\n  1032\t\\end{gather}\n  1033\twhere the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$. Moreover, if $\\line$ is axis-parallel, then the outcome $f \\in \\deg_{d}(\\line)$.\n  1034\t\\end{lemma}\n  1035\t\n  1036\tBefore proving the lemma we give definitions regarding distributions over lines and points.\n  1037\t\n  1038\t\\begin{definition}\\label{def:ith-restricted-line}\n  1039\t  For any $i \\in \\{1, \\dots,\n  1040\t  m\\}$ the \\emph{$i$-th restricted axis parallel line distribution $D_{\\ALine,\n  1041\t    i}$} is the restriction of $D_{\\ALine}$ to pairs  $(\\line, u)$\n  1042\t  where the line $\\line$ is along the direction $e_i$ (i.e. is of the\n  1043\t  form $\\line = (u_0, e_i)$. Similarly, the \\emph{$i$-th restricted\n  1044\t  diagonal line distribution $D_{\\DLine, i}$} is the restriction of\n  1045\t  $D_{\\DLine}$ to pairs $(\\line, u)$, where the line $\\line = (u_0,\n  1050\t  distributions $D_{\\ALine, i}$ for $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$, and\n  1051\t  likewise for $D_{\\DLine}$.\n  1052\t  As a consequence, any approximation that\n  1053\t  holds on average over the line-point distribution $D_{\\Line}$ with\n  1054\t  error $\\delta$ holds over each of the $i$-th restricted distributions\n  1055\t  $D_{\\ALine, i}$ and $D_{\\DLine, i}$ with error $2m\\delta$. Similarly,\n  1056\t  any approximation that holds over $D_{\\Line} \\times D_{\\Line}$ holds\n  1057\t  over any product of the $i$-th restricted distributions with error\n  1058\t  $4m^2 \\delta$. For example, by \\Cref{lem:qld-xz-lines} for any $\\tvar_1, \\tvar_2 \\in \\{\\ALine, \\DLine\\}$ and $i, j \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$, we have\n  1059\t  \\begin{equation}\n  1060\t    \\E_{(\\line_X, x) \\sim D_{\\tvar_1, i}} \\E_{(\\line_Z, z) \\sim D_{\\tvar_2, j}} \\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} (T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z})_{\\alice \\alice'} \\ot (I - (\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x), f_Z(z)}))_{\\bob \\alice''} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}} = O(m^2 \\delta_P)\\;. \\label{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}\n  1061\t    \\end{equation}\n  1062\t\n  1063\t    \\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:qld-sublines}\n  1064\t      There exists a distribution $D$ over tuples $(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z)$ with the following properties:\n  1065\t      \\begin{enumerate}\n  1066\t      \\item The marginal distribution over $\\line$ is the same as the marginal of the line-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$.\n  1067\t\\item For any point of the form $u = (u_X, u_Z, \\alpha, \\beta)$ lying on $\\line$, it holds that $u_X \\in \\line_X$ and $u_Z \\in \\line_Z$. Moreover, for $u$ chosen uniformly at random in $\\line$ the marginal distribution over $(\\line_X,\\line_Z, u_X)$ (resp.\\ over $(\\line_X,\\line_Z, u_Z)$) is such that the marginal over $(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z)$ is a mixture of $D_{\\ALine, i}\\times D_{\\ALine, j}$ and $D_{\\DLine, i}\\times D_{\\DLine, j}$ for $i,j\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$ and moreover, conditioned on $(\\ell_X,\\ell_Z)$, $u_X$ is uniformly random on $\\line_X$ (resp. $u_Z$ uniformly random on $\\line_Z$). \n  1068\t\\end{enumerate}\n  1069\t    \\end{lemma}\n  1070\t\t\t\n  1071\t    \\begin{proof}\n  1072\t      We describe $D$ by giving a procedure to sample from it. We start by sampling $(\\line, u)$ from the line-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$. Write $u = (u_X, u_Z, \\alpha, \\beta)$. \n  1073\t\tThere are two cases to consider: the case where $\\line$ is\n  1074\t        axis-parallel and the case where $\\line$ is diagonal. In each case, we sample lines $\\line_\\xpt$ and $\\line_\\zpt$ such that the tuples  $(\\line_\\xpt,\n  1075\t        \\line_\\zpt,u_\\xpt)$ and $(\\line_\\xpt, \\line_\\zpt,u_\\zpt)$ satisfy Property~2 of the lemma.\n  1076\t        \\begin{enumerate}\n  1077\t          \\item Suppose $\\line$ is an  axis-parallel line $(v,e_j)$ where\n  1078\t            $v = (v_\\xpt,v_\\zpt,\\alpha_0,\\beta_0)$. Then we sample $(v'_\\xpt, i_\\xpt), (v'_\\zpt, i_\\zpt) \\in \\F_q^m \\times \\{1,2, \\ldots, m\\}$ as described below, and set $\\line_\\xpt =\n  1079\t            (v'_\\xpt,e_{i_\\xpt}),\\line_\\zpt = (v'_\\zpt, e_{i_\\zpt})$.\n  1080\t            \\begin{enumerate}\n  1081\t              \\item If $1 \\leq j \\leq m$, then set $i_\\xpt = j$ and\n  1082\t                choose $i_\\zpt$ to be uniformly random in $\\{1, \\dots,\n  1083\t                m\\}$.\n  1084\t              \\item If  $m+1 \\leq j \\leq 2m$, then set $i_\\xpt$ to be\n  1085\t                uniformly random in $\\{1, \\dots, m\\}$ and $i_\\zpt = j - m$.\n  1086\t              \\item If $2m + 1 \\leq j \\leq 2m+2$, then set $i_\\xpt$\n  1087\t                and $i_\\zpt$ to both be uniformly random in $\\{1, \\dots, m\\}$.\n  1088\t              \\item Let $v'_\\xpt$ be the canonical representative\n  1089\t                $L_{e_{i_\\xpt}}^\\lnf(v_\\xpt)$, and\n  1090\t                likewise let $v'_\\zpt$ be the canonical\n  1091\t                representative $L_{e_{i_\\zpt}}^\\lnf(v_{\\zpt})$ (see\n  1092\t                \\Cref{def:line-representative} for the definition of\n  1093\t                the canonical representative of a line). Note that\n  1094\t                $v_\\xpt$ lies on the line $\\line_\\xpt = (v'_\\xpt,\n  1095\t                e_{i_\\xpt})$, and similarly for $v_\\zpt$ and\n  1096\t                $\\line_{\\zpt} = (v'_\\zpt, e_{i_\\zpt})$.\n  1097\t              \\end{enumerate}\n  1098\t              We claim that this construction satisfies Property~2 of the lemma. We show this first $(\\line_X,\\line_Z)$ and $u_X$, the other case being symmetric. If we are in case (a), then $u_X$ is a uniformly random point on the line $\\line_X=(v_X, e_j)$ and $\\line_Z=(v_Z,e_{i_Z})$, which is independent from $\\line_X$. Thus, the marginal distribution over $(\\line_X,u_X)$ conditioned on $j$ in this case is equal to $D_{\\ALine, j}$ and furthermore even conditioned on $(\\line_X,u_X)$, $\\line_Z$ is distributed according to $D_{\\ALine,i_Z}$ for a uniformly random $i_Z$. Cases (b) and (c) are analogous except that in this case even conditioned on $j$, $i_X$ is uniform. \n  1099\t      \\item Suppose $\\ell$ is a random diagonal line $(v, s, w)$,\n  1100\t        where $v = (v_\\xpt, v_\\zpt, \\alpha, \\beta)$, and\n  1101\t        where $w = (w_\\xpt, w_\\zpt, \\gamma, \\delta) $ is a direction vector whose first nonzero coordinate is\n  1102\t         $j = \\chi(s)$ (where $\\chi$ is defined in \\Cref{eq:chi-func}). We sample $\\line_\\xpt =  (v'_\\xpt, s_\\xpt,\n  1103\t        w'_\\xpt)$ and $\\line_\\zpt = (v'_\\zpt, s_\\zpt, w'_\\zpt)$ in the following\n  1104\t        manner:\n  1105\t        \\begin{enumerate}\n  1106\t          \\item If $1 \\leq j \\leq m$, then $w'_\\xpt = w_\\xpt$ and $w'_\\zpt = w_\\zpt$. We choose $s_\\xpt$ and $s_\\zpt$ such that $\\chi(s_\\xpt) = j$ and $\\chi(s_\\zpt) = 1$.\n  1107\t          \\item If $m+1 \\leq j \\leq 2m$, then $w'_\\xpt$ is a random\n  1108\t            diagonal line direction (and $s_{\\xpt}$ is chosen accordingly), and $w'_\\zpt = w_\\zpt$, with $s_\\zpt$ such that $\\chi(s_\\zpt) = j - m$.\n  1109\t          \\item If $2m+1 \\leq j \\leq 2m+2$ then both $w_\\xpt$, and\n  1110\t            $w_\\zpt$ are uniformly random, with $s_\\xpt$ and $s_\\zpt$ chosen accordingly.\n  1111\t          \\item Set $v'_\\xpt = L_{w'_{\\xpt}}^\\lnf(v_\\xpt)$ and $v'_\\zpt = L_{w'_{\\zpt}}^\\lnf(v_\\zpt)$.\n  1112\t          \\end{enumerate}\n  1113\t          We claim that this construction satisfies Property~2 of the Lemma. We show this first $(\\line_X,\\line_Z)$ and $u_X$, the other case being symmetric.  If we are in case (a), then $u_X$ is a uniformly random point on the diagonal line $(v_X, s_X, w_X)$ which is equal to $\\line_X$. Thus, the marginal distribution over $(\\line_X, u_X)$ conditioned on $j$ in this case is equal to $D_{\\DLine, j}$. Furthermore, conditioned on $(\\line_X,u_X)$, $\\line_Z$ is distributed according to $D_{\\DLine,1}$. The other cases can be analyed analogously, with the only difference being how the indices $i,j$ of $\\line_X$ and $\\line_Z$ distributions are chosen. \n  1114\t\t\t\t\t\t    \\end{enumerate}\n  1115\t\tLet $D$ be the distribution over tuples $(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z)$ induced by this procedure. This distribution $D$ satisfies Property 1 in the lemma by  construction, and Property 2 as argued above.\n  1116\t    \\end{proof}\n  1117\t\n  1118\t\\begin{proof}[Proof of \\Cref{lem:qld-4-13}]\n  1119\tWe construct the measurements $\\hat{Q}^\\line_f$\n  1120\tout of the lines measurements $T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}$\n  1121\tguaranteed by \\Cref{lem:qld-xz-lines}. To do so we first show how\n  1122\tto map any line-point pair $(\\line, u)$ in the expanded space\n  1123\t$\\F_q^{2m+2}$ to two pairs  $(\\line_X, u_X)$ and  $(\\line_Z, u_Z)$ in $\\F_q^m$. We will \n  1124\tdefine $\\hat{Q}^\\line$ in terms of $T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}$ and\n  1125\tshow that the resulting measurement is consistent with the combined\n  1126\tpoints measurement $\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha, \\beta}$ from \\Cref{lem:qld-4-12}.\n  1127\t\n  1128\tTo define $\\hat{Q}^\\line$ we make use of the distribution $D$ from \\Cref{lem:qld-sublines}. Let $D_{|\\line}$ denote the distribution derived from $D$ by conditioning on the first element being $\\line$. Then for every line $\\line$ in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and function $f \\in \\deg_{(md+1)}(\\line)$, define\n  1129\t\\[ \\hat{Q}^\\line_f = \\E_{(\\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D_{|\\line}} \\sum_{\\substack{f_X \\in \\deg_{md}(\\line_X), f_Z \\in \\deg_{md}(\\line_Z):\\\\ f = \\combine_{f_X, f_Z}}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z}\\;, \\]\n  1130\twhere $\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}$ is defined in~\\eqref{eq:combine-lines}. Note that $\\{\\hat{Q}^\\line_f\\}$ forms a valid POVM for every choice of line $\\line$, and moreover that $\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}$ is well-defined for the lines $\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z$ because of Property 2 of \\Cref{lem:qld-sublines}. \n  1131\t\n  1132\tObserve that by Property~3 of \\Cref{lem:qld-sublines}, whenever $\\line$ is axis-parallel, $\\line_X$ and $\\line_Z$ are axis-parallel also. Thus, in this case, by \\Cref{lem:qld-xz-lines} it holds that the outcomes $f_X$ and $f_Z$ both have degree $d$. By definition of $\\combine_{f_X, f_Z}$ it holds that the outcome $f$ of $\\{\\hat{Q}^\\line_f\\}$ has degree $d$ as well.\n  1133\t\n  1134\tWe argue the following bound:\n  1135\t\\begin{align}\n  1136\t  \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} &\\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot (I - \\hat{Q}^{x, z }_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\,=\\, O\\big( m \\cdot (\\eps^{1/4} + \\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4} + \\delta_{\\Line}^{1/2})\\big)\\;. \\label{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}\n  1137\t\\end{align}\n  1138\tThis bound will follow from chaining the following three claims. \n  1139\t\n  1140\t\\begin{claim}\\label{claim:17-1}\n  1141\t\\begin{align}\n  1142\t  \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D} \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line} &\\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot \\hat{Q}^{x, z }_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)} \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}  \\notag\\\\\n  1143\t       &\\approx_{\\delta_Q^{1/2}} \\E_{(\\line, \\line_X, \\line_Z) \\sim D}  \\E_{(x,z,\\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\line}  \\sum_{f_X, f_Z} \\bra{\\hat{\\psi}} T^{\\line_X, \\line_Z}_{f_X, f_Z} \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{f_X(x)}  \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\\;. \n  1144\t\\end{align}\n  1145\t\\end{claim}\n  1260\t\n  1261\t\n  1262\t\n  1263\t\t\n  1264\t\\end{proof}\n  1265\t\n  1266\t\n  1267\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:qld-4-7}\n  1268\tThere exists a function $\\delta_S(\\eps,m,d,q) = a(md)^a (\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ for some universal constants $a > 1, 0 < b < 1$ such that the following holds. For $\\mH \\in \\{\\mH_\\alice,\\mH_\\bob\\}$ there exists a projective measurement $\\{ \\hat{S}_{g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt} \\}$ acting on $\\mathcal{H} \\otimes (\\C^q)^{\\otimes \\nqubits}$ with outcomes consisting of pairs $(g_\\xpt,g_\\zpt)$ of polynomials each in $\\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ such that for $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, on average over uniformly random $u \\in \\F_q^m$,\n  1269\t\\begin{align}\n  1270\t  (\\hat{S}_{[\\eval_{u}(\\cdot_{W}) = a]})_{\\alice \\alice'} &\\simeq_{\\delta_S} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\bob \\alice''} \\label{eq:qld-s-point-con-alice} \\\\ \n  1271\t    (\\hat{S}_{[\\eval_{u}(\\cdot_{W}) = a]})_{\\bob \\bob'} &\\simeq_{\\delta_S} (\\hat{M}^{(\\Point, W), u}_{a})_{\\alice \\bob''} \\label{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob},\n  1272\t\\end{align}\n  1273\twhere the notation $\\eval_{u}(\\cdot_W)$ means the evaluation of the first outcome $g_X$ of $\\hat{S}$ if $W = X$, or the second outcome $g_Z$ if $W = Z$, at the point $u$.\n  1274\t\\end{lemma}\n  1275\t\n  1276\t\n  1277\t\\begin{proof}\n  1278\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} establishes the existence of a strategy $\\strategy_\\ld = (\\hat{\\psi}, \\{\n  1279\t        \\hat{Q}^\\line_f \\} \\cup \\{ \\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_a \\} )$\n  1280\t        for the classical low-degree test $\\game^\\ld_\\ldparams$ for\n  1281\t        $\\ldparams = (q,2m + 2,d, 1)$, with value $1 -\n  1282\t        \\delta_{\\combine}$. This strategy is not necessarily projective because the measurement $\\{\\hat{Q}^{\\line}_f\\}$ is not guaranteed to be; however, we may projectivize it by applying Naimark's theorem to this measurement. This preserves the consistency ($\\simeq$) relation obtained as the conclusion of \\Cref{lem:qld-4-13}. Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} applied to this projectivized strategy implies the\n  1283\t        existence of a POVM $\\{ \\hat{S}_g \\}$ with\n  1284\t        outcomes $g \\in \\ideg_{d,2m+2}(\\F_q)$ that is\n  1285\t        $\\delta_\\ld$-self-consistent and consistent with the\n  1286\t        ``points'' measurements $\\{ \\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_a\\}$,\n  1287\t        where $\\delta_\\ld = a(md)^a(\\delta_\\combine^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$ for some universal constants $a > 1, 0 < b < 1$ is given\n  1288\t        by Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. We may apply Naimark's theorem once again to $\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$ to obtain a projective measurement with the same consistency guarantees, and henceforth, we will use $\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$ to refer to this projective measurement.\n  1289\t\t\n  1290\t\tWe now argue that with high probability, the polynomial $g$ returned by the measurement $\\{\\hat{S}_g \\}$ has the form $g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) = \\alpha g_\\xpt(x) + \\beta g_\\zpt (z)$ for polynomials $g_\\xpt, g_\\zpt \\in \\ideg_{d,m}(\\F_q)$. Let $\\mathcal{G}$ denote the set of such polynomials. On average over uniformly random $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$, \n  1291\t\t\\begin{align}\n  1292\t          (\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} &= (\\hat{S}_g)^2_{\\alice \\alice'} \\notag \\\\\n  1293\t                                       &\\approx_{\\delta_\\ld} (\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes (\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)})_{\\bob \\alice''}\\notag\\\\\n  1294\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_Q} (\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} (\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)})_{\\alice \\alice'}\\notag \\\\\n  1295\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_Q} (\\hat{S}_g)_{\\alice \\alice'} \\otimes \\Big ( \\sum_{b,b'} 1_{\\alpha b + \\beta b' = g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)} \\, \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,X),x}_{b} \\hat{M}^{(\\Point,Z),z}_{b'} \\Big)_{\\bob \\alice''} \\label{eq:qld-g-42} \\\\\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:271:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_139","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba references/qpbt-paper/08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex | sed -n '20,45p;220,280p;950,965p'\"","aggregated_output":"    20\tWe sometimes refer to this as the ``classical low-degree test'' for short.\n    21\tThe low-degree test is used as a subroutine in the Pauli Basis test (see\n    22\tSection~\\ref{sec:pauli-verifier}) as well as the answer-reduction normal form\n    23\tverifier (see Section~\\ref{sec:ans}).\n    24\tWe describe the test as a nonlocal game in Section~\\ref{sec:ld-game} and show\n    25\tthat the question distribution can be implemented as a (typed) conditionally\n    26\tlinear distribution (see \\Cref{sec:types} for the definition of typed CL\n    27\tdistributions).\n    28\t\n    29\t\n    30\t\\subsubsection{The game}\n    31\t\\label{sec:ld-game}\n    32\t\n    33\tThe game $\\game^\\ld$ is parametrized by a tuple $\\ldparams = (q, m, d, \\ldc)$\n    34\twhere $m, d, \\ldc \\in \\N$ are integers, $q \\in \\N$ is an admissible field size,\n    35\tand $m$ divides $q$.\n    36\tWe sometimes write $\\game^\\ld_\\ldparams$ to emphasize the dependence of the\n    37\tclassical low-degree test on the parameter tuple $\\ldparams$.\n    38\t\n    39\tWe first provide a high-level description of the game for $k=1$.\n    40\tIt is based on the low-individual degree variant of the multilinearity test\n    41\tof~\\cite{babai1991non}, where one player (called the ``points player'') receives\n    42\ta random point $u \\in \\F_q^m$, and the other player (called the ``lines\n    43\tplayer'') receives a random axis-parallel line $\\line$ that contains $u$, i.e.\\\n    44\tthe line parallel to the $i$-th axis which goes through point $u$, for uniformly\n    45\trandom $i\\in\\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$.\n   220\tfor some $0 \\leq r < q/m$, and we interpret the field element $s \\in \\F_q$ as an\n   221\tinteger between $0$ and $q - 1$).\n   222\tThe function $L_\\ALine$ is the concatenation of the $1$-level CL function that\n   223\tprojects the subspaces $V_{\\coord} \\oplus V_{\\dir{}}$ down to $V_\\coord$ (i.e.\n   224\tzeroes out the entire $V_{\\dir{}}$ register), and the family of $1$-level CL\n   225\tfunctions $\\{L^\\lnf_{e_i} \\}$ acting on $V_\\xpt$, indexed by $i \\in\n   226\t\\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$.\n   227\t(See \\Cref{lem:cl-concat} for definition of CL function concatenation.)\n   228\tThus $L_\\ALine$ is a $2$-level CL function.\n   229\t\n   230\tDefine $L_\\DLine$ to be the following $3$-level CL function: for all $(u,s,v) \\in\n   231\tV$,\n   232\t\\begin{equation}\n   233\t\\label{eq:cl-dlnf}\n   234\t\tL_\\DLine(u,s,v) = (L^\\lnf_{\\pi_{i-1}(v)}(u),s,\\pi_{i-1}(v))\n   235\t\\end{equation}\n   236\twhere $i = \\chi(s)$.\n   237\tThe function $L_\\DLine$ is the concatenation of the following CL functions (again, see \\Cref{lem:cl-concat} for definition of CL function concatenation): (i) the identity function on $V_{\\coord}$ (which is a $1$-level CL function); (ii) for every $i \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$ the $1$-level CL function on $V_{\\dir{}}$ that maps $v \\mapsto \\pi_{i-1}(v)$ (i.e.~it zeroes out the first $i-1$ coordinates of $v$), and (iii) for every pair $(s,v') \\in V_\\coord \\oplus V_{\\dir{}}$, the $1$-level CL function $L^\\lnf_{v'}$ acting on $V_\\xpt$. Thus $L_\\DLine$ is a $3$-level CL function.\n   238\t\n   239\t\n   240\tThe following two lemmas establish that the CL functions\n   241\t$L_\\Point,L_\\ALine,L_\\DLine$ give rise to the axis-parallel line-versus-point and\n   242\tdiagonal line-versus-point distributions.\n   243\t\\begin{lemma}\n   244\t\t\\label{lem:alnf}\n   245\t\tConsider the pair $(\\line, u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the\n   246\t  tuple $((u_0,s,0),(u,0,0))$ from the CL distribution $\\mu_{L_\\ALine,L_\\Point}$,\n   247\t  and let $\\line = \\line(u_0,e_i)$ where $i = \\chi(s)$.\n   248\t  Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\F_q^m$ and $\\line$ is a line that\n   249\t  goes through $u$ and is parallel to a uniformly random axis $i \\in\n   250\t  \\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$.\n   251\t\\end{lemma}\n   252\t\\begin{proof}\n   253\t\tSince $s$ is uniformly random in $\\F_q$ and $m$ divides $q$ by assumption, we\n   254\t  have that $i$ is uniformly random between $\\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$ and therefore\n   255\t  $(u_0,s)$ specifies a uniformly random axis-parallel line $\\line(u_0,e_i)$.\n   256\t  Since $u_0 = L^{\\lnf}_{e_i}(u)$, we have that $u \\in \\line(u_0,e_i)$.\n   257\t\\end{proof}\n   258\t\n   259\t\n   260\t\n   261\t\\begin{lemma}\n   262\t\t\\label{lem:dlnf}\n   263\t\tConsider the pair $(\\line,u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the\n   264\t  tuple $((u_0,s,v),(u,0,0))$ from the CL distribution $\\mu_{L_\\DLine,L_\\Point}$,\n   265\t  and let $\\line = \\line(u_0,v)$ where $i = \\chi(s)$.\n   266\t  Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\F_q^m$ and $\\line$ is a random\n   267\t  diagonal line that goes through $u$ and shares the first $i-1$ coordinates\n   268\t  with $u$, for uniformly random $i$.\n   269\t\\end{lemma}\n   270\t\\begin{proof}\n   271\t\tThe proof of this follows identically to \\Cref{lem:alnf}.\n   272\t\\end{proof}\n   273\t\n   274\tFor later convenience we make the following definition. \n   275\t\n   276\t\\begin{definition}[Line-point distributions]\n   277\t        \\label{def:line-point-dist}\n   278\t The \\emph{axis line-point distribution} $D_{\\ALine}$ is the\n   279\t distribution $\\mu_{L_\\ALine,L_\\Point}$ over pairs $(\\ell=(u_0,e_i),u)$\n   280\t (we omit the $0$ elements for simplicity). The \\emph{diagonal\n   950\tIn the $\\gamma = 1$ case, on the other hand, the measurements anticommute, which implies that they cannot simultaneously be measured.\n   951\tIn spite of this, the verifier can still check that the measurements anticommute by using the Magic Square game from Section~\\ref{sec:ms} above.\n   952\tAs was shown in Theorem~\\ref{thm:ms-from-ac}, any pair of anticommuting measurements\n   953\tcan be used to form a perfect strategy for the Magic Square game\n   954\t(and indeed the reverse is true as well: any perfect strategy for the Magic Square game entails a pair of anticommuting measurements).\n   955\t\n   956\tTogether, these give the main subtests for the game $\\game^\\pauli$.\n   957\t\n   958\t\\begin{definition}[Admissible parameters]\\label{def:admissible}\n   959\t  We say that the tuple $\\qldparams=(q, m, d)$ is \\emph{admissible} if $q$ is\n   960\t  an admissible field size (Definition~\\ref{def:admissible-size}) and $m | q$.\n   961\t\\end{definition}\n   962\t\n   963\t\n   964\t\\paragraph{Question distribution.}\n   965\tWe now describe the question distribution of the game $\\game^\\pauli$, and show\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:273:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_140","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex | sed -n '115,330p;350,455p'\"","aggregated_output":"   115\t\tNatarajan and Vidick~\\cite{Natarajan2017Linearity}, used here as an\n   116\t\texternal result. Two features of the statement are load-bearing for the\n   117\t\tproof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}: the error is preserved exactly\n   118\t\t($\\delta$ in the hypothesis equals $\\delta$ in the conclusion), and the\n   119\t\tlinearity of the $\\{\\mathcal{L}^u\\}$ is exact and holds for all pairs\n   120\t\t$(u,u')$, not merely on average.\n   121\t\\end{remark}\n   122\t\n   123\t\\begin{lemma}[Combined point measurements]\n   124\t\t\\label{lem:qld-4-10}\n   125\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-state, def:expanded-point-measurement,\n   126\t\t\tdef:povm-distance, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   127\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_Q(\\eps) = \\poly(\\eps)$ such that the\n   128\t\tfollowing holds. For every $x, z \\in \\F_q^m$ and\n   129\t\t$\\hilb \\in \\{\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}, \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}\\}$ there are\n   130\t\tprojective measurements $\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\\}_{a,b \\in \\F_q}$ acting\n   131\t\ton $\\hilb \\ot (\\C^q)^{\\ot n}$ such that:\n   132\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   133\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) On average over uniformly random\n   134\t\t\t$x, z \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   135\t\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   136\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-q-self-cons}\n   137\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   138\t\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   139\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,.\n   140\t\t\t\\end{equation}\n   141\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency with $\\hat{M}$}) On average over uniformly\n   142\t\t\trandom $x, z \\in \\F_q^m$,\n   143\t\t\t\\begin{gather}\n   144\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-xz}\n   145\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   146\t\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   147\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a} \\,\n   148\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   149\t\t\t\t\\\\\n   150\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx}\n   151\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   152\t\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   153\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\,\n   154\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,.\n   155\t\t\t\\end{gather}\n   156\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   157\t\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also hold.\n   158\t\tThe operators $\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}$ and\n   159\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b}$ do not commute in general, so the\n   160\t\tproducts in \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-xz} and\n   161\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx} differ and the two relations are separate\n   162\t\tconclusions.\n   163\t\\end{lemma}\n   164\t\\begin{proof}\n   165\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-comm-cons, def:expanded-point-trace-projection,\n   166\t\t\tfact:add-a-proj, fact:agreement, lem:ortho, thm:linearity,\n   167\t\t\tlem:avg-closeness, lem:cancellation}\n   168\t\tThroughout the proof every intermediate error is a $\\poly(\\eps)$ function\n   169\t\tthat may differ from one use of $\\approx_\\delta$ to the next. For\n   170\t\t$\\omega = (x,z,r,s) \\in \\F_q^m \\times \\F_q^m \\times \\F_q \\times \\F_q$\n   171\t\tand $a, b \\in \\F_2$ define the sandwich POVM\n   172\t\t$\\hat{R}^{\\omega}_{a,b}\n   173\t\t= \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z,s}_{b}\n   174\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x,r}_{a}\n   175\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z,s}_{b}$\n   176\t\tfrom the binary refinements of\n   177\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-trace-projection}; by the approximate\n   178\t\tcommutation of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}, projectivity, and\n   179\t\tLemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj}, on average over $\\omega$ one has\n   180\t\t$\\hat{R}^{\\omega}_{a,b}\n   181\t\t\\approx_{\\sqrt{\\delta_{\\mathrm{acom}}}}\n   182\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z,s}_{b}\n   183\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x,r}_{a}$,\n   184\t\tand a Cauchy--Schwarz chain with successive errors\n   185\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{acom}}^{1/4}$, $\\delta_{\\mathrm{acom}}^{1/4}$,\n   186\t\t$\\sqrt{\\eps}$, $\\eps$ establishes the self-consistency\n   187\t\t$(\\hat{R}^{\\omega}_{a,b})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\simeq_\\delta\n   188\t\t(\\hat{R}^{\\omega}_{a,b})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$, hence the\n   189\t\tcorresponding $\\approx_\\delta$ relation by Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement}.\n   190\t\tThe orthonormalization lemma (Lemma~\\ref{lem:ortho}) then produces\n   191\t\tprojective measurements $\\{\\hat{L}^{\\omega}_{a,b}\\}$ with\n   192\t\t$\\hat{L}^{\\omega}_{a,b} \\approx_\\delta \\hat{R}^{\\omega}_{a,b}$ on\n   193\t\taverage over $\\omega$. Next, the refined point measurements satisfy the\n   194\t\texact linearity\n   195\t\t$\\sum_{b+b'=c} \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u,r}_{b}\n   196\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u,r'}_{b'}\n   197\t\t= \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u,r+r'}_{c}$\n   198\t\t(a direct consequence of projectivity of\n   199\t\t$\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a\\}$), which transfers, on average over\n   200\t\t$x, z, r, r', s, s'$, to an approximate linearity of the\n   201\t\t$\\{\\hat{R}^{\\omega}_{a,b}\\}$ in the index pair $(r,s)$ and then to the\n   202\t\t$\\{\\hat{L}^{\\omega}_{a,b}\\}$. Consequently the observables\n   203\t\t$\\mathcal{O}^{\\omega}\n   204\t\t= \\sum_{a,b \\in \\F_2} (-1)^{a+b} \\hat{L}^{\\omega}_{a,b}$\n   205\t\tsatisfy\n   206\t\t$\\mathcal{O}^{x,z,r,s} \\mathcal{O}^{x,z,r',s'}\n   207\t\t\\approx_\\delta \\mathcal{O}^{x,z,r+r',s+s'}$\n   208\t\ton average; identifying $\\F_q \\cong \\F_2^t$ so that the pair $(r,s)$\n   209\t\tranges over $\\F_2^{2t}$, Theorem~\\ref{thm:linearity} applied for each\n   210\t\tfixed $(x,z)$ yields exactly linear observables\n   211\t\t$\\{\\mathcal{L}^{x,z,r,s}\\}$ that are $\\delta$-close to the\n   212\t\t$\\{\\mathcal{O}^{x,z,r,s}\\}$ on average over $x, z, r, s$; no further\n   213\t\textension of the state is needed because $\\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ is padded with\n   214\t\tsufficiently many ancilla qubits in the zero state. Define\n   215\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\n   216\t\t= \\E_{r,s} (-1)^{\\tr(ra+sb)} \\mathcal{L}^{x,z,r,s}$\n   217\t\tfor $a, b \\in \\F_q$: exact linearity makes each $\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$ a\n   218\t\tprojection, and the family sums to the identity because\n   219\t\t$\\mathcal{L}^{x,z,0,0} = \\Id$, so $\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\\}$ is a\n   220\t\tprojective measurement recovering $\\F_q$-valued outcomes from the binary\n   221\t\tdata by Fourier averaging over $(r,s)$. Its self-consistency\n   222\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-q-self-cons} follows from Lemma~\\ref{lem:avg-closeness}\n   223\t\ttogether with the self-consistency of the $\\{\\mathcal{O}^{\\omega}\\}$;\n   224\t\tthe consistency \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx} follows from a Fourier\n   225\t\tcomputation that replaces $\\mathcal{L}$ by $\\mathcal{O}$, then by\n   226\t\t$\\hat{R}$, then by the ordered product of refined point measurements, and\n   227\t\tfinally resolves the averages over $r$ and $s$ using\n   228\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:cancellation}; and \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-xz} follows\n   229\t\tby inserting one application of the approximate commutation of\n   230\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons} into the same chain. Repeating the argument\n   231\t\twith the tensor factors on registers $\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{B}'$ and\n   232\t\t$\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{B}''$ gives all symmetric equivalents.\n   233\t\\end{proof}\n   234\t\n   235\t\\section{Combined line measurements}\n   236\t\n   237\t\\begin{lemma}[Combined line measurements]\n   238\t\t\\label{lem:qld-xz-lines}\n   239\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, def:line, def:line-point-dist, def:consistency,\n   240\t\t\tdef:bracket, def:expanded-state, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   241\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_P(\\eps,m,d,q) = \\poly(\\eps, md/q)$ such\n   242\t\tthat the following holds. For\n   243\t\t$\\hilb \\in \\{\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}, \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}\\}$ and every pair of\n   244\t\tlines $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$ in $\\F_q^m$ there exists a POVM\n   245\t\t$\\{T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\}$ acting on $\\hilb \\ot (\\C^q)^{\\ot n}$,\n   246\t\twith answers consisting of pairs of polynomials\n   247\t\t$(f_X, f_Z) \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X) \\times \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$, such that on\n   248\t\taverage over pairs $(\\ell_X, x)$ and $(\\ell_Z, z)$ independently sampled\n   249\t\tfrom the line-point distribution\n   250\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:line-point-dist}),\n   251\t\t\\[\n   252\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}\n   253\t\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a,\\ \\mathrm{eval}_z(\\cdot)=b]}\n   254\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   255\t\t\t\\simeq_{\\delta_P}\n   256\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   257\t\t\\]\n   258\t\tas well as all symmetric equivalents of this approximation. Moreover, if\n   259\t\t$\\ell_W$ is axis-parallel, then $f_W \\in \\deg_{d}(\\ell_W)$.\n   260\t\\end{lemma}\n   261\t\\begin{proof}\n   262\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-line-measurement, lem:qld-4-10, fact:add-a-proj,\n   263\t\t\tlem:cool-closeness-fact, lem:qld-comm-line-cons, fact:agreement,\n   264\t\t\tfact:data-processing, lem:pasting, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree}\n   265\t\tDefine, for all lines $\\ell_X, \\ell_Z$ and\n   266\t\t$(f_X, f_Z) \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X) \\times \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$, the sandwich\n   267\t\toperator\n   268\t\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\n   269\t\t= \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{f_X}\n   270\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},Z),\\ell_Z}_{f_Z}\n   271\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{f_X}$,\n   272\t\twhich forms a valid POVM; since the lines measurements come from a valid\n   273\t\tstrategy for the low-degree test, on an axis-parallel line $\\ell_W$ the\n   274\t\toutcome $f_W$ lies in $\\deg_d(\\ell_W)$, and this property is inherited by\n   275\t\t$T$. As intermediate consistency relations, write\n   276\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,\\bullet} = \\sum_b \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$ and\n   277\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{\\bullet,b} = \\sum_a \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$ for the two\n   278\t\tmarginals of the combined point measurement of\n   279\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}. Applying\n   280\t\tLemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj} to \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx} and then\n   281\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:cool-closeness-fact} gives, on average over uniform\n   282\t\t$x, z$,\n   283\t\t$(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,\\bullet})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   284\t\t\\approx_\\delta\n   285\t\t\\sum_b (\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   286\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$,\n   287\t\tand a direct norm computation from this display and\n   288\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx} yields the marginal consistencies\n   289\t\t$(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,\\bullet})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   290\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$ and\n   291\t\t$(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{\\bullet,b})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   292\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$.\n   293\t\tFrom items 1 and 3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-line-cons}, converting\n   294\t\tbetween $\\approx$ and $\\simeq$ with Lemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} and\n   295\t\tsumming outcomes with Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}, the lines\n   296\t\tmeasurements are consistent with the points measurements, and combining\n   297\t\twith the marginal consistencies gives, on average over the line-point\n   298\t\tdistribution,\n   299\t\t$(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,\\bullet})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\simeq_{\\delta_Q}\n   300\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}\n   301\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a]})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$\n   302\t\ttogether with the analogous relation for $Z$. The conclusion now follows\n   303\t\tfrom the pasting lemma (Lemma~\\ref{lem:pasting}) applied with\n   304\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$ in the role of the combined measurement,\n   305\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}$ and\n   306\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},Z),\\ell_Z}$ in the roles of the two factor\n   307\t\tmeasurements, $T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}$ in the role of the pasted measurement,\n   308\t\t$\\delta := \\delta_Q$, and $\\eta := md/q$, the last justified by the\n   309\t\tSchwartz--Zippel bound (Lemma~\\ref{lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree})\n   310\t\tapplied to the polynomials $f_X, f_Z$ of total degree at most $md$; the\n   311\t\ttwo hypotheses of the pasting lemma are supplied by the preceding display\n   312\t\tand by \\eqref{eq:qld-q-self-cons}, and the distribution over\n   313\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$ is that of two independent uniformly random lines.\n   314\t\tThis gives the stated relation with $\\delta_P$ equal to the pasting\n   315\t\terror, a $\\poly(\\eps, md/q)$ function, and the identical argument with\n   316\t\tthe registers modified gives all symmetric equivalents.\n   317\t\\end{proof}\n   318\t\n   319\t\\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}\n   320\t\\label{sec:apply-ldt}\n   321\t\n   322\tThe combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next\n   323\tmerged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that\n   324\tencode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is\n   325\tdone by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking\n   326\tthe quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test\n   327\t(Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}).\n   328\t\n   329\t\\begin{definition}[The combining map]\n   330\t\t\\label{def:combine-map}\n   350\t\t\t\\label{eq:combine-lines}\n   351\t\t\t\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g}(u + t \\cdot v)\n   352\t\t\t= (u_\\alpha + t \\cdot v_\\alpha)\\, f(u_X + t \\cdot v_X)\n   353\t\t\t+ (u_\\beta + t \\cdot v_\\beta)\\, g(u_Z + t \\cdot v_Z)\\,,\n   354\t\t\t\\qquad t \\in \\F_q\\,.\n   355\t\t\\end{equation}\n   356\t\tThe degree bound holds because $f$ and $g$, restricted to lines, are\n   357\t\tpolynomials of degree at most $md$ in the line parameter, and each is\n   358\t\tmultiplied by an affine factor of degree at most one.\n   359\t\\end{definition}\n   360\t\n   361\t\\begin{lemma}[Combined point measurement on the extended coordinates]\n   362\t\t\\label{lem:qld-4-12}\n   363\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, def:expanded-state, def:expanded-point-measurement,\n   364\t\t\tdef:povm-distance, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   365\t\tFor all $x, z \\in \\F_q^m$, $\\alpha, \\beta \\in \\F_q$, and\n   366\t\t$\\hilb \\in \\{\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}, \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}\\}$ there exists a\n   367\t\tprojective measurement\n   368\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c}\\}_{c \\in \\F_q}$ acting on\n   369\t\t$\\hilb \\ot (\\C^q)^{\\ot n}$ such that the following holds, where\n   370\t\t$\\delta_Q$ is the function of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}:\n   371\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   372\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Self-consistency}) On average over uniformly random\n   373\t\t\t$(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\F_q^{2m+2}$,\n   374\t\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   375\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-12-self-cons}\n   376\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   377\t\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   378\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,.\n   379\t\t\t\\end{equation}\n   380\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency with $\\hat{M}$}) On average over uniformly\n   381\t\t\trandom $(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\F_q^{2m+2}$,\n   382\t\t\t\\begin{gather}\n   383\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-12-cons-m-hat}\n   384\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   385\t\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   386\t\t\t\t\\Big( \\sum_{\\substack{a,b:\\\\ \\alpha a + \\beta b = c}}\n   387\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a} \\,\n   388\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b}\n   389\t\t\t\t\\Big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,, \\\\\n   390\t\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-12-cons-m-hat2}\n   391\t\t\t\t(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   392\t\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q}\n   393\t\t\t\t\\Big( \\sum_{\\substack{a,b:\\\\ \\alpha a + \\beta b = c}}\n   394\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\,\n   395\t\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\n   396\t\t\t\t\\Big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,.\n   397\t\t\t\\end{gather}\n   398\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   399\t\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also hold.\n   400\t\\end{lemma}\n   401\t\\begin{proof}\n   402\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, fact:data-processing}\n   403\t\tDefine\n   404\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{c}\n   405\t\t= \\sum_{a,b \\,:\\, \\alpha a + \\beta b = c} \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$,\n   406\t\ta coarse-graining of the projective measurement of\n   407\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}, hence itself projective. A uniformly random\n   408\t\tpoint of $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ has uniformly random coordinates $(x,z)$, so the\n   409\t\tself-consistency and consistency properties follow from those of\n   410\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\\}$ established in Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}\n   411\t\ttogether with Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}, which shows that\n   412\t\tcoarse-graining the outcomes preserves the $\\delta_Q$ bounds.\n   413\t\\end{proof}\n   414\t\n   415\t\\begin{lemma}[Combined line measurement on the extended coordinates]\n   416\t\t\\label{lem:qld-4-13}\n   417\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-12, def:line, def:line-point-dist, def:consistency,\n   418\t\t\tdef:bracket, def:expanded-state}\n   419\t\tThere exists a function\n   420\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = \\poly(m^2\\eps, md/q)$ such that\n   421\t\tthe following holds. For every line $\\ell$ in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and\n   422\t\t$\\hilb \\in \\{\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}, \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}\\}$ there exists a\n   423\t\tPOVM $\\{\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}_{f}\\}$ acting on $\\hilb \\ot (\\C^q)^{\\ot n}$ with\n   424\t\toutcomes $f \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$ such that on average over a uniformly\n   425\t\trandom pair $(\\ell, (x,z,\\alpha,\\beta))$ drawn from the line-point\n   426\t\tdistribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$,\n   427\t\t\\begin{gather}\n   428\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-13}\n   429\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}\n   430\t\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)}(\\cdot)=a]}\n   431\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   432\t\t\t\\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}}\n   433\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   434\t\t\t\\\\\n   435\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}\n   436\t\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)}(\\cdot)=a]}\n   437\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{B}'}\n   438\t\t\t\\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}}\n   439\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{B}''}\\,,\n   440\t\t\t\\notag\n   441\t\t\\end{gather}\n   442\t\twhere the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$. Moreover, if $\\ell$ is\n   443\t\taxis-parallel, then the outcome $f \\in \\deg_{d}(\\ell)$.\n   444\t\\end{lemma}\n   445\t\n   446\tBefore proving the lemma we record definitions and estimates concerning\n   447\tdistributions over lines and points. Recall from\n   448\tDefinition~\\ref{def:line-point-dist} that the line-point distribution over\n   449\t$\\F_q^m$ is an even mixture of an axis-parallel component $D_{\\mathrm{ALine}}$\n   450\tand a diagonal component $D_{\\mathrm{DLine}}$.\n   451\t\n   452\t\\begin{definition}[Restricted line distributions]\n   453\t\t\\label{def:ith-restricted-line}\n   454\t\t\\uses{def:line-point-dist, def:ld-question-distribution}\n   455\t\tFor any $i \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$, the \\emph{$i$-th restricted\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:275:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_141","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex | sed -n '585,780p'\"","aggregated_output":"   585\t\\begin{remark}[Property~3 of the sub-line distribution]\n   586\t\t\\label{rem:qld-sublines-property-three}\n   587\t\tThe source proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} in \\cite{Ji2020MIPStar}\n   588\t\tappeals to a ``Property~3'' of the sub-line lemma, while the statement\n   589\t\tgiven there lists only two numbered properties, so the reference is\n   590\t\tdangling. The intended assertion is the axis-parallel closure property\n   591\t\trecorded as Property~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} above, which is\n   592\t\timmediate from the sampling procedure in the proof.\n   593\t\\end{remark}\n   594\t\n   595\tThe proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} rests on three scalar estimates, stated\n   596\tnext. In all three, $D$ is the distribution of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines},\n   597\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}$ is the measurement of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines} placed\n   598\ton registers $\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'$, the second tensor factor is placed on\n   599\tregisters $\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''$, and the sums range over\n   600\t$(f_X, f_Z) \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X) \\times \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$.\n   601\t\n   602\t\\begin{lemma}[Replacing the combined point measurement by the ordered product]\n   603\t\t\\label{lem:claim-17-1}\n   604\t\t% claim:17-1 in the paper; the original label is recorded here as a\n   605\t\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity.\n   606\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10,\n   607\t\t\tdef:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state}\n   608\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   609\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   610\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   611\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   612\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   613\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   614\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\\\\n   615\t\t\t\\approx_{\\delta_Q^{1/2}}\\\n   616\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   617\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   618\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   619\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   620\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\,\n   621\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{f_X(x)}\n   622\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   623\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\,.\n   624\t\t\\end{align*}\n   625\t\\end{lemma}\n   626\t\\begin{proof}\n   627\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-10, lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   628\t\t\tdef:line-point-dist}\n   629\t\tWrite\n   630\t\t$W^{x,z}_{a,b} = \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\n   631\t\t- \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b}\n   632\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}$\n   633\t\tand apply the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality to the difference of the two\n   634\t\tsides. The first factor is at most $1$ because\n   635\t\t$\\sum_{f_X,f_Z \\,:\\, f_X(x)=a,\\ f_Z(z)=b}\n   636\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z} \\le \\Id$\n   637\t\tfor every $x, z, a, b$. For the second factor, note that when $\\ell$ is\n   638\t\tdrawn from the line-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$, a uniformly\n   639\t\trandom point $(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)$ on $\\ell$ is a uniformly random point\n   640\t\tof $\\F_q^{2m+2}$, so the coordinates $x, z$ are independent and uniform\n   641\t\tin $\\F_q^m$; hence\n   642\t\t$\\E_{x,z} \\sum_{a,b}\n   643\t\t\\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\Id \\ot (W^{x,z}_{a,b})^2 \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   644\t\t\\le \\delta_Q$\n   645\t\tby \\eqref{eq:qld-q-cons-m-hat-zx}. The total error is therefore at most\n   646\t\t$\\sqrt{\\delta_Q}$.\n   647\t\\end{proof}\n   648\t\n   649\t\\begin{lemma}[Removing the $X$-point factor]\n   650\t\t\\label{lem:claim-17-2}\n   651\t\t% claim:17-2 in the paper; the original label is recorded here as a\n   652\t\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity. The source right-hand side\n   653\t\t% carries a typographical superscript \"Z\" in place of the point \"z\";\n   654\t\t% the corrected form is stated here.\n   655\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   656\t\t\tdef:expanded-point-measurement, lem:qld-comm-line-cons,\n   657\t\t\tdef:expanded-state}\n   658\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   659\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   660\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   661\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   662\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   663\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)} \\,\n   664\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{f_X(x)}\n   665\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   666\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi} \\\\\n   667\t\t\t\\approx_{m\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}}\\\n   668\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   669\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   670\t\t\t&\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   671\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   672\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}\n   673\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   674\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\,,\n   675\t\t\\end{align*}\n   676\t\twhere $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}$ is the error function of\n   677\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-line-cons}.\n   678\t\\end{lemma}\n   679\t\\begin{proof}\n   680\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-comm-line-cons, lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines,\n   681\t\t\tlem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds, def:ith-restricted-line,\n   682\t\t\tdef:expanded-line-measurement}\n   683\t\tBy Cauchy--Schwarz, the difference of the two sides is bounded by the\n   684\t\tsquare root of the deficit\n   685\t\t\\[\n   686\t\t\t1 - \\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   687\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell} \\sum_{a}\n   688\t\t\t\\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   689\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,x}_{a} \\ot\n   690\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\n   691\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\\,,\n   692\t\t\t\\qquad\n   693\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,x}_{a}\n   694\t\t\t= \\sum_{f_X,f_Z \\,:\\, f_X(x)=a}\n   695\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\,.\n   696\t\t\\]\n   697\t\tBy the sandwich definition of $T$ and the projectivity of the lines\n   698\t\tmeasurement $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{f_X}\\}$, the\n   699\t\tsubtracted expectation equals\n   700\t\t$\\E \\sum_a \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   701\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},X),\\ell_X}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a]} \\ot\n   702\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$,\n   703\t\twhich by Property~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} is a mixture of the\n   704\t\tcorresponding averages over the restricted distributions\n   705\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{v},i}$. By the error inflation of\n   706\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds} applied to the\n   707\t\tline--point consistency in item~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-line-cons},\n   708\t\teach restricted term has deficit $O(m\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}})$, so the\n   709\t\ttotal deficit is $O(m^2\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}})$ (the final line of the\n   710\t\tsource proof asserts this bound for the consistency term itself rather\n   711\t\tthan for its deficit; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}).\n   712\t\tTaking the square root gives the claimed error\n   713\t\t$m\\,\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2}$.\n   714\t\\end{proof}\n   715\t\n   716\t\\begin{lemma}[The remaining $Z$-point term is close to one]\n   717\t\t\\label{lem:claim-17-3}\n   718\t\t% claim:17-3 in the paper; the original label is recorded here as a\n   719\t\t% comment for cross-reference fidelity.\n   720\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-sublines, lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10,\n   721\t\t\tdef:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-state}\n   722\t\t\\[\n   723\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   724\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   725\t\t\t\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   726\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   727\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{f_Z(z)}\n   728\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   729\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   730\t\t\t\\ \\approx_{\\sqrt{m}\\,(\\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4} +\n   731\t\t\t\t\\eps^{1/4})}\\ 1\\,.\n   732\t\t\\]\n   733\t\\end{lemma}\n   734\t\\begin{proof}\n   735\t\t\\uses{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds, lem:qld-sublines,\n   736\t\t\tlem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-4-10, lem:qld-comm-cons,\n   737\t\t\tdef:ith-restricted-line}\n   738\t\tBy Cauchy--Schwarz it suffices to bound the deficit\n   739\t\t$1 - \\E \\sum_b \\bra{\\hat\\psi} T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z}_{b} \\ot\n   740\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$,\n   741\t\twhere\n   742\t\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z}_{b}\n   743\t\t= \\sum_{f_X} T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,[\\mathrm{eval}_z(\\cdot)=b]}$.\n   744\t\tBy Property~2 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines}, the expectation can be\n   745\t\trewritten over the mixture of product distributions\n   746\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{v},i} \\times D_{\\mathrm{v},j}$ with $z$ uniformly random on\n   747\t\t$\\ell_Z$, and, since\n   748\t\t$T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z,z}_{b}\n   749\t\t= \\E_{x \\in \\ell_X} \\sum_a\n   750\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}\n   751\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_x(\\cdot)=a,\\ \\mathrm{eval}_z(\\cdot)=b]}$,\n   752\t\tone may additionally average over $x$ uniformly random on $\\ell_X$. Up\n   753\t\tto error $m(\\sqrt{\\delta_P} + \\sqrt{\\delta_Q})$ --- obtained by\n   754\t\tapplying the error inflation of\n   755\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:restricted-line-mixture-bounds} to the conclusion of\n   756\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines} (transported to the sub-line distribution\n   757\t\tvia Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines}) and to the consistency relations of\n   758\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10} --- the resulting quantity is close to\n   759\t\t$\\E \\sum_{a,b} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   760\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\n   761\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ot\n   762\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}$,\n   763\t\twhich, since $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},X),x}_{a}\\}$ is a measurement,\n   764\t\tequals\n   765\t\t$\\sum_b \\bra{\\hat\\psi} \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ot\n   766\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},Z),z}_{b} \\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   767\t\t\\ge 1 - \\sqrt{\\eps}$\n   768\t\tby the self-consistency of the points measurements\n   769\t\t(Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-comm-cons}). The deficit is therefore\n   770\t\t$O(m(\\sqrt{\\delta_P} + \\sqrt{\\delta_Q}) + \\sqrt{\\eps})$, and taking the\n   771\t\tsquare root gives the claim.\n   772\t\\end{proof}\n   773\t\n   774\t\\begin{proof}[Proof of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}]\n   775\t\t\\proves{lem:qld-4-13}\n   776\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-xz-lines, lem:qld-sublines, def:combine-map,\n   777\t\t\tlem:qld-4-12, lem:claim-17-1, lem:claim-17-2, lem:claim-17-3}\n   778\t\tLet $D$ be the distribution of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} and let\n   779\t\t$D_{|\\ell}$ denote $D$ conditioned on the first element being $\\ell$.\n   780\t\tFor every line $\\ell$ in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and $f \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
