/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:305:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_156","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch12_qpbt_games.tex | sed -n '185,275p;330,375p;505,545p'\"","aggregated_output":"   185\t\t\t\\E_{x \\sim \\mu} \\sum_{a \\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^x_b \\ket{\\psi} = O(\\delta)\\,.\n   186\t\t\\]\n   187\t\tIn this case we say that $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ are \\emph{$\\delta$-consistent} on $\\ket{\\psi}$. As in Definition~\\ref{def:state-distance}, the $O(\\cdot)$ refers to families of POVMs and states indexed by an implicit parameter $n \\in \\N$, and is taken as $n \\to \\infty$.\n   188\t\\end{definition}\n   189\t\n   190\tA measurement that is consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$ in the sense of Definition~\\ref{def:consistent-measurement} is $0$-consistent with itself on the same state under the singleton distribution, and vice versa: if $\\sum_a \\bra{\\psi} M_a \\ot M_a \\ket{\\psi} = 1$, then $M_a \\ot \\Id\\, \\ket{\\psi} = \\Id \\ot M_a\\, \\ket{\\psi}$ for every $a$, by the equality case of the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality applied outcome by outcome.\n   191\t\n   192\t\\begin{definition}[Distance between POVMs]\\label{def:povm-distance}\n   193\t\t\\uses{def:povm-conventions, def:state-distance}\n   194\t\tLet $\\mathcal X$ be a finite set and $\\mu$ a distribution on $\\mathcal X$. Let $\\ket{\\psi} \\in \\mathcal H$ be a quantum state, and for all $x \\in \\mathcal X$ let $\\{M^x_a\\}$ and $\\{N^x_a\\}$ be two POVMs on $\\mathcal H$. We say that \\emph{$\\{M^x_a\\}$ and $\\{N^x_a\\}$ are $\\delta$-close on state $\\ket{\\psi}$ and under distribution $\\mu$} if\n   195\t\t\\[\n   196\t\t\t\\E_{x \\sim \\mu} \\sum_a \\lVert (M^x_a - N^x_a)\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 \\le O(\\delta)\\,,\n   197\t\t\\]\n   198\t\tand we write $M^x_a \\approx_\\delta N^x_a$ to denote this when the state $\\ket{\\psi}$ and the distribution $\\mu$ are clear from context. This distance is referred to as the \\emph{state-dependent} distance.\n   199\t\\end{definition}\n   200\t\n   201\t\\begin{definition}[Distance between strategies]\\label{def:strategy-distance}\n   202\t\t\\uses{def:tensor-product-strategy, def:state-distance, def:povm-distance}\n   203\t\tLet $\\mathfrak G = (\\mathcal X, \\mathcal Y, \\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mu, D)$ be a nonlocal game and let $\\mathcal S = (\\psi, A, B)$ and $\\mathcal S' = (\\psi', A', B')$ be strategies for $\\mathfrak G$. For $\\delta \\in [0,1]$ we say that \\emph{$\\mathcal S$ is $\\delta$-close to $\\mathcal S'$} if the following conditions hold.\n   204\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   205\t\t\t\\item The states $\\ket{\\psi}, \\ket{\\psi'}$ are states in the same Hilbert space $\\mathcal H_A \\ot \\mathcal H_B$ and are $\\delta$-close.\n   206\t\t\t\\item For all $x \\in \\mathcal X$ and $y \\in \\mathcal Y$, we have $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta (A')^x_a$ and $B^y_b \\approx_\\delta (B')^y_b$, with the approximations holding under the distribution $\\mu$ (i.e.\\ under its corresponding marginals), on either $\\ket{\\psi}$ or $\\ket{\\psi'}$.\n   207\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   208\t\\end{definition}\n   209\t\n   210\t\\begin{remark}[Asymptotic reading of the distance relations]\\label{rem:asymptotic-distance}\n   211\t\tDefinitions~\\ref{def:state-distance}, \\ref{def:consistency}, and \\ref{def:povm-distance} are asymptotic: every $\\approx_\\delta$ and $\\simeq_\\delta$ statement refers to families of states and measurement families indexed by an implicit parameter $n \\in \\N$, and the $O(\\cdot)$ is taken as $n \\to \\infty$. None of these relations is a pointwise inequality with an explicit constant. By contrast, the corresponding relations used in the low individual degree chapters (Definitions~\\ref{def:simeq} and~\\ref{def:approx_delta}) carry no hidden constant. Any explicit quantitative version of the present relations therefore retains the constants absorbed into the $O(\\cdot)$; for instance, the triangle inequality of Lemma~\\ref{fact:triangle} carries a factor $2$ inside the $O(\\cdot)$.\n   212\t\\end{remark}\n   213\t\n   214\tWe record several useful facts about the consistency measure and the state-dependent distance. For the statements imported without proof, readers are referred to Sections~4.4 and~4.5 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}.\n   215\t\n   216\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency versus closeness; Facts 4.13 and 4.14 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:agreement}\n   217\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:povm-distance}\n   218\t\tFor POVMs $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$, the following hold.\n   219\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   220\t\t\t\\item If $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   221\t\t\t\\item If $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ \\emph{and} $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ are projective measurements, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   222\t\t\t\\item If $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$ and either $\\{A^x_a\\}$ or $\\{B^x_a\\}$ is a projective measurement, then $A^x_a \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\delta^{1/2}} \\Id_A \\ot B^x_a$.\n   223\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   224\t\\end{lemma}\n   225\t\\begin{proof}\n   226\t\tStated in the source without proof; these are Facts 4.13 and 4.14 of \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For the first item, expand each term $\\lVert (A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2$ and use $ (A^x_a)^2 \\le A^x_a$, $(B^x_a)^2 \\le B^x_a$ together with completeness of the two POVMs: summing over $a$ and averaging over $x$ leaves exactly twice the off-diagonal mass, which is $O(\\delta)$ by hypothesis. For the converse items, write the off-diagonal mass $\\sum_{a \\ne b} \\bra{\\psi} A^x_a \\ot B^x_b \\ket{\\psi}$ as an inner product involving $(A^x_a \\ot \\Id - \\Id \\ot B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi}$ and use projectivity of both families, or of one family combined with the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality; the latter route costs a square root in the error.\n   227\t\\end{proof}\n   228\t\n   229\t\\begin{lemma}[Fact 4.20 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:add-a-proj}\n   230\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   231\t\tLet $\\mathcal A, \\mathcal B, \\mathcal C$ be finite sets, and let $\\mu$ be a distribution over question pairs $(x,y)$. Let $\\{A^x_{a,\\,b}\\}$ and $\\{B^x_{a,\\,b}\\}$ be operators whose outcomes range over the product set $\\mathcal A \\times \\mathcal B$. Suppose a set of operators $\\{C^y_{a,\\,c}\\}$, whose outcomes range over the product set $\\mathcal A \\times \\mathcal C$, satisfies the condition $\\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\,c})^\\dagger C^y_{a,\\,c} \\le \\Id$ for all $a$ and all $y$. If $A^x_{a,\\,b} \\approx_\\delta B^x_{a,\\,b}$ on average over $x$ sampled from the corresponding marginal of the distribution $\\mu$, then $C^y_{a,\\,c} A^x_{a,\\,b} \\approx_\\delta C^y_{a,\\,c} B^x_{a,\\,b}$ on average over $(x,y)$ sampled from $\\mu$.\n   232\t\\end{lemma}\n   233\t\\begin{proof}\n   234\t\tFix questions $x, y$ and answers $a \\in \\mathcal A$, $b \\in \\mathcal B$. Then\n   235\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   236\t\t\t\\sum_c \\bigl\\lVert (C^y_{a,\\,c} A^x_{a,\\,b} - C^y_{a,\\,c} B^x_{a,\\,b})\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2\n   237\t\t\t&= \\bra{\\psi} (A^x_{a,\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\,b})^\\dagger \\Bigl( \\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\,c})^\\dagger C^y_{a,\\,c} \\Bigr) (A^x_{a,\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\,b}) \\ket{\\psi}\\\\\n   238\t\t\t&\\le \\bigl\\lVert (A^x_{a,\\,b} - B^x_{a,\\,b})\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2\\,,\n   239\t\t\\end{align*}\n   240\t\twhere the inequality uses the assumption $\\sum_c (C^y_{a,\\,c})^\\dagger C^y_{a,\\,c} \\le \\Id$. Summing over $a, b$ and averaging over $(x,y) \\sim \\mu$ yields the conclusion.\n   241\t\\end{proof}\n   242\t\n   243\t\\begin{lemma}[Multiplying by function-indexed operators]\\label{fact:add-a-proj2}\n   244\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   245\t\tLet $\\mathcal X, \\mathcal A$ be finite sets, and let $\\mathcal G$ be a set of functions $g \\colon \\mathcal X \\to \\mathcal A$. Let $\\{A^x_a\\}$ and $\\{B^x_a\\}$ be POVMs indexed by $\\mathcal X$ with outcomes in $\\mathcal A$. Let $\\{S^x_g\\}_{x \\in \\mathcal X,\\, g \\in \\mathcal G}$ be a set of operators such that for all $x \\in \\mathcal X$, $\\sum_g (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\le \\Id$. If $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ on average over $x$, then $S^x_g A^x_{g(x)} \\approx_\\delta S^x_g B^x_{g(x)}$.\n   246\t\\end{lemma}\n   247\t\\begin{proof}\n   248\t\tExpand the left-hand side and group the sum over $g$ according to the value $a = g(x)$:\n   249\t\t\\[\n   250\t\t\t\\E_x \\sum_g \\bigl\\lVert S^x_g (A^x_{g(x)} - B^x_{g(x)})\\, \\ket{\\psi} \\bigr\\rVert^2\n   251\t\t\t= \\E_x \\sum_a \\bra{\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\dagger \\Bigl( \\sum_{g\\,:\\,g(x)=a} (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\Bigr) (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi}\\,.\n   252\t\t\\]\n   253\t\tSince $\\sum_{g\\,:\\,g(x)=a} (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\le \\sum_g (S^x_g)^\\dagger S^x_g \\le \\Id$, this is at most $\\E_x \\sum_a \\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2 \\le \\delta$.\n   254\t\\end{proof}\n   255\t\n   256\t\\begin{lemma}[Absorbing an approximating family into a projective sum]\\label{lem:cool-closeness-fact}\n   257\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance, def:povm-conventions}\n   258\t\tLet $\\mathcal X$ and $\\mathcal A$ be finite sets and $\\mu$ a distribution on $\\mathcal X$. Let $\\{A^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ be a projective measurement and let $\\{B^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal A}$ be a set of matrices. If $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$, then for all subsets $S \\subseteq \\mathcal A$ we have\n   259\t\t\\[\n   260\t\t\t\\sum_{a \\in S} A^x_a \\approx_\\delta \\sum_{a \\in S} A^x_a \\cdot B^x_a\\,.\n   261\t\t\\]\n   262\t\\end{lemma}\n   263\t\\begin{proof}\n   264\t\tBy projectivity, $\\sum_{a \\in S} (A^x_a - A^x_a B^x_a) = \\sum_{a \\in S} A^x_a (A^x_a - B^x_a)$. Expanding the squared norm of the corresponding vector, the cross terms for distinct $a \\ne a' \\in S$ vanish because $A^x_a A^x_{a'} = 0$, so\n   265\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   266\t\t\t\\E_x \\Bigl\\lVert \\sum_{a \\in S} \\bigl(A^x_a - A^x_a B^x_a\\bigr) \\ket{\\psi} \\Bigr\\rVert^2\n   267\t\t\t&= \\E_x \\sum_{a \\in S} \\bra{\\psi} (A^x_a - B^x_a)^\\dagger A^x_a (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi}\\\\\n   268\t\t\t&\\le \\E_x \\sum_a \\lVert (A^x_a - B^x_a)\\ket{\\psi} \\rVert^2\\,,\n   269\t\t\\end{align*}\n   270\t\twhere the last step uses $A^x_a \\le \\Id$. All expectations are taken according to $\\mu$, and the last quantity is at most $\\delta$ by assumption.\n   271\t\\end{proof}\n   272\t\n   273\t\\begin{lemma}[Triangle inequality; Fact 4.28 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{fact:triangle}\n   274\t\t\\uses{def:povm-distance}\n   275\t\tIf $A^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a$ and $B^x_a \\approx_{\\eps} C^x_a$, then $A^x_a \\approx_{\\delta+\\eps} C^x_a$.\n   330\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:commutation-analysis-3}\n   331\t\t\tA^x_{a,\\,b}\\, C^x_{a,\\,c} \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}\\,.\n   332\t\t\\end{equation}\n   333\t\tA symmetric argument gives\n   334\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:commutation-analysis-4}\n   335\t\t\tC^x_{a,\\,c}\\, A^x_{a,\\,b} \\ot \\Id_B \\approx_\\delta \\Id_A \\ot B^x_{a,\\,b,\\,c}\\,.\n   336\t\t\\end{equation}\n   337\t\tThe claim follows by subtracting \\eqref{eq:commutation-analysis-3} and \\eqref{eq:commutation-analysis-4}.\n   338\t\\end{proof}\n   339\t\n   340\t\\begin{lemma}[Sandwiched simultaneous measurement; modified from Fact 4.34 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{lem:ld-sandwich}\n   341\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n   342\t\tLet $k \\ge 0$ be an integer and let $\\eps > 0$. Let $\\mathcal X$ be a finite set and $\\mu$ a distribution over $\\mathcal X$. For each $1 \\le i \\le k$ let $\\mathcal G_i$ be a finite set of functions $g_i \\colon \\mathcal Y \\to \\mathcal R_i$, and for each $x \\in \\mathcal X$ let $\\{G^{i,\\,x}_g\\}_{g \\in \\mathcal G_i}$ be a projective measurement. Suppose that for all $i \\in \\{1,\\dots,k\\}$ the set $\\mathcal G_i$ satisfies the following property: for any two $g_i \\ne g_i' \\in \\mathcal G_i$, the probability that $g_i(y) = g_i'(y)$ over a uniformly random $y \\in \\mathcal Y$ is at most $\\eps$.\n   343\t\n   344\t\tLet $\\bigl\\{ A^x_{g_1,\\,g_2,\\,\\dots,\\,g_k} \\bigr\\}$ be a projective measurement with outcomes $(g_1,\\dots,g_k) \\in \\mathcal G_1 \\times \\cdots \\times \\mathcal G_k$. Here $\\operatorname{eval}_y$ denotes the evaluation map $g \\mapsto g(y)$, so that $A^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot)_i = a_i]}$ post-processes the outcome tuple by evaluating its $i$-th component at $y$ (Definition~\\ref{def:bracket}). For each $1 \\le i \\le k$, suppose that on average over $x \\sim \\mu$ and $y \\in \\mathcal Y$ sampled uniformly at random,\n   345\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:ld-sandwich-1}\n   346\t\t\tA^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot)_i = a_i]} \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{\\delta} \\Id_A \\ot G^{i,\\,x}_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot) = a_i]}\\,.\n   347\t\t\\end{equation}\n   348\t\t% The source (references/qpbt-paper/06_nonlocal_games_and_mipstar.tex:465-495) prints the\n   349\t\t% transposed pairing G^{k,x}_{g_1} ... G^{1,x}_{g_k} ... G^{k,x}_{g_1}, which is undefined\n   350\t\t% when the outcome sets differ; the pairing below is the one of Fact 4.34 in the NEEXP\n   351\t\t% source. The empty product for k = 0 is also made explicit here. See\n   352\t\t% rem:ld-sandwich-indexing.\n   353\t\tDefine the POVM family $\\{C^x_{g_1,\\,g_2,\\,\\dots,\\,g_k}\\}$, for $x \\in \\mathcal X$, by\n   354\t\t\\[\n   355\t\t\tC^x_{g_1,\\,g_2,\\,\\dots,\\,g_k} = G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k} \\cdots G^{2,\\,x}_{g_2}\\; G^{1,\\,x}_{g_1}\\; G^{2,\\,x}_{g_2} \\cdots G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k}\\,;\n   356\t\t\\]\n   357\t\tfor $k = 0$ the empty product is understood as the identity, so that $C^x$ is the measurement whose single outcome, the empty tuple, carries the operator $\\Id$. Then on average over $x \\sim \\mu$ and $y \\in \\mathcal Y$ sampled uniformly at random,\n   358\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:ld-sandwich-2}\n   359\t\t\tA^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot) = (a_1,\\,a_2,\\,\\dots,\\,a_k)]} \\ot \\Id_B \\simeq_{k(\\delta+\\eps)^{1/2}} \\Id_A \\ot C^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_y(\\cdot) = (a_1,\\,a_2,\\,\\dots,\\,a_k)]}\\,.\n   360\t\t\\end{equation}\n   361\t\\end{lemma}\n   362\t\\begin{proof}\n   363\t\tThe source proof is a citation: the argument is identical to the proof of Fact 4.34 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, with the only modification being the insertion of the dependence on the question $x$ for all measurements considered. In outline, the consistency hypotheses \\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-1} allow one to insert the projective factors $G^{i,\\,x}$ next to $A^x$ one index at a time, and the $\\eps$-bound on the pairwise agreement of distinct functions in $\\mathcal G_i$ controls the events on which two different outcomes collide at the sampled point $y$. Each of the $k$ insertions costs $(\\delta+\\eps)^{1/2}$ by the Cauchy--Schwarz inequality, producing the sandwich $C^x$ with total error $k(\\delta+\\eps)^{1/2}$.\n   364\t\\end{proof}\n   365\t\n   366\t\\begin{remark}[Index pairing in the sandwich]\\label{rem:ld-sandwich-indexing}\n   367\t\tAs printed in the source, the sandwich in Lemma~\\ref{lem:ld-sandwich} pairs the $i$-th measurement with the outcome $g_{k+1-i}$: the outermost factor $G^{k,\\,x}$ carries the outcome $g_1$ and the innermost factor $G^{1,\\,x}$ carries $g_k$. Since the measurement $\\{G^{i,\\,x}_g\\}$ is indexed by $g \\in \\mathcal G_i$, the operator $G^{k,\\,x}_{g_1}$ is undefined whenever $\\mathcal G_1 \\ne \\mathcal G_k$, so the printed pairing is a typographical slip. The statement above instead uses the pairing $G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k} \\cdots G^{1,\\,x}_{g_1} \\cdots G^{k,\\,x}_{g_k}$ of Fact 4.34 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}, from which the lemma is imported. The source also leaves the case $k = 0$ implicit; with the empty-product convention recorded in the statement, both sides of \\eqref{eq:ld-sandwich-2} are then the identity on the single empty-tuple outcome and the conclusion holds with error zero.\n   368\t\\end{remark}\n   369\t\n   370\t\\begin{lemma}[Pasting two consistent measurements; Fact 4.35 in \\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}]\\label{lem:pasting}\n   371\t\t\\uses{def:consistency, def:bracket, def:povm-conventions}\n   372\t\tLet $\\mathcal D$ be a distribution on $(x, y_1, y_2) \\in \\mathcal X \\times \\mathcal Y_1 \\times \\mathcal Y_2$. For $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$ let $\\mathcal G_i$ be a collection of functions $g_i \\colon \\mathcal Y_i \\to \\mathcal R_i$ and let $\\{(G_i)^x_g\\}_{g \\in \\mathcal G_i}$ be families of measurements such that $\\{(G_2)^x_g\\}_g$ is projective for every $x$. Suppose further that for every $(x, y_1)$ it holds that for $g_2 \\ne g_2' \\in \\mathcal G_2$ the probability, on average over $y_2$ chosen from $\\mathcal D$ conditioned on $(x, y_1)$, that $g_2(y_2) = g_2'(y_2)$ is at most $\\eta$. Let $\\{A^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_1,\\,a_2}\\}$ be a family of projective measurements with outcomes $(a_1, a_2) \\in \\mathcal R_1 \\times \\mathcal R_2$ such that for $i \\in \\{1,2\\}$,\n   373\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:pasting-1}\n   374\t\t\tA^{x,y_1,y_2}_{a_i} \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot (G_i)^x_{[\\operatorname{eval}_{y_i}(\\cdot)=a_i]}\n   375\t\t\\end{equation}\n   505\t\\label{sec:types}\n   506\t\n   507\tThe question distribution of the Pauli basis test is \\emph{typed}: each question is a pair consisting of a type, drawn together with the other player's type from the edges of a fixed \\emph{type graph}, and a question content, drawn from a CL distribution determined by the pair of types.\n   508\t\n   509\t\\begin{definition}[Typed conditionally linear functions]\\label{def:typed-cl-functions}\n   510\t\t\\uses{def:cl-func}\n   511\t\tLet $\\mathcal T$ be a finite set (the \\emph{type set}) and let $V$ be $\\F^n$ for some integer $n \\ge 0$. A \\emph{$\\mathcal T$-typed family of $\\ell$-level conditionally linear functions} (implicitly, \\emph{on $V$}) is a collection $\\{L_t\\}_{t \\in \\mathcal T}$ such that, for each $t \\in \\mathcal T$, $L_t$ is an $\\ell$-level conditionally linear function on $V$.\n   512\t\\end{definition}\n   513\t\n   514\t% Nonemptiness of the edge set is stated explicitly here: with E empty, m = k = 0 and the\n   515\t% normalization 1/(2m-k) below is undefined. The source\n   516\t% (references/qpbt-paper/07_types.tex:65-82) leaves this boundary hypothesis implicit.\n   517\t\\begin{definition}[Graph distribution]\\label{def:graph-distribution}\n   518\t\tLet $G = (U, E)$ be an undirected graph with vertex set $U$ and nonempty edge set $E$. Edges in $E$ are written as multisets $\\{u, v\\}$ of two vertices; the case $u = v$ represents a self-loop. Suppose there are $m \\ge 1$ edges, $k$ of which are self-loops. Then the \\emph{graph distribution $\\mu_G$ of $G$} is the distribution over $U \\times U$ such that for every $(u,v) \\in U \\times U$,\n   519\t\t\\[\n   520\t\t\t\\mu_G(u, v) =\n   521\t\t\t\\begin{cases}\n   522\t\t\t\t1/(2m-k) & \\text{if } \\{u,v\\} \\in E\\,,\\\\\n   523\t\t\t\t0 & \\text{otherwise}\\,.\n   524\t\t\t\\end{cases}\n   525\t\t\\]\n   526\t\tThis is identical to the uniform distribution over ordered pairs $(u,v) \\in U \\times U$ such that $\\{u,v\\} \\in E$: each edge that is not a self-loop contributes two ordered pairs, and each self-loop contributes one, whence the normalization $2m-k$.\n   527\t\\end{definition}\n   528\t\n   529\t\\begin{definition}[Typed conditionally linear distributions]\\label{def:typed-cl-distributions}\n   530\t\t\\uses{def:typed-cl-functions, def:graph-distribution, def:cl-dist}\n   531\t\tLet $\\mathcal T$ be a type set and let $L = \\{L_u\\}_{u \\in \\mathcal T}$ and $R = \\{R_v\\}_{v \\in \\mathcal T}$ be $\\mathcal T$-typed families of conditionally linear functions on $V$. Let $G = (\\mathcal T, E)$ be a graph with vertex set $\\mathcal T$. The \\emph{$(\\mathcal T, G)$-typed conditionally linear distribution $\\mu^G_{L,\\,R}$ corresponding to $(L, R)$} is the distribution over pairs $\\bigl( (u, x), (v, y) \\bigr)$, where the type pair $(u,v)$ is drawn from the graph distribution $\\mu_G$ and the content pair $(x,y)$ is drawn from $\\mu_{L_u,\\,R_v}$.\n   532\t\\end{definition}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:306:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_157","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch13_qpbt_test.tex | sed -n '1,155p;225,255p;335,365p'\"","aggregated_output":"     1\t\\chapter{The Pauli basis test}\n     2\t\\label{chap:qpbt-test}\n     3\t% Paper label sec:pauli-verifier kept for cross-reference fidelity; it resolves to this chapter.\n     4\t\\label{sec:pauli-verifier}\n     5\t\n     6\tThis chapter defines the two testing primitives underlying the question-reduction step: the \\emph{classical low individual degree game}, in its simultaneous form, and the \\emph{Pauli basis test}, a nonlocal game built from the classical game together with the Magic Square game. We state the quantum soundness of the classical game (Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}, imported; see Remark~\\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider}), the rigidity of the Magic Square game (Theorem~\\ref{thm:ms-rigidity}, imported), completeness of the Pauli basis test (Lemma~\\ref{lem:pauli-completeness}), and the main soundness theorem for the Pauli basis test (Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli}), whose proof occupies Chapters~\\ref{chap:qpbt-observables}--\\ref{chap:qpbt-extraction} and is completed in Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-extraction}. The chapter closes with the qubit form of the soundness statement (Corollary~\\ref{cor:pauli-binary}) and the canonical parameter choice with its soundness bound (Definition~\\ref{def:introparams}, Lemma~\\ref{lem:delta-bound}).\n     7\t\n     8\tThroughout, the two players are denoted $\\mathrm A$ and $\\mathrm B$; for $w \\in \\{\\mathrm A,\\mathrm B\\}$ we write $\\overline w$ for the other player. Games, strategies, values, conditionally linear (CL) functions and distributions, typed distributions sampled from the edges of a graph, and the state-dependent approximation $\\approx_\\delta$ between families of measurement operators are as in Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-games}; the algebraic material on lines, canonical linear maps, the low-degree encoding, and generalized Pauli observables is from Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra}.\n     9\t\n    10\t\\section{The classical low individual degree game}\n    11\t% Paper labels sec:ld-verifier and sec:ld-game kept for cross-reference fidelity.\n    12\t\\label{sec:ld-verifier}\n    13\t\\label{sec:ld-game}\n    14\t\n    15\tThe classical low individual degree game is a two-player game based on the low individual degree variant of the multilinearity test of Babai, Fortnow, and Lund. In the basic version, one player (the ``points player'') receives a uniformly random point $u \\in \\F_q^m$ and answers with a value $a \\in \\F_q$, while the other player (the ``lines player'') receives a random line through $u$ --- either axis-parallel or diagonal --- and answers with a univariate polynomial describing the restriction of a global function to that line. If the players share a polynomial $g\\colon \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$ of individual degree at most $d$ and answer with $g(u)$ and with the restriction $g|_\\ell$, they win with probability $1$; the soundness theorem below states that the converse approximately holds. In the simultaneous version, the players' answers are $k$-tuples, checked coordinatewise against a $k$-tuple of low individual degree polynomials.\n    16\t\n    17\t\\begin{definition}[The classical low individual degree game: parameters and registers]\\label{def:ld-game}\n    18\t\t\\uses{def:game, def:admissible-size}\n    19\t\tThe game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}$ is parametrized by a tuple $\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$, where $m,d,k \\in \\N$, $q \\in \\N$ is an admissible field size (Definition~\\ref{def:admissible-size}), and $m$ divides $q$. We write $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\mathsf{ldparams}}$ to emphasize the dependence on the parameter tuple. The type set of $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}$ is\n    20\t\t\\[\n    21\t\t\t\\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{ld}} = \\{\\mathsf{Point}, \\mathsf{ALine}, \\mathsf{DLine}\\}\\;,\n    22\t\t\\]\n    23\t\tand the distribution over type pairs $(t_{\\mathrm A}, t_{\\mathrm B})$ is uniform over $\\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{ld}} \\times \\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{ld}}$. The ambient space is the direct sum $V = V_X \\oplus V_I \\oplus V_V$ of three register subspaces: the \\emph{point register} $V_X \\cong \\F_q^m$, the \\emph{coordinate register} $V_I \\cong \\F_q$, and the \\emph{direction register} $V_V \\cong \\F_q^m$. Elements of $V$ are identified with triples $(u,s,v) \\in \\F_q^m \\times \\F_q \\times \\F_q^m$. The question distribution and the win predicate of $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}$ are specified in Definitions~\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} and~\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} below.\n    24\t\\end{definition}\n    25\t\n    26\t\\begin{definition}[Question distribution of the classical low individual degree game]\\label{def:ld-question-distribution}\n    27\t\t\\uses{def:ld-game, def:cl-func, def:cl-dist, lem:cl-concat, def:cl-canonical, def:line-representative}\n    28\t\tLet $\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ be as in Definition~\\ref{def:ld-game}. Define $L_{\\mathsf{Point}}$ to be the $1$-level CL function on $V$ that projects onto $V_X$: for every $(u,s,v) \\in V$,\n    29\t\t\\begin{equation}\n    30\t\t\t\\label{eq:cl-ptf}\n    31\t\t\tL_{\\mathsf{Point}}(u,s,v) = (u,0,0)\\;.\n    32\t\t\\end{equation}\n    33\t\tFor $s \\in \\F_q$, let $\\chi(s)$ be the unique integer such that\n    34\t\t\\begin{equation}\n    35\t\t\t\\label{eq:chi-func}\n    36\t\t\ts = (\\chi(s)-1)\\,\\frac{q}{m} + r\n    37\t\t\\end{equation}\n    38\t\tfor some $0 \\le r < q/m$, where $s$ is interpreted as an integer in $\\{0,1,\\ldots,q-1\\}$ under the identification of $\\F_q$ with binary strings fixed in Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra}. Since $m$ divides $q$, when $s$ is uniformly distributed on $\\F_q$ the index $\\chi(s)$ is uniformly distributed on $\\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$. Define $L_{\\mathsf{ALine}}$ by\n    39\t\t\\begin{equation}\n    40\t\t\t\\label{eq:cl-alnf}\n    41\t\t\tL_{\\mathsf{ALine}}(u,s,v) = \\bigl(L^{\\mathrm{Ln}}_{e_i}(u),\\, s,\\, 0\\bigr)\\quad\\text{for all } (u,s,v) \\in V\\;,\n    42\t\t\\end{equation}\n    43\t\twhere $i = \\chi(s)$ and $L^{\\mathrm{Ln}}_v$ denotes the canonical linear map with one-dimensional kernel spanned by $v$, used to compute canonical representatives of lines (Definition~\\ref{def:line-representative}). The function $L_{\\mathsf{ALine}}$ is the concatenation (in the sense of Lemma~\\ref{lem:cl-concat}) of the $1$-level CL function projecting $V_I \\oplus V_V$ onto $V_I$ with the family of $1$-level CL functions $\\{L^{\\mathrm{Ln}}_{e_i}\\}$ on $V_X$ indexed by $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots,m\\}$; thus $L_{\\mathsf{ALine}}$ is a $2$-level CL function. Define $L_{\\mathsf{DLine}}$ by\n    44\t\t\\begin{equation}\n    45\t\t\t\\label{eq:cl-dlnf}\n    46\t\t\tL_{\\mathsf{DLine}}(u,s,v) = \\bigl(L^{\\mathrm{Ln}}_{\\pi_{i-1}(v)}(u),\\, s,\\, \\pi_{i-1}(v)\\bigr)\\;,\n    47\t\t\\end{equation}\n    48\t\twhere $i = \\chi(s)$ and $\\pi_{i-1}$ zeroes out the first $i-1$ coordinates of its input. The function $L_{\\mathsf{DLine}}$ is the concatenation of (i) the identity on $V_I$, (ii) for each $i$ the $1$-level CL function $v \\mapsto \\pi_{i-1}(v)$ on $V_V$, and (iii) for each pair $(s,v') \\in V_I \\oplus V_V$ the $1$-level CL function $L^{\\mathrm{Ln}}_{v'}$ on $V_X$; thus $L_{\\mathsf{DLine}}$ is a $3$-level CL function.\n    49\t\n    50\t\tThe question distribution of $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}$ is the typed CL distribution obtained as follows: sample a type pair $(t_{\\mathrm A}, t_{\\mathrm B})$ uniformly from $\\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{ld}} \\times \\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{ld}}$, sample $z = (u,s,v)$ uniformly from $V$, and send each player $w \\in \\{\\mathrm A,\\mathrm B\\}$ the question $(t_w, L_{t_w}(z))$.\n    51\t\\end{definition}\n    52\t\n    53\tThe next two lemmas show that the CL functions $L_{\\mathsf{Point}}$, $L_{\\mathsf{ALine}}$, $L_{\\mathsf{DLine}}$ give rise to the axis-parallel and diagonal line-versus-point distributions.\n    54\t\n    55\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:alnf}\n    56\t\t\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:line}\n    57\t\tConsider the pair $(\\ell, u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the tuple $\\bigl((u_0,s,0),(u,0,0)\\bigr)$ from the CL distribution $\\mu_{L_{\\mathsf{ALine}}, L_{\\mathsf{Point}}}$, and let $\\ell = \\ell(u_0, e_i)$ where $i = \\chi(s)$. Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\F_q^m$, and $\\ell$ is a line that goes through $u$ and is parallel to a uniformly random axis $i \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$.\n    58\t\\end{lemma}\n    59\t\\begin{proof}\n    60\t\t\\uses{def:line-representative}\n    61\t\tSince $s$ is uniformly random in $\\F_q$ and $m$ divides $q$, the index $i = \\chi(s)$ is uniformly random in $\\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$, so $(u_0,s)$ specifies a uniformly random axis-parallel line $\\ell(u_0,e_i)$. Since $u_0 = L^{\\mathrm{Ln}}_{e_i}(u)$ is the canonical representative of the line through $u$ in direction $e_i$, we have $u \\in \\ell(u_0,e_i)$.\n    62\t\\end{proof}\n    63\t\n    64\t\\begin{lemma}\\label{lem:dlnf}\n    65\t\t\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:line}\n    66\t\tConsider the pair $(\\ell, u)$ sampled in the following way: first, sample the tuple $\\bigl((u_0,s,v),(u,0,0)\\bigr)$ from the CL distribution $\\mu_{L_{\\mathsf{DLine}}, L_{\\mathsf{Point}}}$, and let $\\ell = \\ell(u_0, v)$ where $i = \\chi(s)$. Then $u$ is a uniformly random point in $\\F_q^m$, and $\\ell$ is a random diagonal line that goes through $u$ and shares its first $i-1$ coordinates with $u$, for uniformly random $i \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,m\\}$.\n    67\t\\end{lemma}\n    68\t\\begin{proof}\n    69\t\t\\uses{lem:alnf}\n    70\t\tThe proof is identical to that of Lemma~\\ref{lem:alnf}: uniformity of $s$ makes $i = \\chi(s)$ uniform, the direction component of the sampled tuple is $\\pi_{i-1}(v)$ for uniform $v$ (so its first $i-1$ coordinates vanish), and $u_0 = L^{\\mathrm{Ln}}_{\\pi_{i-1}(v)}(u)$ places $u$ on the line $\\ell(u_0,\\pi_{i-1}(v))$.\n    71\t\\end{proof}\n    72\t\n    73\t\\begin{definition}[Line-point distributions]\\label{def:line-point-dist}\n    74\t\t\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, lem:alnf, lem:dlnf}\n    75\t\tThe \\emph{axis line-point distribution} $D_{\\mathsf{ALine}}$ is the distribution $\\mu_{L_{\\mathsf{ALine}}, L_{\\mathsf{Point}}}$ over pairs $(\\ell = (u_0,e_i),\\, u)$, where we omit the $0$ components for simplicity. The \\emph{diagonal line-point distribution} $D_{\\mathsf{DLine}}$ is the distribution $\\mu_{L_{\\mathsf{DLine}}, L_{\\mathsf{Point}}}$ over pairs $(\\ell = (u_0,s,v),\\, u)$. The \\emph{line-point distribution} $D_{\\mathsf{Line}}$ is the equal mixture of the two: with probability $\\tfrac12$ output a sample from $D_{\\mathsf{ALine}}$, and with probability $\\tfrac12$ output a sample from $D_{\\mathsf{DLine}}$.\n    76\t\\end{definition}\n    77\t\n    78\t\\begin{definition}[Win predicate of the classical low individual degree game]\\label{def:ld-win-predicate}\n    79\t\t\\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:line, def:polyfunc}\n    80\t\tFix $\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$. Answers in the game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\mathsf{ldparams}}$ take the following forms, depending on the question type; answers not of the prescribed form are rejected.\n    81\t\t\\begin{itemize}\n    82\t\t\t\\item Type $\\mathsf{Point}$, question content $u \\in \\F_q^m$: the answer is an element of $\\F_q^k$.\n    83\t\t\t\\item Type $\\mathsf{ALine}$, question content $(u_0,s) \\in \\F_q^m \\times \\F_q$, specifying the axis-parallel line $\\ell(u_0,e_i)$ with $i = \\chi(s)$: the answer is a $k$-tuple $(f_1,\\ldots,f_k)$ of univariate polynomials $f_j\\colon \\F_q \\to \\F_q$ of degree at most $d$, each given by its $d+1$ coefficients.\n    84\t\t\t\\item Type $\\mathsf{DLine}$, question content $(u_0,s,v) \\in \\F_q^m \\times \\F_q \\times \\F_q^m$, specifying the diagonal line $\\ell(u_0,v')$ with $v' = \\pi_{i-1}(v)$ and $i = \\chi(s)$: the answer is a $k$-tuple of univariate polynomials of degree at most $md$.\n    85\t\t\\end{itemize}\n    86\t\tThe win predicate $D^{\\mathrm{ld}} = D^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\mathsf{ldparams}}$ is the function of the tuple $(t_{\\mathrm A}, x_{\\mathrm A}, t_{\\mathrm B}, x_{\\mathrm B}, a_{\\mathrm A}, a_{\\mathrm B})$ determined by the following clauses, evaluated for $w \\in \\{\\mathrm A,\\mathrm B\\}$; in all cases where no action is indicated, the predicate accepts.\n    87\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n    88\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Consistency test}) If $t_{\\mathrm A} = t_{\\mathrm B}$, accept if and only if $a_{\\mathrm A} = a_{\\mathrm B}$.\n    89\t\t\t% The source phrases both clauses with \"the\" t such that x = u_0 + tv; that phrase\n    90\t\t\t% does not determine t on diagonal questions with direction v' = 0, which the\n    91\t\t\t% question distribution samples with positive probability. The universal\n    92\t\t\t% quantification over t used here is the local correction; see the remark below.\n    93\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Axis-parallel line-versus-point test}) If $t_w = \\mathsf{ALine}$ and $t_{\\overline w} = \\mathsf{Point}$, accept if and only if $f_j(t) = (a_{\\overline w})_j$ for all $j \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,k\\}$ and all $t \\in \\F_q$ such that $x_{\\overline w} = u_0 + t e_i$, where $i = \\chi(s)$.\n    94\t\t\t\\item (\\textbf{Diagonal line-versus-point test}) If $t_w = \\mathsf{DLine}$ and $t_{\\overline w} = \\mathsf{Point}$, accept if and only if $f_j(t) = (a_{\\overline w})_j$ for all $j \\in \\{1,2,\\ldots,k\\}$ and all $t \\in \\F_q$ such that $x_{\\overline w} = u_0 + t v'$, where $v' = \\pi_{i-1}(v)$ and $i = \\chi(s)$.\n    95\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n    96\t\tThe game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\mathsf{ldparams}}$ is the typed game with type set $\\mathcal T^{\\mathrm{ld}}$, the question distribution of Definition~\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution}, and win predicate $D^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\mathsf{ldparams}}$.\n    97\t\\end{definition}\n    98\t\n    99\t\\begin{remark}[Diagonal questions with zero direction]\\label{rem:ld-win-zero-direction}\n   100\t\tThe source states the two line-versus-point clauses with ``the'' $t \\in \\F_q$ such that $x_{\\overline w} = u_0 + t v$. When the direction is nonzero and $x_{\\overline w}$ lies on the line, exactly one such $t$ exists and the formulation above agrees with the source. But zero directions occur: Definition~\\ref{def:line} explicitly permits them, and the question distribution of Definition~\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution} produces $\\mathsf{DLine}$ questions with $v' = \\pi_{i-1}(v) = 0$ with positive probability, namely whenever the last $m - i + 1$ coordinates of $v$ vanish. In that case $\\ell(u_0, 0) = \\{u_0\\}$, every $t \\in \\F_q$ satisfies $x_{\\overline w} = u_0 + t v'$ when $x_{\\overline w} = u_0$, and the source clause does not determine acceptance for a nonconstant answer polynomial. The universal quantification over $t$ adopted in Definition~\\ref{def:ld-win-predicate} makes the predicate a well-defined function of its arguments; it accepts vacuously when no such $t$ exists, a case the question distribution never produces. Completeness is unaffected: the restriction of a polynomial $g$ to the singleton line $\\ell(u_0,0)$, expressed through the parameterization $t \\mapsto u_0 + t \\cdot 0$, is the constant polynomial with value $g(u_0)$, which passes the check for every $t$.\n   101\t\\end{remark}\n   102\t\n   103\tThe soundness properties of the low individual degree game are expressed in terms of the following class of measurements.\n   104\t\n   105\t\\begin{definition}[Low-degree polynomial measurements]\\label{def:ld-meas}\n   106\t\t\\uses{def:polyfunc, def:polymeasurements, def:polynomials-degree}\n   107\t\tLet $\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ denote the set of POVM measurements indexed by $\\polyfunc{m}{q}{d}$, the polynomial representatives of individual degree at most $d$ in each variable; this is the notion of Definition~\\ref{def:polymeasurements}, and the argument order $(m,q,d)$ follows that definition (the paper orders the arguments as $(m,d,q)$). By Definition~\\ref{def:polynomials-degree}, evaluation identifies these representatives with the corresponding polynomial functions $g\\colon \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$, bijectively whenever $d \\le q-1$; the parameter regimes of this chapter (Definition~\\ref{def:introparams} fixes $d = 1$, and admissibility gives $q \\ge 2$) always satisfy this. More generally, for an integer $k$ and tuples $m = (m_1,\\ldots,m_k)$, $q = (q_1,\\ldots,q_k)$, $d = (d_1,\\ldots,d_k)$, let $\\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$ denote the set of measurements $G = \\{G_{g_1,\\ldots,g_k}\\}$ such that for each $i \\in \\{1,\\ldots,k\\}$, the outcome index $g_i$ is a polynomial $g_i\\colon \\F_{q_i}^{m_i} \\to \\F_{q_i}$ of individual degree at most $d_i$.\n   108\t\\end{definition}\n   109\t\n   110\tQuantum soundness of a version of the classical low-degree test for polynomials of low \\emph{total} degree was claimed by Natarajan and Vidick, building on an analysis of a three-prover version of the test by Vidick; there is a known gap in the soundness analysis of that test, and it is not used here. Instead, the construction relies on the low \\emph{individual} degree test, whose quantum soundness is proved in~\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} and generalized in~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}. The statement below is the form most directly useful in what follows.\n   111\t\n   112\t% The paper states this result in a theorem environment with the label lem:ld-soundness.\n   113\t% We keep both the environment and the label verbatim for cross-reference fidelity.\n   114\t\\begin{theorem}[Quantum soundness of the simultaneous classical low individual degree test]\\label{lem:ld-soundness}\n   115\t\t\\uses{def:ld-game, def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:projective-strategy-general, def:tensor-product-value, def:povm-distance, def:bracket}\n   116\t\tThere exists a function\n   117\t\t\\[\n   118\t\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}(\\eps, q, m, d, k) = a\\,(dmk)^a \\bigl(\\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd}\\bigr)\n   119\t\t\\]\n   120\t\tfor universal constants $a \\ge 1$ and $0 < b \\le 1$ such that the following holds. For all $\\eps > 0$ and every parameter tuple $\\mathsf{ldparams} = (q,m,d,k)$ as in Definition~\\ref{def:ld-game} (so $q$ is an admissible field size and $m$ divides $q$), and for all projective strategies $(\\psi, A, B)$ that succeed with probability at least $1-\\eps$ in the game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{\\mathsf{ldparams}}$, there exist measurements\n   121\t\t\\[\n   122\t\t\tG^w \\in \\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)\n   123\t\t\\]\n   124\t\ton $\\hilb_w$ for $w \\in \\{\\mathrm A,\\mathrm B\\}$, where by slight abuse of notation $m$, $q$, $d$ denote the constant tuples $(m,\\ldots,m)$, $(q,\\ldots,q)$, $(d,\\ldots,d)$ of length $k$, such that\n   125\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   126\t\t\tA^{\\mathsf{Point},\\, u}_{a_1,\\ldots,a_k} \\ot I_{\\mathrm B}\n   127\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}}\n   128\t\t\tI_{\\mathrm A} \\ot G^{\\mathrm B}_{[(g_1(u),\\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\\ldots,a_k)]}\\;,\\\\\n   129\t\t\tG^{\\mathrm A}_{[(g_1(u),\\ldots,g_k(u)) = (a_1,\\ldots,a_k)]} \\ot I_{\\mathrm B}\n   130\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}}\n   131\t\t\tI_{\\mathrm A} \\ot B^{\\mathsf{Point},\\, u}_{a_1,\\ldots,a_k}\\;,\\\\\n   132\t\t\tG^{\\mathrm A}_{g_1,\\ldots,g_k} \\ot I_{\\mathrm B}\n   133\t\t\t&\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}}\n   134\t\t\tI_{\\mathrm A} \\ot G^{\\mathrm B}_{g_1,\\ldots,g_k}\\;,\n   135\t\t\\end{align*}\n   136\t\twhere $\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}} = \\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}(\\eps,q,m,d,k)$, all three approximations are with respect to the state $\\ket{\\psi}$, and the bracket notation for post-processed outcomes is that of Definition~\\ref{def:bracket}. The first two approximations hold on average over the uniform distribution on $u \\in \\F_q^m$; no question distribution is associated with the third.\n   137\t\\end{theorem}\n   138\t\\begin{proof}\n   139\t\t\\uses{def:ld-question-distribution, def:ld-win-predicate, def:ld-meas, def:povm-distance}\n   140\t\tLet $\\Sigma = \\F_q$ and let $\\code \\subseteq \\Sigma^q$ be the Reed--Solomon code of degree $d$: the set of all $(p(x_1),\\ldots,p(x_q))$ where $p\\colon \\F_q \\to \\F_q$ is a univariate polynomial of degree at most $d$ and $x_1,\\ldots,x_q$ enumerate $\\F_q$. We may assume $d < q$. In the opposite regime the statement is immediate: for $k \\ge 1$ and $d \\ge q$ the prefactor gives $\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}} \\ge a\\,(dmk)^a\\, q^{-b} \\ge q^{a-b} \\ge 1$, while the quantity bounded in Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance} never exceeds $2$ for families of POVM elements, so the three approximations hold for an arbitrary choice of $G^w \\in \\mathrm{PolyMeas}(m,q,d,k)$; and for $k = 0$ every answer tuple is empty, all measurements involved are single-outcome, and the approximations hold with error zero. For $d < q$, evaluation is injective on polynomials of degree at most $d$ and a nonzero polynomial of degree at most $d$ vanishes at no more than $d$ of the $q$ points, so $\\code$ is a linear $[q,\\, d+1,\\, q-d]_\\Sigma$ code.\n   141\t\t% The paper prints the parameters as [q, d+1, n-d+1] with an undefined n. With the\n   142\t\t% intended value n = q the printed distance q-d+1 exceeds the true minimum distance\n   143\t\t% q-d of the degree-d evaluation code, and the dimension claim d+1 requires d < q.\n   144\t\t% Both points are corrected above; the regime d >= q, which the parameter tuple of\n   145\t\t% Definition def:ld-game permits, is handled by the triviality argument.\n   146\t\tThe two-prover tensor-code test for the code $\\code^{\\ot m}$, as described in Figures~1 and~2 of~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, coincides with the game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}$ with parameters $\\mathsf{ldparams}$ and $k=1$: the axis-parallel line-versus-point test is their axis-parallel lines test, with the roles of the two players chosen uniformly at random, which is consistent with the two-prover variant of the tensor-code test from Section~4.1 there; the diagonal line-versus-point test is their subcube commutation test, again with the added symmetrization between the players; and the consistency test is their synchronicity test. Theorem~4.7 of~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$ (this parameter is unrelated to the simultaneity parameter $k$ of $\\mathsf{ldparams}$), therefore yields the present statement for $k = 1$. The case of general $k$ follows by a standard reduction, following exactly the derivation of Theorem~4.43 from Theorem~4.40 in~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Quantum soundness of the low individual degree test itself is the result of~\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree}; Remark~\\ref{rem:ld-soundness-provider} relates the present statement to Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal}, which proves that result.\n   147\t\\end{proof}\n   148\t\n   149\t\\begin{remark}[Relation to the low individual degree test of Chapter~\\ref{chap:test}]\\label{rem:ld-soundness-provider}\n   150\t\tTheorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is imported: the proof above reduces it to Theorem~4.7 of~\\cite{Ji2021Quantum}, instantiated with its internal parameter $k$ chosen as $m^3 d$, followed by the extension to simultaneous $k$-tuples of~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. The result of~\\cite{Ji2020LowIndividualDegree} that underlies it is proved here as Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal}: for every projective strategy passing the $(m,q,d)$ low individual degree test of Definition~\\ref{def:low-individual-degree-test} with probability at least $1-\\eps$, that theorem produces measurements in $\\polymeas{m}{q}{d}$ satisfying consistency relations of the same shape as the three approximations of Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}. Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} is not a consequence of Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal} alone: a derivation from it would require, in addition, an equivalence between the typed game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{ld}}_{(q,m,d,1)}$ and the $(m,q,d)$ test of Chapter~\\ref{chap:test} --- matching the two question distributions, the two win conditions, and the two error functions --- together with the extension from $k = 1$ to general $k$ by the reduction of~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} cited above. Neither implication is proved in this chapter, and the proof of Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} given above does not invoke Theorem~\\ref{thm:main-formal}.\n   151\t\\end{remark}\n   152\t\n   153\t\\section{The Magic Square game}\n   154\t% Paper label sec:ms kept for cross-reference fidelity.\n   155\t\\label{sec:ms}\n   225\t\t\\begin{equation}\\label{eq:am-i-hermitian}\n   226\t\t\t\\mathcal O_A \\mathcal O_B \\ot \\sigma^Z \\sigma^X\n   227\t\t\t= - \\mathcal O_A \\mathcal O_B \\ot \\sigma^X \\sigma^Z\n   228\t\t\t= \\mathcal O_B \\mathcal O_A \\ot \\sigma^X \\sigma^Z\n   229\t\t\t= (\\mathcal O_A \\mathcal O_B \\ot \\sigma^Z \\sigma^X)^\\dagger\\;,\n   230\t\t\\end{equation}\n   231\t\tso the bottom-right cell is Hermitian, and it squares to the identity; hence every cell is a genuine $\\pm 1$-valued observable. The three observables in each row and each column commute pairwise --- for the third row and third column this again uses Equation~\\eqref{eq:am-i-hermitian} --- so the $\\mathsf{Constraint}$ measurements are well defined, and the strategy is symmetric, projective, and commuting. A two-outcome projective measurement is consistent on a state if and only if its observable $\\mathcal O$ satisfies $\\mathcal O \\ot I \\ket{\\psi} = I \\ot \\mathcal O \\ket{\\psi}$; since $A$ and $B$ are consistent on $\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot n}$ by hypothesis and $\\sigma^X$, $\\sigma^Z$ are consistent on $\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_2}$, each cell observable is a product of consistent, commuting factors and is therefore consistent on $\\ket{\\psi}$, and consistency of the $\\mathsf{Constraint}$ measurements follows from the cellwise consistencies together with commutativity. Finally, the observables in each row and each of the first two columns multiply to $I$, while those of the third column multiply to $-I$; for the measurement $\\{S_0, S_1\\}$ obtained by measuring a $\\mathsf{Constraint}_i$ triple and outputting the sum of the three bits, the observable $S_0 - S_1$ equals the product of the three cell observables, hence $\\pm I$, which forces the three answer bits to sum to $0$ (respectively, $1$ for $\\mathsf{Constraint}_6$) with certainty. Together with consistency this shows that every check of the Magic Square game is passed with probability $1$, so the strategy has value $1$.\n   232\t\\end{proof}\n   233\t\n   234\t\\section{The Pauli basis test}\n   235\t% Paper label sec:qld-game kept for cross-reference fidelity.\n   236\t\\label{sec:qld-game}\n   237\t\n   238\tThe Pauli basis test is the quantum low individual degree test of Natarajan and Vidick, in the slightly modified form introduced by Natarajan and Wright~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. Informally, the test certifies that the players share the state $\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}$, where $M = 2^m$. On a question of type $(\\mathsf{Pauli}, W)$ with $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, a player is expected to measure the generalized Pauli basis POVM $\\{\\tau^W_h\\}_{h \\in \\F_q^M}$ of Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra} and report the outcome $h$. Most subtests probe limited information about $h$: given a point $u_W \\in \\F_q^m$, a player is expected to report the evaluation $g_h(u_W)$ of the low-degree encoding $g_h$ of $h$, and for a fixed basis $W$ these probes are checked against each other by the classical low individual degree game of Section~\\ref{sec:ld-verifier}. Consistency \\emph{between} the two bases is checked through two-outcome probes: given additionally $r_W \\in \\F_q$, a player reports $\\tr(g_h(u_W)\\, r_W) \\in \\F_2$. The two binary measurements obtained in this way for $W = X$ and $W = Z$ either commute or anticommute, according to the value $\\gamma \\in \\F_2$ of Equation~\\eqref{eq:gamma-value} below. When $\\gamma = 0$ the verifier checks commutation directly by asking for both bits simultaneously; when $\\gamma = 1$ the verifier checks anticommutation by playing the Magic Square game, using Theorem~\\ref{thm:ms-from-ac}.\n   239\t\n   240\t\\begin{definition}[Admissible parameters]\\label{def:admissible}\n   241\t\t\\uses{def:admissible-size}\n   242\t\tThe tuple $\\mathsf{qldparams} = (q,m,d)$ is \\emph{admissible} if $q$ is an admissible field size (Definition~\\ref{def:admissible-size}) and $m$ divides $q$.\n   243\t\\end{definition}\n   244\t\n   245\t\\begin{definition}[Question distribution of the Pauli basis test]\\label{def:pauli-question-distribution}\n   246\t\t\\uses{def:admissible, def:ld-question-distribution, def:ms-game, def:cl-func, def:cl-dist, def:graph-distribution}\n   247\t\t% Admissibility of the full tuple is required here, not only the divisibility m | q:\n   248\t\t% the source parametrizes the Pauli basis test by the tuple qldparams introduced\n   249\t\t% together with Definition def:admissible (references/qpbt-paper/\n   250\t\t% 08_classical_and_quantum_low_degree_tests.tex:958-1075), and both the fixed binary\n   251\t\t% identification of F_q used by the map chi and the trace tr in eq:gamma-value\n   252\t\t% require q to be a power of 2.\n   253\t\tThe game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{pauli}}$ (also $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{pauli}}_{\\mathsf{qldparams}}$) is parametrized by an admissible tuple $\\mathsf{qldparams} = (q,m,d)$ (Definition~\\ref{def:admissible}): admissibility makes $q$ a power of $2$, so that the field $\\F_q$, its fixed identification with binary strings (Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra}), and the trace $\\tr\\colon \\F_q \\to \\F_2$ used by the win predicate below are defined, and it provides the divisibility $m \\mid q$ required by the index map $\\chi$ of Definition~\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution}. The question type set is\n   254\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   255\t\t\t\\label{eq:pauli-type}\n   335\t\\begin{proof}\n   336\t\t\\uses{thm:ms-from-ac, def:bracket, def:consistent-measurement, def:EPR, def:indicator-vector, lem:pauli-observable-expansion, lem:twisted-commutation}\n   337\t\tThe strategy uses the state $\\ket{\\psi} = \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_2}$ with $M = 2^m$. On a question $((\\mathsf{Point},W), y)$ a player measures the generalized Pauli basis POVM $\\{\\tau^W_h\\}_{h \\in \\F_q^M}$ and reports $g_h(y)$; on $((\\mathsf{ALine},W), \\ell)$ or $((\\mathsf{DLine},W), \\ell)$ they report the restriction of $g_h$ to the line $\\ell$, expressed as a univariate polynomial through the canonical parameterization of $\\ell$; and on $(\\mathsf{Pauli},W)$ they report $h$ itself --- in the bracket notation of Definition~\\ref{def:bracket}, these measurements are $\\tau^W_{[g_\\cdot(y) = a]} \\ot I$, $\\tau^W_{[(g_\\cdot)|_\\ell = f]} \\ot I$, and $\\tau^W_a \\ot I$. For a content $\\omega = (u_X, u_Z, r_X, r_Z)$, consider the two binary measurements $A_b = \\tau^X_{[\\tr(g_\\cdot(u_X)\\, r_X) = b]} \\ot I$ and $B_b = \\tau^Z_{[\\tr(g_\\cdot(u_Z)\\, r_Z) = b]} \\ot I$. Since $g_h(u) = h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(u)$ (Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra}), the relation between the Pauli projections and observables gives $\\mathcal O_A = A_0 - A_1 = \\tau^X(\\mathrm{ind}_m(u_X)\\, r_X) \\ot I$ and $\\mathcal O_B = \\tau^Z(\\mathrm{ind}_m(u_Z)\\, r_Z) \\ot I$, and the twisted commutation relation yields\n   338\t\t\\[\n   339\t\t\t\\mathcal O_A \\mathcal O_B = (-1)^\\gamma\\, \\mathcal O_B \\mathcal O_A\n   340\t\t\\]\n   341\t\twith $\\gamma$ as in Equation~\\eqref{eq:gamma-value}: the two measurements commute when $\\gamma = 0$ and anticommute when $\\gamma = 1$. If $\\gamma = 0$, define $E^{\\mathsf{Pair},\\omega}_{\\beta_X,\\beta_Z} = A_{\\beta_X} B_{\\beta_Z}$ and $E^{(\\mathsf{Pair},X),\\omega}_a = A_a$, $E^{(\\mathsf{Pair},Z),\\omega}_a = B_a$, and let the $\\mathsf{Constraint}$ and $\\mathsf{Variable}$ measurements be trivial (identity on the all-zeros outcome). If $\\gamma = 1$, let the $\\mathsf{Constraint}$ and $\\mathsf{Variable}$ measurements be those produced by Theorem~\\ref{thm:ms-from-ac} from the anticommuting consistent pair $(A, B)$, acting on the auxiliary $\\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_2}$ factor, and let the $\\mathsf{Pair}$ measurements be trivial. The strategy is symmetric and projective by construction; every measurement is either a post-processed Pauli basis measurement, a measurement supplied by Theorem~\\ref{thm:ms-from-ac}, or (in the $\\gamma = 0$ case) a product of two commuting consistent measurements, so the strategy is consistent, and commutativity along every edge of $G^{\\mathrm{pauli}}$ is checked case by case --- for instance, $(\\mathsf{Point},X)$ and $\\mathsf{Variable}_1$ commute because both are $X$-basis measurements. Consistency and projectivity give the consistency checks with certainty, and the low-degree checks pass because the answers agree with the strategy determined by the polynomial $g_h$ in the classical game. For the remaining checks: when $\\gamma = 0$ the commutation check passes by construction, and when $\\gamma = 1$ the Magic Square check passes by Theorem~\\ref{thm:ms-from-ac}; the consistency checks 5 and 7 pass with certainty because $\\tau^W_{[\\tr(g_\\cdot(y)\\, r_W) = \\cdot]} \\ot I$ coincides with $E^{(\\mathsf{Pair},W),\\omega}$, respectively with the embedded $\\mathsf{Variable}_1$ and $\\mathsf{Variable}_5$ measurements of Theorem~\\ref{thm:ms-from-ac} (item~3 there). Hence the strategy has value $1$.\n   342\t\\end{proof}\n   343\t\n   344\tWe now state the main soundness property of the Pauli basis test; the statement is an adaptation of the self-testing theorem of~\\cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP} (Theorem~6.4 there). Unlike Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}, no projectivity assumption is made on the strategy.\n   345\t\n   346\t\\begin{theorem}[Soundness of the Pauli basis test]\\label{thm:pauli}\n   347\t\t\\uses{def:pauli-question-distribution, def:pauli-win-predicate, def:admissible, def:tensor-product-value, def:EPR, lem:pauli-observable-expansion, def:povm-distance}\n   348\t\tThere exists a function\n   349\t\t\\[\n   350\t\t\t\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps, m, d, q) = a\\,(md)^a \\bigl( \\eps^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd} \\bigr)\n   351\t\t\\]\n   352\t\tfor universal constants $a \\ge 1$ and $0 < b < 1$ such that the following holds. For all admissible parameter tuples $\\mathsf{qldparams} = (q,m,d)$ and for all strategies $\\mathcal S = (\\ket\\psi, A, B)$ for the game $\\mathcal G^{\\mathrm{pauli}}_{\\mathsf{qldparams}}$ that succeed with probability at least $1 - \\eps$, there exist local isometries $\\phi_{\\mathrm A}\\colon \\hilb_{\\mathrm A} \\to \\hilb_{\\mathrm A'} \\ot \\hilb_{\\mathrm A''}$ and $\\phi_{\\mathrm B}\\colon \\hilb_{\\mathrm B} \\to \\hilb_{\\mathrm B'} \\ot \\hilb_{\\mathrm B''}$, where $\\ket\\psi \\in \\hilb_{\\mathrm A} \\ot \\hilb_{\\mathrm B}$ and $\\hilb_{\\mathrm A''}, \\hilb_{\\mathrm B''} \\cong (\\C^q)^{\\ot M}$ with $M = 2^m$, and a state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\in \\hilb_{\\mathrm A'} \\ot \\hilb_{\\mathrm B'}$, such that:\n   353\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   354\t\t\t\\item $\\bigl\\| \\phi_{\\mathrm A} \\ot \\phi_{\\mathrm B} \\ket\\psi - \\ket{\\mathrm{aux}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M} \\bigr\\| \\le \\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}(\\eps, m, d, q)$;\n   355\t\t\t% The paper here has the typo \"phi_alice \\ot phi_B\"; both maps are the isometries above.\n   356\t\t\t\\item letting $\\tilde A^x_a = \\phi_{\\mathrm A} A^x_a \\phi_{\\mathrm A}^\\dagger$ and $\\tilde B^y_b = \\phi_{\\mathrm B} B^y_b \\phi_{\\mathrm B}^\\dagger$, for $W \\in \\{X, Z\\}$,\n   357\t\t\t\\begin{align*}\n   358\t\t\t\t\\tilde A^{(\\mathsf{Pauli},W)}_u \\ot I_{\\mathrm{B'B''}} &\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} (\\tau^W_u)_{\\mathrm A''} \\ot I_{\\mathrm{A'B'B''}}\\;,\\\\\n   359\t\t\t\tI_{\\mathrm{A'A''}} \\ot \\tilde B^{(\\mathsf{Pauli},W)}_u &\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}} I_{\\mathrm{A'A''B'}} \\ot (\\tau^W_u)_{\\mathrm B''}\\;,\n   360\t\t\t\\end{align*}\n   361\t\t\twhere the $\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{qld}}}$ statements hold with respect to the state $\\ket{\\mathrm{aux}}_{\\mathrm{A'B'}} \\ot \\ket{\\mathrm{EPR}_q}^{\\ot M}_{\\mathrm{A''B''}}$ and the answer summation is over $u \\in \\F_q^M$.\n   362\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   363\t\\end{theorem}\n   364\t\n   365\tThe proof of Theorem~\\ref{thm:pauli} is the adaptation, to low individual degree, of the analysis of the quantum low-degree test of Natarajan and Vidick; it is developed in Chapters~\\ref{chap:qpbt-observables} and~\\ref{chap:qpbt-combining} and completed in Chapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-extraction}. It relies on the classical soundness theorem (Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}) and on the rigidity of the Magic Square game (Theorem~\\ref{thm:ms-rigidity}).\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:308:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_158","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch14_qpbt_observables.tex | sed -n '35,70p;250,280p;745,885p'\"","aggregated_output":"    35\t\\label{sec:qld-prelim}\n    36\t\n    37\t\\begin{definition}[Individual and total degree polynomial classes]\n    38\t\t\\label{def:ideg-deg-polynomials}\n    39\t\t\\uses{def:line}\n    40\t\tGiven integers $d, m \\geq 0$ and a prime power $q$, denote by\n    41\t\t$\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ the set of polynomials in $m$ variables over\n    42\t\t$\\F_q$ with \\emph{individual} degree at most $d$ in each variable. When\n    43\t\tthe field is clear from context we write $\\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}$, and when\n    44\t\tthe number of variables is also clear from context, $\\mathrm{ideg}_d$. We\n    45\t\tdenote by $\\deg_d$ the set of polynomials with \\emph{total} degree at\n    46\t\tmost $d$.\n    47\t\t% The following usage is introduced only implicitly in the source, in the\n    48\t\t% statement of the lemma labelled lem:qld-comm-line-cons.\n    49\t\tFor a line $\\ell \\subseteq \\F_q^m$ (Definition~\\ref{def:line}) we write\n    50\t\t$\\deg_{md}(\\ell)$ for the set of functions $\\ell \\to \\F_q$ given by a\n    51\t\tunivariate polynomial of degree at most $md$ in a parameterization of\n    52\t\t$\\ell$.\n    53\t\\end{definition}\n    54\t\n    55\tThroughout this chapter $q = 2^k$ with $k$ odd is an admissible field size\n    56\t(Definition~\\ref{def:admissible-size}) and $n = 2^m$. Recall from\n    57\tChapter~\\ref{chap:qpbt-algebra} the low-degree code\n    58\t(see Definition~\\ref{def:low-degree-encoding}), which maps each vector\n    59\t$h \\in \\F_q^{n}$, with\n    60\tcoordinates indexed by binary strings of length $m$, to a polynomial\n    61\t$g_h \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$ such that $g_h(u) = h_u$ for every\n    62\t$u \\in \\{0,1\\}^m$; for $x \\in \\F_q^m$ the associated vector\n    63\t$\\mathrm{ind}_m(x) \\in \\F_q^{n}$ satisfies\n    64\t$g_h(x) = h \\cdot \\mathrm{ind}_m(x)$ for all $h$. Recall also the finite\n    65\tfield trace $\\tr = \\tr_{q \\to 2} \\colon \\F_q \\to \\F_2$\n    66\t(see Definition~\\ref{def:subfield-trace}).\n    67\t\n    68\t\\begin{definition}[Commuting and anticommuting tuples]\n    69\t\t\\label{def:anticommuting-tuple}\n    70\t\t% The quantity gamma is introduced in the source at eq:gamma-value, inside\n   250\t\t\\label{lem:povm-to-obs}\n   251\t\t\\uses{def:approx-question-indexed-operators, def:povm-distance}\n   252\t\tLet $\\mathcal{A}$ be a finite set, let $\\{A^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}}$\n   253\t\tand $\\{B^x_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}}$ be POVMs, and let\n   254\t\t$\\{\\alpha_a\\}_{a \\in \\mathcal{A}}$ be complex numbers on the unit\n   255\t\tcircle. Let $A^x = \\sum_a \\alpha_a A^x_a$ and\n   256\t\t$B^x = \\sum_a \\alpha_a B^x_a$. Then\n   257\t\t\\[\n   258\t\t\tA^x_a \\approx_\\delta B^x_a \\quad\\text{implies}\\quad A^x \\approx_{\\delta'} B^x\n   259\t\t\\]\n   260\t\tfor $\\delta' = |\\mathcal{A}| \\cdot \\delta$, where the hypothesis is the\n   261\t\tanswer-indexed closeness of Definition~\\ref{def:povm-distance} and the\n   262\t\tconclusion is the question-indexed closeness of\n   263\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:approx-question-indexed-operators}.\n   264\t\\end{lemma}\n   265\t\\begin{proof}\n   266\t\tExpand\n   267\t\t\\[\n   268\t\t\t\\E_x \\Big\\| \\sum_a \\alpha_a (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi} \\Big\\|^2\n   269\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, \\E_x \\Big( \\sum_a \\big\\| (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi} \\big\\| \\Big)^2\n   270\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, |\\mathcal{A}| \\cdot \\E_x \\sum_a \\big\\| (A^x_a - B^x_a) \\ket{\\psi} \\big\\|^2\n   271\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, |\\mathcal{A}| \\cdot \\delta\\,,\n   272\t\t\\]\n   273\t\tusing the triangle inequality followed by the Cauchy--Schwarz\n   274\t\tinequality.\n   275\t\\end{proof}\n   276\t\n   277\tThe following lemma shows that POVMs that are approximately projective, in\n   278\tthe sense of being self-consistent, are close to exact projective\n   279\tmeasurements.\n   280\t\n   745\t\t(\\eqref{eq:pts-obs-commutation} of\n   746\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications-obs}), while the generalized Pauli\n   747\t\tobservables exactly commute or anticommute with the same sign\n   748\t\t(see Lemma~\\ref{lem:twisted-commutation}). The two signs cancel, so\n   749\t\t$\\hat{X}^{r_X}(u_X)\\hat{Z}^{r_Z}(u_Z) \\approx_{\\sqrt{\\eps}} \\hat{Z}^{r_Z}(u_Z)\\hat{X}^{r_X}(u_X)$\n   750\t\ton average over uniformly random $\\omega$, giving the approximate\n   751\t\tcommutation with $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Anticom}} = \\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n   752\t\\end{proof}\n   753\t\n   754\t\\begin{definition}[Expanded line measurements]\n   755\t\t\\label{def:expanded-line-measurement}\n   756\t\t% In the source this measurement is defined inside the proof of the lemma\n   757\t\t% labelled lem:qld-comm-line-cons (lines 552-556); it is recorded as a\n   758\t\t% standalone definition because Chapter chap:qpbt-combining uses the\n   759\t\t% concrete measurement, not merely its existence.\n   760\t\t\\uses{def:projective-strategy-setup, def:expanded-state, def:expanded-observables, def:ideg-deg-polynomials, def:low-degree-encoding, lem:pauli-observable-expansion, def:line}\n   761\t\tFor $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$, a line $\\ell$, and $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$,\n   762\t\tdefine\n   763\t\t\\[\n   764\t\t\t\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\n   765\t\t\t\\,=\\, \\sum_{\\substack{f',f'' \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell) \\\\ f' + f'' = f}}\n   766\t\t\tM^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{f'} \\ot \\tau^{W,\\ell}_{f''}\\,,\n   767\t\t\\]\n   768\t\twhere the projector $\\tau^{W,\\ell}_{f''}$, acting on register\n   769\t\t$\\mathsf{A}'$ (or on $\\mathsf{A}''$, following the register conventions\n   770\t\tof Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-observables}), is the outcome-$f''$\n   771\t\tprojector of the following projective measurement: measure all $n$\n   772\t\tqudits in the basis $W$, obtaining an outcome $h \\in \\F_q^{n}$, and\n   773\t\treturn the restriction $f''$ of the low-degree encoding\n   774\t\t$g_h \\colon \\F_q^m \\to \\F_q$\n   775\t\t(see Definition~\\ref{def:low-degree-encoding}) to the line\n   776\t\t$\\ell$. Since $g_h$ has individual degree at most $d$, hence total\n   777\t\tdegree at most $md$, its restriction to $\\ell$ lies in\n   778\t\t$\\deg_{md}(\\ell)$, and\n   779\t\t$\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)}$ is a\n   780\t\tprojective measurement.\n   781\t\\end{definition}\n   782\t\n   783\t\\begin{lemma}[Consistency of the expanded line measurements]\n   784\t\t\\label{lem:qld-comm-line-cons}\n   785\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-point-measurement, def:expanded-line-measurement, def:expanded-state, def:line-point-dist, def:approx-question-indexed-operators, def:povm-distance, def:ideg-deg-polynomials, def:symmetric-equivalents}\n   786\t\tThere exists a function $\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}(\\eps) = \\poly(\\eps)$\n   787\t\tsuch that the following holds, with respect to the state\n   788\t\t$\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$ of Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-state}. For all\n   789\t\t$W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and lines $\\ell$ there exists a projective measurement\n   790\t\t$\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)}$,\n   791\t\tacting on registers $\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'$ or\n   792\t\t$\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''$, such that:\n   793\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   794\t\t\t\\item \\label{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons}\n   795\t\t\t(\\textbf{Self-consistency}) For all $W \\in \\{X,Z\\}$ and\n   796\t\t\t$r \\in \\F_q$ (the relation does not depend on $r$), on average over\n   797\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   798\t\t\tdistribution (Definition~\\ref{def:line-point-dist}),\n   799\t\t\t\\[\n   800\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   801\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   802\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   803\t\t\t\\]\n   804\t\t\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$.\n   805\t\t\t\\item \\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons}\n   806\t\t\t(\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements I}) On average over\n   807\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   808\t\t\tdistribution,\n   809\t\t\t\\[\n   810\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   811\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   812\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   813\t\t\t\t\\ot (\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{f(u)})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   814\t\t\t\\]\n   815\t\t\twhere the answer summation is over $f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell)$.\n   816\t\t\t\\item \\label{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2}\n   817\t\t\t(\\textbf{Consistency with points measurements II}) On average over\n   818\t\t\ta uniformly random pair $(\\ell,u)$ drawn from the line-point\n   819\t\t\tdistribution,\n   820\t\t\t\\[\n   821\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]})_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   822\t\t\t\t\\,\\approx_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}}\\,\n   823\t\t\t\t(\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   824\t\t\t\\]\n   825\t\t\twhere\n   826\t\t\t$\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]} = \\sum_{f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell) \\colon f(u)=a} \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f$\n   827\t\t\tand the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$.\n   828\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   829\t\tFurthermore, all symmetric equivalents of these approximations also\n   830\t\thold. The proof exhibits the measurements of\n   831\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:expanded-line-measurement} as witnesses, with\n   832\t\titem~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} holding with error $\\eps$ and\n   833\t\titems~\\ref{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons}\n   834\t\tand~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2} with\n   835\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}(\\eps) = \\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n   836\t\\end{lemma}\n   837\t\\begin{proof}\n   838\t\t\\uses{def:expanded-line-measurement, def:expanded-point-measurement, lem:qld-win-implications, fact:data-processing, fact:add-a-proj2, fact:agreement, def:line-point-dist}\n   839\t\tTake $\\{\\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}$ to be the measurement of\n   840\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:expanded-line-measurement}.\n   841\t\tItem~\\ref{enu:qld-comm-line-self-cons} follows from the fact that the\n   842\t\toriginal line measurements $\\{M^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_f\\}$ are\n   843\t\t$\\eps$-self-consistent between registers $\\mathsf{A}$ and $\\mathsf{B}$\n   844\t\tin $\\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}$ (item~\\ref{enu:qld-cons} of\n   845\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}), the Pauli projections\n   846\t\t$\\{\\tau^{W,\\ell}_{f''}\\}$ are perfectly self-consistent between\n   847\t\tregisters $\\mathsf{A}'$ and $\\mathsf{A}''$, and the data processing\n   848\t\tinequality (Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}). For\n   849\t\titem~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons}: insert the projection\n   850\t\t$M^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=f'(u)]}$ by\n   851\t\tprojectivity, replace it by\n   852\t\t$(M^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_{f'(u)})_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}$ using the\n   853\t\tline-point consistency (item~\\ref{enu:qld-ld} of\n   854\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-win-implications}) together with\n   855\t\tLemma~\\ref{fact:add-a-proj2}, adjoin the exactly consistent projector\n   856\t\t$\\tau^{W,u}_{f''(u)}$ on register $\\mathsf{A}''$, and recombine by the\n   857\t\tconvolution form of Definition~\\ref{def:expanded-point-measurement};\n   858\t\tthis gives item~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} with error $\\eps$. For\n   859\t\titem~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2}, the data processing inequality\n   860\t\tdoes not apply directly, since the bound of\n   861\t\titem~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} is stated with $\\approx$ rather\n   862\t\tthan $\\simeq$ and its right-hand side is not of the form\n   863\t\t$\\Id \\ot B$; instead, a direct computation from\n   864\t\titem~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons} together with projectivity gives\n   865\t\t\\[\n   866\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,u)} \\sum_{a \\in \\F_q}\n   867\t\t\t\\bra{\\hat{\\psi}}\\, \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]}\n   868\t\t\t\\ot \\big( \\Id - \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a \\big) \\ket{\\hat{\\psi}}\n   869\t\t\t\\,\\leq\\, \\eps\\,.\n   870\t\t\\]\n   871\t\tAn elementary bound for projective sub-measurements $\\{A_i\\}$,\n   872\t\t$\\{B_i\\}$ and a state $\\ket{\\varphi}$, namely\n   873\t\t$\\sum_i \\bra{\\varphi}(A_i - B_i)\\ket{\\varphi} \\leq 2 \\big( \\sum_i \\big\\|(A_i - B_i)\\ket{\\varphi}\\big\\|^2 \\big)^{1/2}$,\n   874\t\tupgrades this to\n   875\t\t$\\Id \\approx_{\\sqrt{\\eps}} \\sum_a \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Line},W),\\ell}_{[\\mathrm{eval}_u(\\cdot)=a]} \\ot \\hat{M}^{(\\mathrm{Point},W),u}_a$.\n   876\t\tA second elementary fact about projective measurements --- if\n   877\t\t$\\Id \\approx_\\delta \\sum_i A^x_i \\ot B^x_i$ for families of projective\n   878\t\tmeasurements $\\{A^x_i\\}$ and $\\{B^x_i\\}$ acting on separate registers\n   879\t\tof a bipartite state, then $A^x_i \\ot \\Id \\simeq_\\delta \\Id \\ot B^x_i$,\n   880\t\tand hence $A^x_i \\ot \\Id \\approx_\\delta \\Id \\ot B^x_i$ by item~1 of\n   881\t\tLemma~\\ref{fact:agreement} --- then yields\n   882\t\titem~\\ref{eq:qld-comm-line-pt-cons2} with error $\\sqrt{\\eps}$, so that\n   883\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}(\\eps) = \\sqrt{\\eps}$.\n   884\t\\end{proof}\n   885\t\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
/Users/dengnifer/Documents/ghz/ldt/MIPStarRE-dev/results/telemetry/sessions/reviewer-pr0001-20260830-03.jsonl:310:{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_159","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba blueprint/src/chapter/ch15_qpbt_combining.tex | sed -n '315,350p;405,450p;515,585p;785,825p;905,940p;980,1015p'\"","aggregated_output":"   315\t\terror, a $\\poly(\\eps, md/q)$ function, and the identical argument with\n   316\t\tthe registers modified gives all symmetric equivalents.\n   317\t\\end{proof}\n   318\t\n   319\t\\section{Applying the classical low-degree test}\n   320\t\\label{sec:apply-ldt}\n   321\t\n   322\tThe combined point and line measurements of the previous two sections are next\n   323\tmerged into a single measurement returning a pair of global polynomials that\n   324\tencode the $X$-basis and the $Z$-basis information simultaneously. This is\n   325\tdone by encoding the pair as one polynomial in $2m+2$ variables and invoking\n   326\tthe quantum soundness of the classical low individual degree test\n   327\t(Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}).\n   328\t\n   329\t\\begin{definition}[The combining map]\n   330\t\t\\label{def:combine-map}\n   331\t\t\\uses{def:polyfunc, def:line}\n   332\t\tFor $f, g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$, the polynomial\n   333\t\t$\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g} \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,2m+2}(\\F_q)$ is defined by\n   334\t\t\\[\n   335\t\t\t\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g}(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta)\n   336\t\t\t= \\alpha f(x) + \\beta g(z)\\,,\n   337\t\t\t\\qquad x \\in \\F_q^m,\\ z \\in \\F_q^m,\\ \\alpha \\in \\F_q,\\\n   338\t\t\t\\beta \\in \\F_q\\,.\n   339\t\t\\]\n   340\t\tThe combining map is also defined for polynomials restricted to lines.\n   341\t\tLet $\\ell$ be a line in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ with intercept\n   342\t\t$u = (u_X, u_Z, u_\\alpha, u_\\beta)$ and slope\n   343\t\t$v = (v_X, v_Z, v_\\alpha, v_\\beta)$, and let $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$ be\n   344\t\tlines in $\\F_q^m$ such that for every point\n   345\t\t$(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta) \\in \\ell$ it holds that $x \\in \\ell_X$ and\n   346\t\t$z \\in \\ell_Z$. Then for any two polynomials\n   347\t\t$f \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X)$ and $g \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z)$, the polynomial\n   348\t\t$\\mathrm{combine}_{f,g} \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$ is defined by\n   349\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   350\t\t\t\\label{eq:combine-lines}\n   405\t\t= \\sum_{a,b \\,:\\, \\alpha a + \\beta b = c} \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}$,\n   406\t\ta coarse-graining of the projective measurement of\n   407\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}, hence itself projective. A uniformly random\n   408\t\tpoint of $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ has uniformly random coordinates $(x,z)$, so the\n   409\t\tself-consistency and consistency properties follow from those of\n   410\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{a,b}\\}$ established in Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-10}\n   411\t\ttogether with Lemma~\\ref{fact:data-processing}, which shows that\n   412\t\tcoarse-graining the outcomes preserves the $\\delta_Q$ bounds.\n   413\t\\end{proof}\n   414\t\n   415\t\\begin{lemma}[Combined line measurement on the extended coordinates]\n   416\t\t\\label{lem:qld-4-13}\n   417\t\t\\uses{lem:qld-4-12, def:line, def:line-point-dist, def:consistency,\n   418\t\t\tdef:bracket, def:expanded-state}\n   419\t\tThere exists a function\n   420\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}(\\eps,m,d,q) = \\poly(m^2\\eps, md/q)$ such that\n   421\t\tthe following holds. For every line $\\ell$ in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and\n   422\t\t$\\hilb \\in \\{\\hilb_{\\mathsf{A}}, \\hilb_{\\mathsf{B}}\\}$ there exists a\n   423\t\tPOVM $\\{\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}_{f}\\}$ acting on $\\hilb \\ot (\\C^q)^{\\ot n}$ with\n   424\t\toutcomes $f \\in \\deg_{md+1}(\\ell)$ such that on average over a uniformly\n   425\t\trandom pair $(\\ell, (x,z,\\alpha,\\beta))$ drawn from the line-point\n   426\t\tdistribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$,\n   427\t\t\\begin{gather}\n   428\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-4-13}\n   429\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}\n   430\t\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)}(\\cdot)=a]}\n   431\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'}\n   432\t\t\t\\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}}\n   433\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\\,,\n   434\t\t\t\\\\\n   435\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}\n   436\t\t\t_{[\\mathrm{eval}_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta)}(\\cdot)=a]}\n   437\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{B}'}\n   438\t\t\t\\simeq_{\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}}\n   439\t\t\t\\big(\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{B}''}\\,,\n   440\t\t\t\\notag\n   441\t\t\\end{gather}\n   442\t\twhere the answer summation is over $a \\in \\F_q$. Moreover, if $\\ell$ is\n   443\t\taxis-parallel, then the outcome $f \\in \\deg_{d}(\\ell)$.\n   444\t\\end{lemma}\n   445\t\n   446\tBefore proving the lemma we record definitions and estimates concerning\n   447\tdistributions over lines and points. Recall from\n   448\tDefinition~\\ref{def:line-point-dist} that the line-point distribution over\n   449\t$\\F_q^m$ is an even mixture of an axis-parallel component $D_{\\mathrm{ALine}}$\n   450\tand a diagonal component $D_{\\mathrm{DLine}}$.\n   515\t\tline-point distribution is precisely the statement that the left-hand\n   516\t\tside of \\eqref{eq:qld-xz-lines-restricted}, averaged over\n   517\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{Line}} \\times D_{\\mathrm{Line}}$, is at most $\\delta_P$.\n   518\t\\end{proof}\n   519\t\n   520\t\\begin{lemma}[The sub-line distribution]\n   521\t\t\\label{lem:qld-sublines}\n   522\t\t\\uses{def:line, def:line-point-dist, def:ith-restricted-line}\n   523\t\tThere exists a distribution $D$ over tuples $(\\ell, \\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$,\n   524\t\twhere $\\ell$ is a line in $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and $\\ell_X, \\ell_Z$ are lines in\n   525\t\t$\\F_q^m$, with the following properties:\n   526\t\t\\begin{enumerate}\n   527\t\t\t\\item The marginal distribution over $\\ell$ is the same as the\n   528\t\t\tmarginal of the line-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$.\n   529\t\t\t\\item For any point of the form $u = (u_X, u_Z, \\alpha, \\beta)$\n   530\t\t\tlying on $\\ell$, it holds that $u_X \\in \\ell_X$ and\n   531\t\t\t$u_Z \\in \\ell_Z$. Moreover, for $u$ chosen uniformly at random on\n   532\t\t\t$\\ell$, the marginal distribution over $(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z, u_X)$\n   533\t\t\t(resp.\\ over $(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z, u_Z)$) is such that the marginal over\n   534\t\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$ is a mixture of the product distributions\n   535\t\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{ALine},i} \\times D_{\\mathrm{ALine},j}$ and\n   536\t\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{DLine},i} \\times D_{\\mathrm{DLine},j}$ for\n   537\t\t\t$i, j \\in \\{1, \\dots, m\\}$, and, conditioned on\n   538\t\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$, the point $u_X$ is uniformly random on $\\ell_X$\n   539\t\t\t(resp.\\ $u_Z$ is uniformly random on $\\ell_Z$).\n   540\t\t\t\\item If $\\ell$ is axis-parallel, then $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$ are\n   541\t\t\taxis-parallel.\n   542\t\t\\end{enumerate}\n   543\t\t(Property~3 is not part of the numbered statement in the source; see\n   544\t\tRemark~\\ref{rem:qld-sublines-property-three}.)\n   545\t\\end{lemma}\n   546\t\\begin{proof}\n   547\t\t\\uses{def:line-representative, def:ld-question-distribution,\n   548\t\t\tdef:line-point-dist, def:ith-restricted-line}\n   549\t\tThe distribution $D$ is described by a sampling procedure. First sample\n   550\t\t$(\\ell, u)$ from the line-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$ and write\n   551\t\t$u = (u_X, u_Z, \\alpha, \\beta)$, so that Property~1 holds by\n   552\t\tconstruction. If $\\ell$ is axis-parallel, $\\ell = (v, e_j)$ with\n   553\t\t$v = (v_X, v_Z, \\alpha_0, \\beta_0)$, set\n   554\t\t$\\ell_X = (v'_X, e_{i_X})$ and $\\ell_Z = (v'_Z, e_{i_Z})$ where: for\n   555\t\t$1 \\le j \\le m$, $i_X = j$ and $i_Z$ is uniformly random in\n   556\t\t$\\{1,\\dots,m\\}$; for $m+1 \\le j \\le 2m$, $i_X$ is uniformly random and\n   557\t\t$i_Z = j - m$; for $2m+1 \\le j \\le 2m+2$, both $i_X$ and $i_Z$ are\n   558\t\tuniformly random; and $v'_X, v'_Z$ are the canonical line\n   559\t\trepresentatives of $v_X, v_Z$ for the chosen directions\n   560\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:line-representative}), so that $v_X \\in \\ell_X$\n   561\t\tand $v_Z \\in \\ell_Z$. If $\\ell$ is diagonal, $\\ell = (v, s, w)$ with\n   562\t\tdirection $w = (w_X, w_Z, \\gamma, \\delta)$ whose first nonzero\n   563\t\tcoordinate has index $j = \\chi(s)$, sample\n   564\t\t$\\ell_X = (v'_X, s_X, w'_X)$ and $\\ell_Z = (v'_Z, s_Z, w'_Z)$ where: for\n   565\t\t$1 \\le j \\le m$, $w'_X = w_X$ and $w'_Z = w_Z$ with $s_X, s_Z$ chosen so\n   566\t\tthat $\\chi(s_X) = j$ and $\\chi(s_Z) = 1$; for $m+1 \\le j \\le 2m$,\n   567\t\t$w'_X$ is a uniformly random diagonal direction (with $s_X$ chosen\n   568\t\taccordingly) and $w'_Z = w_Z$ with $\\chi(s_Z) = j - m$; for\n   569\t\t$2m+1 \\le j \\le 2m+2$, both directions are uniformly random with\n   570\t\t$s_X, s_Z$ chosen accordingly; and again $v'_X, v'_Z$ are the canonical\n   571\t\trepresentatives. In each case a uniformly random point $u$ on $\\ell$\n   572\t\tprojects to a uniformly random point $u_X$ on $\\ell_X$ (resp.\\ $u_Z$ on\n   573\t\t$\\ell_Z$), and a case analysis over $j$ shows that the marginal over\n   574\t\t$(\\ell_X, \\ell_Z)$ is a mixture of the required product distributions:\n   575\t\tfor instance, in the axis-parallel case with $1 \\le j \\le m$, the\n   576\t\tmarginal over $(\\ell_X, u_X)$ conditioned on $j$ equals\n   577\t\t$D_{\\mathrm{ALine},j}$, and even conditioned on $(\\ell_X, u_X)$ the line\n   578\t\t$\\ell_Z$ is distributed as $D_{\\mathrm{ALine},i_Z}$ for uniformly random\n   579\t\t$i_Z$; the remaining cases are analogous, differing only in how the\n   580\t\tindices are chosen. This gives Property~2. Property~3 is immediate from\n   581\t\tthe procedure: when $\\ell$ is axis-parallel, both $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$\n   582\t\tare constructed to be axis-parallel.\n   583\t\\end{proof}\n   584\t\n   585\t\\begin{remark}[Property~3 of the sub-line distribution]\n   785\t\t\t\\sum_{\\substack{f_X \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_X),\\\n   786\t\t\t\tf_Z \\in \\deg_{md}(\\ell_Z):\\\\\n   787\t\t\t\tf = \\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}}}\n   788\t\t\tT^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\,,\n   789\t\t\\]\n   790\t\ta valid POVM; the polynomial $\\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}$ of\n   791\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:combine-map} is well defined here because Property~2\n   792\t\tof Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} guarantees that $\\ell_X$ and $\\ell_Z$\n   793\t\tare compatible with $\\ell$. If $\\ell$ is axis-parallel, then by\n   794\t\tProperty~3 of Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} so are $\\ell_X$ and\n   795\t\t$\\ell_Z$, hence $f_X, f_Z \\in \\deg_d$ by Lemma~\\ref{lem:qld-xz-lines};\n   796\t\talong such a line either $(\\alpha,\\beta)$ is constant, in which case\n   797\t\t$f = \\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}$ is a fixed linear combination of\n   798\t\tpolynomials of degree at most $d$, or $(x,z)$ is constant, in which case\n   799\t\t$f$ is affine in the line parameter, so in either case\n   800\t\t$f \\in \\deg_d(\\ell)$. Chaining Lemmas~\\ref{lem:claim-17-1},\n   801\t\t\\ref{lem:claim-17-2} and~\\ref{lem:claim-17-3} with the triangle\n   802\t\tinequality for scalars gives the consistency bound\n   803\t\t\\begin{equation}\n   804\t\t\t\\label{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}\n   805\t\t\t\\E_{(\\ell,\\ell_X,\\ell_Z) \\sim D}\\\n   806\t\t\t\\E_{(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) \\in \\ell}\n   807\t\t\t\\sum_{f_X,f_Z} \\bra{\\hat\\psi}\n   808\t\t\t\\big(T^{\\ell_X,\\ell_Z}_{f_X,f_Z}\\big)_{\\mathsf{A}\\mathsf{A}'} \\ot\n   809\t\t\t\\big(\\Id - \\hat{Q}^{x,z}_{f_X(x),f_Z(z)}\n   810\t\t\t\\big)_{\\mathsf{B}\\mathsf{A}''}\n   811\t\t\t\\ket{\\hat\\psi}\n   812\t\t\t= O\\big(m\\,(\\eps^{1/4} + \\delta_P^{1/4} + \\delta_Q^{1/4}\n   813\t\t\t+ \\delta_{\\mathrm{Line}}^{1/2})\\big)\\,;\n   814\t\t\\end{equation}\n   815\t\tthe function\n   816\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}} = \\poly(m^2\\eps, md/q)$ dominates this\n   817\t\tbound. (The source states a second bound for the same quantity, whose\n   818\t\tderivation is not supported by the properties proved for the sub-line\n   819\t\tdistribution; see Remark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-13-source-defects}.) Now\n   820\t\tif the pair $(f_X, f_Z)$ evaluates to $(a, b)$ at $(x, z)$, then\n   821\t\t$\\mathrm{combine}_{f_X,f_Z}$ evaluates to $\\alpha a + \\beta b$ at\n   822\t\t$(x, z, \\alpha, \\beta)$; hence the consistency of $T$ with\n   823\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{x,z}$ along the sub-line pairs expressed by\n   824\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-combined-lines-consistency}, the definition of\n   825\t\t$\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}$ as the image of $T$ under the combining map, and the\n   905\t\t\tfact:data-processing, lem:schwartz-zippel-total-degree,\n   906\t\t\tdef:combine-map}\n   907\t\tBy Lemmas~\\ref{lem:qld-4-12} and~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}, the expanded state\n   908\t\t$\\ket{\\hat\\psi}$ together with the lines measurements\n   909\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}_{f}\\}$ and the points measurements\n   910\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\}$ constitutes a strategy for the\n   911\t\tclassical low individual degree game (Definition~\\ref{def:ld-game}) with\n   912\t\tparameters $(q, 2m+2, d, 1)$ succeeding with probability\n   913\t\t$1 - \\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}$; the axis-parallel clause of\n   914\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13} supplies the degree-$d$ answer format that the\n   915\t\tgame requires on axis-parallel lines.\n   916\t\t% Caveat added locally: the source applies the classical soundness\n   917\t\t% theorem at dimension 2m+2 without the divisibility hypothesis that\n   918\t\t% def:ld-game requires; see rem:qld-4-7-divisibility.\n   919\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:ld-game} is stated only when its dimension\n   920\t\tparameter divides $q$; the divisibility of $q$ by $2m+2$ needed here\n   921\t\tdoes not follow from admissibility of $(q,m,d)$ and in general fails,\n   922\t\tso this instantiation, which follows the source, is not covered by\n   923\t\tTheorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} as stated; see\n   924\t\tRemark~\\ref{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility}.) Since $\\{\\hat{Q}^{\\ell}_{f}\\}$ is\n   925\t\tonly a POVM, it is first made projective by Naimark dilation\n   926\t\t(Theorem~\\ref{thm:naimark}), which preserves the $\\simeq$ conclusions of\n   927\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}. The quantum soundness of the classical test\n   928\t\t(Theorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}, applied with $m$ replaced by $2m+2$ and\n   929\t\t$\\eps$ by $\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}$) then yields a POVM\n   930\t\t$\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$ with outcomes $g \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,2m+2}(\\F_q)$ that\n   931\t\tis $\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}$-self-consistent and\n   932\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}$-consistent with the points measurements\n   933\t\t$\\{\\hat{Q}^{x,z,\\alpha,\\beta}_{a}\\}$, where\n   934\t\t$\\delta_{\\mathrm{ld}}\n   935\t\t= a(md)^a(\\delta_{\\mathrm{combine}}^b + q^{-b} + 2^{-bmd})$\n   936\t\tfor universal constants $a, b$; a second application of Naimark dilation\n   937\t\tmakes $\\{\\hat{S}_g\\}$ projective with the same guarantees. Let\n   938\t\t$\\mathcal{G}$ be the set of polynomials of the form\n   939\t\t$g(x,z,\\alpha,\\beta) = \\alpha g_X(x) + \\beta g_Z(z)$ with\n   940\t\t$g_X, g_Z \\in \\mathrm{ideg}_{d,m}(\\F_q)$. A chain of approximations\n   980\t\t\\eqref{eq:qld-s-point-con-alice} for $W = X$. For $W = Z$ the same\n   981\t\tsteps are performed with $X$ and $Z$ interchanged, starting from the\n   982\t\topposite-order correlation, and entirely analogous calculations with\n   983\t\tthe registers swapped yield \\eqref{eq:qld-s-point-con-bob}.\n   984\t\\end{proof}\n   985\t\n   986\t% This remark records a defect of the source argument; it is not a statement\n   987\t% from the paper.\n   988\t\\begin{remark}[Divisibility hypothesis in the application of the classical\n   989\t\tsoundness theorem]\n   990\t\t\\label{rem:qld-4-7-divisibility}\n   991\t\tDefinition~\\ref{def:ld-game} defines the classical low individual\n   992\t\tdegree game only when its dimension parameter divides $q$: the index\n   993\t\tfunction $\\chi$ of the question distribution\n   994\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:ld-question-distribution}) partitions $\\F_q$ into\n   995\t\tclasses of equal size, one for each coordinate. The proof of\n   996\t\tLemma~\\ref{lem:qld-4-7} instantiates the game, and\n   997\t\tTheorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness}, at dimension $2m+2$, while\n   998\t\tadmissibility of $(q,m,d)$ (Definition~\\ref{def:admissible}) supplies\n   999\t\tonly that $m$ divides $q$. An admissible field size\n  1000\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:admissible-size}) is a power of two, so $m$\n  1001\t\tdividing $q$ forces $m$ to be a power of two; for $m \\ge 2$ the\n  1002\t\tinteger $2m+2 = 2(m+1)$ then has the odd factor $m+1 \\ge 3$ and does\n  1003\t\tnot divide $q$. The same divisibility hypothesis underlies the\n  1004\t\tline-point distribution over $\\F_q^{2m+2}$\n  1005\t\t(Definition~\\ref{def:line-point-dist} instantiated at dimension\n  1006\t\t$2m+2$) used in Lemmas~\\ref{lem:qld-sublines} and~\\ref{lem:qld-4-13}.\n  1007\t\tThe source proof in \\cite{Ji2020MIPStar} makes the same instantiation\n  1008\t\twithout addressing the divisibility; a complete proof requires a\n  1009\t\tformulation of the game, of its question distribution, and of\n  1010\t\tTheorem~\\ref{lem:ld-soundness} that applies to dimensions not dividing\n  1011\t\t$q$. No local correction is recorded here.\n  1012\t\\end{remark}\n  1013\t\n  1014\t\\begin{remark}[Constant regimes in the quotation of the classical soundness\n  1015\t\ttheorem]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
