x.sh:452` — autofix ignores local-ci/summary and can dispatch from stale failed step statuses during a CI rerun  <!-- lane=code -->\n- [ ] F9 (blocker) `local/bin/autofix.sh:511` — review-fix trusts a body marker without requiring the review's commit_id to equal HEAD_SHA  <!-- lane=code -->\n- [ ] F10 (blocker) `local/bin/autofix.sh:884` — a successful fix reruns CI but leaves the bot head permanently without an exact-head review  <!-- lane=code -->\n- [ ] F11 (changes) `local/bin/autofix.sh:559` — the cap ritual still does not remove the auto-fix label required by the normative protocol  <!-- lane=code -->\n- [ ] F12 (changes) `local/bin/issue_new.py:96` — lossy title slugs silently alias distinct issues as the same idempotent creation  <!-- lane=code -->\n- [ ] F13 (changes) `local/bin/review.sh:1013` — every review writes a tracked telemetry file after cleanliness checks, dirtying or advancing the reviewed lifecycle  <!-- lane=code -->\n- [ ] F14 (changes) `local/personas/scout.md:22` — the scout persona promises GitHub comment publication that dispatch.sh never performs  <!-- lane=code -->\n- [ ] F15 (changes) `local/personas/orchestrator.md:95` — active personas still require deleted local issue files and deny the restored GitHub issue surface  <!-- lane=code -->\n- [ ] F16 (changes) `local/briefs/0007-github-native-workflow-brief.md:11` — the active brief requires a local write-through registry that this PR deletes  <!-- lane=code -->\n- [ ] F17 (changes) `local/protocols/EVOLUTION.md:239` — the protocol amendment omits the required Change and Expected effect fields  <!-- lane=code -->\n<!-- findings:end -->\n\n## Code review\n\n**F1.** With `--base \"$BRANCH\"`, the merge base equals `HEAD_SHA`, the changed-file set is empty, all eight jobs become skipped-success, and the merge gate accepts the resulting statuses. Publishing runs must use the GitHub PR base; an override must be non-publishing.\n\n**F2.** The initial cleanliness check is insufficient. In particular, `blueprint_lean_sync.py --update-lean-decls` can rewrite tracked `blueprint/lean_decls`; the final check sees the same commit SHA and certifies those modified bytes. Recheck cleanliness before publishing any success.\n\n**F3.** If manifest-comment publication fails, `ci.sh` exits nonzero but leaves every merge-required status green. Publish the manifest first and `local-ci/summary` last.\n\n**F4.** Both this condition and `pr_merge.py:176` treat `APPROVED` as unconditional. A malformed reviewer response containing blocker findings and an APPROVED trailer therefore merges. Parsed blocker/changes findings must force failure regardless of the trailer.\n\n**F5.** `TRUSTED_REF` defaults to `main`, while the generated prompt explicitly permits commits. This bypasses the exclusive PR/CI/review merge path in `local/protocols/issues-prs.md:78-79`. Writable issue sessions must require an issue branch.\n\n**F6.** GitHub's issues endpoint also returns pull requests. No `pull_request` check precedes the PATCH, so `issue_close.py N` can close PR N without merging it. Fetch and reject PR-shaped records first.\n\n**F7.** The exact-SHA merge guard protects head bytes, not mutable statuses. A concurrent CI run can replace green evidence with pending or failure after the gate read but before the merge PUT. Merge and lifecycle publishers need shared exclusion through the irreversible operation.\n\n**F8.** CI marks its summary pending before replacing old per-step statuses. Autofix discards that summary and can act on an old build failure while CI is reading the same worktree. Require a terminal exact-head summary failure before dispatch.\n\n**F9.** The marker includes a SHA, but it is not proof that the review is attached to that SHA. Require both the marker and `row.commit_id == HEAD_SHA` before authorizing a write-enabled fixer.\n\n**F10.** After a fix, only CI runs; `review.sh` skips bot commits, and the next autofix invocation can exit “nothing to fix” before cap handling. The resulting green bot head cannot satisfy the exact-head review gate. The terminal successful fix needs one forced review.\n\n**F11.** `local/protocols/autofix.md:163-169` requires removing `auto-fix-codex` at the cap. The repair standardized the label name but never implemented removal.\n\n**F12.** `slugify` truncates and normalizes titles, while adoption does not compare title, body, or labels. Distinct issues can therefore reuse an old issue and even attach it to the wrong parent. Require an explicit key or reject mismatched adoption.\n\n**F13.** The current head, which commits the prior review ledger, demonstrates the feedback loop. Review artifacts should remain in runtime storage or use a defined out-of-band archival path that neither dirties the merge worktree nor changes the reviewed head.\n\n**F14-F17.** The operative documentation remains internally inconsistent: scout output is not published, several personas reference retired issue files, the new brief specifies the opposite authority model, and the amendment does not follow `local/protocols/meta.md:36-40`. These must be synchronized and covered by the hygiene tests.\n\nNo Lean, blueprint, or paper-gap declarations changed, so source-statement fidelity and proof-debt checks are not applicable. `git diff --check`, shell syntax checking, and AST parsing of all extant changed Python files passed. I did not rerun the temporary-file-writing test suite in the read-only reviewer sandbox; the supplied exact-head CI status reports success. Prior round findings explicitly deferred to follow-up issues were not duplicated here.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
