   218	## 6. Concurrency and locks
   219	
   220	Two advisory `mkdir`-based lease directories, both under
   221	`~/.cache/mipstarre-dev/` (`MIPSTARRE_CACHE_ROOT` overrides the root). Each
   222	holds an `owner` file: pid, ISO timestamp, tag. A lock is broken only when its
   223	owner pid is dead or its stamp is older than `MIPSTARRE_FULL_BUILD_LOCK_STALE_S`
   224	(default 3 h).
   225	
   226	| Lock | Scope | On contention |
   227	|---|---|---|
   228	| `locks/ci-<pr-id>.lock` | one `ci.sh` per PR | **refuse immediately** (exit 2) |
   229	| `$CACHE_ROOT/.full-build-lock` | one full `lake build` machine-wide (invariant 7) | wait up to `MIPSTARRE_CI_BUILD_LOCK_WAIT_S` (default 4 h), then record `build` as `error` and continue with the Python steps |
   230	
   231	Only the `build` step takes the full-build lock. The audits are pure Python and
   232	take nothing; single-file `lake env lean` checks are read-only over oleans and
   233	take nothing either — which is precisely why `.githooks/pre-push` can use them
   234	per-file without serializing.
   235	
   236	`ci.sh` never cancels a running build. GitHub cancels in-progress PR runs
   237	(`pr-ci.yml:48-51`) because its runners are disposable and main-branch runs —
   238	the ones that seed the cache — are exempt. Locally there is nothing disposable:
   239	killing a build mid-flight leaves a half-written `.lake/build` and a held lease.
   240	So a second run for the same PR is refused, and a foreign live build lease is
   241	waited on, never stolen. Do not `kill -9` a running `ci.sh`; if you must, remove
   242	the lease directories by hand.
